(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2c.5326062a03.js"]={"37":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the Physiological Anatomy of the Parathyroid Glands","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the typical number and anatomical location of the parathyroid glands in most individuals?","choices":{"E":"Four parathyroid glands are typically present, situated on the posterior surface of the superior and inferior poles of each thyroid lobe, embedded in surrounding adipose tissue","A":"Six parathyroid glands are typically present, with two on the posterior thyroid surface and four distributed along the trachea and esophagus in the superior mediastinum","B":"Two parathyroid glands are present bilaterally, one on the anterior surface of each thyroid lobe, immediately deep to the pretracheal fascia and superior thyroid vessels","C":"Four parathyroid glands are present anteriorly on the thyroid, arranged symmetrically along the midline and identifiable intraoperatively by their characteristic bright-red vascular appearance","D":"Three parathyroid glands are typically found on the posterior thyroid surface, while one ectopic gland is routinely located within the thymus in the anterior mediastinum as a normal anatomical variant"},"explanation":"There are normally four parathyroid glands, each approximately 6×3×2 mm and dark brown due to their fat content. They lie on the posterior surface of the superior and inferior poles of the thyroid lobes. Ectopic positions (mediastinal, intrathyroidal) occur in a minority of individuals, not as a typical arrangement. The glands are closely adherent to the posterior thyroid and are at surgical risk during thyroidectomy; their inadvertent removal or devascularization causes acute hypoparathyroidism with hypocalcemia and tetany. Humans normally have four parathyroid glands, one behind each upper and lower pole of the thyroid, not six with four along the trachea and esophagus. There are normally four parathyroid glands on the posterior, not anterior, surface of the thyroid. The glands lie behind the upper and lower thyroid poles and look like dark brown fat, not anteriorly with a bright-red appearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Post-thyroidectomy hypocalcemia = inadvertent parathyroid removal or devascularization. Know the posterior thyroid location as the anatomical basis for this surgical complication. COMLEX: The OMM sources give no separate parathyroid level; the thyroid region has reflex levels C4–C6 and T2 with sympathetic T1–T4 (OMM atlas Ch 15) vs the head-and-neck range T1–T5 (OMM course map), so the lower cervical and upper thoracic spine are relevant in suspected hypoparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism occurs when parathyroid glands are removed or fail, causing hypocalcemia and tetany","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Humans normally have four parathyroid glands, one behind eac","B":"There are normally four parathyroid glands on the posterior,","C":"The glands lie behind the upper and lower thyroid poles and ","D":"Ectopic positions (mediastinal"}},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the Physiological Anatomy of the Parathyroid Glands","batch":1,"stem":"Histological examination of parathyroid gland tissue reveals two distinct cell populations. Which of the following correctly matches each cell type with its known functional role?","choices":{"D":"Chief cells have pale cytoplasm with central nuclei and are the primary secretors of PTH; oxyphil cells are larger, with granular, mitochondria-rich eosinophilic cytoplasm, and their secretory role is uncertain","A":"Chief cells contain abundant mitochondria conferring granular eosinophilic cytoplasm and are responsible for calcitonin synthesis; oxyphil cells secrete PTH in response to hypocalcemia through a cAMP-dependent exocytosis mechanism","B":"Oxyphil cells are the primary secretors of PTH and are identifiable by their pale cytoplasm and small, dark-staining nuclei; chief cells are non-secretory stromal support cells that maintain the parathyroid microenvironment","C":"Both chief cells and oxyphil cells secrete PTH in response to hypocalcemia, but chief cells respond to acute calcium changes while oxyphil cells maintain tonic baseline PTH secretion during normocalcemic states","E":"Chief cells are progenitor cells that differentiate into PTH-secreting oxyphil cells under chronic hypocalcemic stimulation; the ratio of chief to oxyphil cells reflects the degree of parathyroid gland activation"},"explanation":"The parathyroid gland contains two main cell types. Chief cells have pale, lipid-containing cytoplasm and a centrally placed nucleus; they synthesize and secrete PTH as the gland's primary secretory cells. Oxyphil cells are larger and contain abundant mitochondria giving them a granular, eosinophilic appearance; their secretory function is not clearly established and they may represent depleted or transitional chief cells. This distinction is testable in histology-based board questions, particularly regarding parathyroid adenomas (predominantly chief cell origin). Chief cells, not oxyphil cells, are the primary PTH secretors, and chief cells are not non-secretory stromal cells. Chief cells are the primary secretory cells; the secretory function of oxyphil cells is not clearly established, so the described tonic secretion role is not given. Oxyphil cells are believed to be modified or depleted chief cells that no longer secrete hormone, not PTH-secreting descendants of chief cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Parathyroid adenomas are almost always composed of chief cells. Oxyphil adenomas exist but are rare. First Aid emphasizes chief cells as the PTH source. COMLEX: Chief cell hyperplasia vs. adenoma vs. carcinoma drives different management strategies in primary hyperparathyroidism; osteopathic physicians must distinguish these on clinical exam and labs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism occurs when parathyroid glands are removed or fail, causing hypocalcemia and tetany","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"Chief cells, not oxyphil cells, are the primary PTH secretor","C":"Chief cells are the primary secretory cells; the secretory f","E":"Oxyphil cells are believed to be modified or depleted chief "}},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the Physiological Anatomy of the Parathyroid Glands","batch":1,"stem":"PTH is synthesized as a 110-amino acid preprohormone and undergoes sequential cleavage before secretion. Which of the following correctly describes a key characteristic of PTH fragments and their renal clearance?","choices":{"C":"The N-terminal 1–34 fragment of PTH retains full biological activity; intact PTH (84 amino acids) is removed by the kidneys within minutes, whereas many fragments persist for hours and account for a large share of hormonal activity","A":"The C-terminal fragment of PTH retains full biological activity with a longer half-life than the intact hormone; the N-terminal fragment is a non-functional cleavage product rapidly degraded by hepatic enzymes within seconds of entering the portal circulation","B":"Intact PTH must be cleaved to the 1–34 N-terminal fragment in peripheral tissues before binding to PTH receptors; cleavage occurs in osteoblasts and renal tubular cells and is the rate-limiting step in PTH biological action","D":"All circulating PTH fragments are biologically inactive; only the intact 84-amino acid molecule binds to the PTH receptor, and all fragments represent end-stage degradation products cleared uniformly by renal glomerular filtration","E":"The middle region (amino acids 35–84) of PTH contains the receptor-binding domain and retains full hormonal activity; N-terminal and C-terminal fragments are equally inactive and cleared at identical rates regardless of renal function"},"explanation":"PTH is synthesized as preproPTH (110 AA) → proPTH (90 AA) → PTH (84 AA). The N-terminal 1–34 AA fragment retains full biological activity at PTH receptors. Guyton (course reading) says the kidneys rapidly remove the whole 84-amino acid hormone within minutes but fail to remove many of the fragments for hours, so a large share of the hormonal activity is caused by the fragments. Fragments are therefore neither uniformly inactive nor cleared at the same rate as the intact hormone, and the receptor-active region lies at the N terminus, not the C terminus or the middle region.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Intact PTH\" two-site immunometric assays measure only the 84-AA form and are preferred in CKD. Older assays overestimate PTH in renal failure due to C-terminal fragment accumulation — a classic biochemical trap. COMLEX: Renal osteodystrophy in CKD involves secondary hyperparathyroidism; treatment includes phosphate binders, calcitriol, and calcimimetics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism occurs when parathyroid glands are removed or fail, causing hypocalcemia and tetany","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Fragments are therefore neither uniformly inactive","D":"The N-terminal 1–34 AA fragmen"}},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the Parathyroid Hormone Effects on Extracellular Fluid Calcium and Phosphate Concentrations","batch":1,"stem":"PTH produces two distinct phases of bone calcium mobilization. Which of the following best describes the rapid phase of PTH-mediated bone calcium release occurring within minutes to hours of PTH elevation?","choices":{"A":"Activation of the osteocytic membrane pump system, in which PTH-stimulated osteocytes and surrounding osteoblastic lining cells transfer calcium from the bone canalicular fluid compartment into the ECF without destruction of organic bone matrix","B":"PTH immediately stimulates differentiation of circulating monocyte precursors into mature osteoclasts, which begin dissolving hydroxyapatite crystals within 15–30 minutes through acid secretion at the ruffled border membrane","C":"PTH binds directly to osteoclast surface receptors, activating H+ ATPase at the ruffled border and dissolving the mineralized matrix within minutes; this is the primary mechanism for the rapid rise in serum calcium seen in acute hyperparathyroidism","D":"PTH activates matrix metalloproteinases in periosteal fibroblasts within minutes, rapidly degrading organic bone matrix collagen and releasing calcium bound to collagen cross-links into the extravascular space","E":"PTH activates alkaline phosphatase in cortical osteoblasts through a cAMP-dependent mechanism, cleaving inorganic pyrophosphate from the bone matrix and releasing free calcium ions into the surrounding extravascular fluid compartment"},"explanation":"The rapid bone effect of PTH (within minutes to hours) is mediated by the osteocytic membrane pump: PTH activates osteocytes and associated lining osteoblasts to pump calcium from bone canalicular fluid into the ECF without matrix destruction. This is a non-destructive, rapid calcium transfer mechanism. The slower phase (hours to days) involves RANKL-mediated osteoclast activation and new osteoclast formation, which does involve organic matrix dissolution. Importantly, PTH receptors are absent on mature osteoclasts; PTH acts indirectly via osteoblast RANKL/OPG signaling. The rapid phase is osteolysis by the osteocytic membrane pump, without resorption of the bone's fibrous and gel matrix; matrix resorption belongs to the slow osteoclastic phase. The rapid phase is the PTH-activated osteocytic calcium pump; osteoblast alkaline phosphatase breaks down pyrophosphate to promote calcification rather than releasing calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH receptors are on osteoblasts, NOT osteoclasts. PTH stimulates osteoblasts → RANKL → activates osteoclasts (indirect). The rapid osteolytic phase via the osteocytic pump is a classic physiology distinction from the slower osteoclastic phase. COMLEX: High-turnover bone disease from PTH excess leads to subperiosteal bone resorption visible on X-ray — classic clinical finding in primary hyperparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — PTH is the master regulator of ECF calcium and phosphate through coordinated effects on bone, kidney, and intestine via 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The slower phase (hours to day","C":"Importantly, PTH receptors are","D":"The rapid phase is osteolysis by the osteocytic membrane pum","E":"The rapid phase is the PTH-activated osteocytic calcium pump"}},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the Parathyroid Hormone Effects on Extracellular Fluid Calcium and Phosphate Concentrations","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the direct renal tubular effects of PTH on calcium and phosphate handling in the nephron?","choices":{"D":"PTH increases calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and the distal convoluted tubule, while simultaneously decreasing phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule by reducing NaPi-2a and NaPi-2c cotransporter expression","A":"PTH increases both calcium and phosphate reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule through a shared sodium-dependent cotransport mechanism; its net effect on the distal nephron is to decrease calcium reabsorption to prevent hypercalcemia during PTH excess","B":"PTH decreases calcium reabsorption in the proximal tubule and the loop of Henle while increasing distal convoluted tubule phosphate reabsorption, resulting in hypocalciuria and hyperphosphaturia as the primary urinary signature of elevated PTH","C":"PTH acts exclusively on the collecting duct principal cells, activating vasopressin-independent aquaporin channels to increase free water reabsorption, which concentrates both calcium and phosphate in the tubular lumen and reduces their urinary excretion","E":"PTH increases phosphate reabsorption in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and simultaneously decreases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, producing net hyperphosphatemia and hypocalciuria in primary hyperparathyroidism"},"explanation":"PTH acts on the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and the distal convoluted tubule to increase calcium reabsorption via TRPV5 channel upregulation (cAMP-mediated). In the proximal tubule, PTH decreases expression of NaPi-2a and NaPi-2c sodium-phosphate cotransporters, reducing phosphate reabsorption and causing phosphaturia. This dual effect — calciotrophic and phosphaturic — explains the hallmark lab findings of primary hyperparathyroidism: hypercalcemia + hypophosphatemia + hyperphosphaturia. Hypercalciuria can occur when the filtered calcium load exceeds tubular reabsorptive capacity. PTH decreases proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption and increases calcium reabsorption, mainly in the thick ascending limb and distal tubules. PTH increases, not decreases, calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubule, and it decreases phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. PTH acts on the proximal tubule (phosphate) and on the thick ascending limb and distal tubules (calcium), not on collecting duct aquaporins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The phosphaturic effect of PTH is why primary hyperparathyroidism causes hypophosphatemia. First Aid classic: elevated PTH → ↑Ca, ↓PO4, ↑urine cAMP, ↑1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. COMLEX: Parathyroid dysfunction — viscerosomatic reflexes from the neck and upper thoracic region may accompany symptoms including bone pain and nephrolithiasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — PTH is the master regulator of ECF calcium and phosphate through coordinated effects on bone, kidney, and intestine via 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"PTH decreases proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption and in","B":"PTH increases, not decreases, calcium reabsorption in the th","C":"PTH acts on the proximal tubule (phosphate) and on the thick"}},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the Parathyroid Hormone Effects on Extracellular Fluid Calcium and Phosphate Concentrations","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which PTH increases intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"B":"PTH stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule to convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), which induces calbindin-D9K and other calcium transport proteins in enterocytes to mediate transcellular absorption","A":"PTH binds directly to PTH/PTHrP receptors on duodenal enterocytes, activating adenylyl cyclase and protein kinase A, which phosphorylates the apical TRPV6 calcium channel to increase transcellular calcium influx independent of calcitriol synthesis","C":"PTH activates intestinal alkaline phosphatase in the brush border of the jejunum and ileum, cleaving inorganic phosphate groups from dietary calcium-phosphate complexes and increasing the pool of free ionized calcium available for passive paracellular absorption","D":"PTH increases intestinal permeability by phosphorylating tight junction proteins claudin-2 and occludin, allowing enhanced paracellular calcium flux that bypasses the saturable transcellular transport pathway in the duodenum and upper jejunum","E":"PTH stimulates osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, and the calcium released into the ECF is recycled back through the intestinal wall via a calcium-sensing transporter that preferentially absorbs calcium from the intestinal lumen when ECF calcium rises"},"explanation":"PTH does not have direct receptors on intestinal epithelial cells. Its effect on intestinal calcium absorption is entirely indirect: PTH stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule, converting 25-hydroxyvitamin D to 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol). Calcitriol acts as a nuclear hormone in enterocytes, inducing synthesis of calbindin (calcium-binding protein), TRPV6 (apical uptake channel), and PMCA1b (basolateral extrusion pump) to facilitate active transcellular calcium absorption, primarily in the duodenum and upper jejunum. This indirect pathway explains why hypoparathyroidism reduces intestinal calcium absorption. PTH raises intestinal calcium absorption indirectly, by increasing renal formation of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, which induces calbindin, not by activating brush-border alkaline phosphatase. PTH's intestinal effect is indirect, through renal 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol inducing calbindin-mediated calcium transport, not phosphorylation of tight junction proteins. The course gives the indirect calcitriol-mediated mechanism, not recycling of bone-derived calcium through a calcium-sensing transporter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH → kidney → calcitriol → intestine. No direct PTH receptor on enterocytes. First Aid: PTH is the most important regulator of 1-alpha-hydroxylase. Hypoparathyroidism → low calcitriol → low intestinal Ca absorption → hypocalcemia. COMLEX: Vitamin D deficiency mimics lost intestinal calcium absorption, driving secondary hyperparathyroidism even with normal PTH receptor function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — PTH is the master regulator of ECF calcium and phosphate through coordinated effects on bone, kidney, and intestine via 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"PTH raises intestinal calcium absorption indirectly, by incr","D":"PTH's intestinal effect is indirect, through renal 1,25-dihy","E":"The course gives the indirect calcitriol-mediated mechanism,"}},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the process of regulation of Parathyroid hormone secretion","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary physiological stimulus for PTH secretion and the time course of parathyroid gland adaptation to sustained chronic hypocalcemia?","choices":{"A":"Decreased extracellular fluid ionized calcium concentration is the primary stimulus for PTH secretion, producing a detectable rise in PTH within minutes; chronic sustained hypocalcemia over weeks leads to parathyroid gland hypertrophy that can increase gland mass up to five-fold","B":"Elevated extracellular phosphate is the primary stimulus for PTH secretion, acting through a phosphate-sensing receptor distinct from the CaSR; the parathyroid response to hyperphosphatemia is slower than to hypocalcemia, requiring 12–24 hours to generate a measurable increase in circulating PTH","C":"Decreased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is the primary and most rapid stimulus for PTH secretion; hypocalcemia is a secondary trigger requiring 4–6 hours to generate a measurable PTH response as the gland must first deplete its pre-formed secretory granule reserve","D":"Decreased extracellular magnesium is the primary trigger for PTH secretion; ionized calcium directly suppresses rather than stimulates PTH by binding to the PTH gene promoter, and hypomagnesemia alone causes the fivefold increase in parathyroid mass seen in secondary hyperparathyroidism","E":"Decreased extracellular ionized calcium triggers PTH secretion within hours, and the response plateaus within 6–8 hours regardless of stimulus duration; gland hypertrophy does not occur physiologically and is seen only in pathological conditions such as CKD or malignancy"},"explanation":"The primary regulator of PTH secretion is ionized calcium in the ECF. When ECF calcium falls, PTH secretion increases within minutes, providing rapid correction. If hypocalcemia is sustained chronically (malabsorption, vitamin D deficiency, CKD), the parathyroid glands undergo hypertrophy that can increase gland mass up to five times normal — representing compensatory secondary hyperparathyroidism. This gland enlargement explains the markedly elevated PTH and enlarged glands on imaging in patients with long-standing CKD or chronic vitamin D deficiency. Ionized calcium, not phosphate, is the major regulator of PTH release, sensed by the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor. Ionized calcium is the primary stimulus and PTH rises within minutes, whereas low calcitriol is not the main stimulus. Low calcium, not low magnesium, is the major PTH trigger, sensed by a membrane calcium-sensing receptor; severe hypomagnesemia actually decreases PTH secretion. PTH rises within minutes, and persistent low calcium makes the glands hypertrophy 5-fold or more, as happens physiologically in pregnancy and lactation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CaSR senses ionized Ca, not total Ca. Minutes → PTH released from granules. Days → increased PTH synthesis. Weeks → gland hypertrophy. First Aid: secondary hyperparathyroidism causes gland hypertrophy vs. autonomous adenoma in primary hyperparathyroidism. COMLEX: Secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD leads to renal osteodystrophy; screen with intact PTH and treat early to prevent osteitis fibrosa cystica.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism results from autonomous PTH oversecretion, typically from a parathyroid adenoma, causing hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Ionized calcium, not phosphate, is the major regulator of PT","C":"Ionized calcium is the primary stimulus and PTH rises within","D":"Low calcium, not low magnesium, is the major PTH trigger, se","E":"PTH rises within minutes, and persistent low calcium makes t"}},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the process of regulation of Parathyroid hormone secretion","batch":1,"stem":"The calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) on parathyroid chief cells is the key molecular detector of ECF calcium concentration. Which of the following correctly describes the intracellular signaling cascade activated when extracellular calcium rises and binds to CaSR?","choices":{"C":"CaSR is a G protein–coupled receptor that activates phospholipase C via Gq, generating IP3 and DAG; IP3 triggers calcium release from the endoplasmic reticulum, raising intracellular calcium concentration and inhibiting PTH secretory granule exocytosis","A":"CaSR is a receptor tyrosine kinase that undergoes dimerization and autophosphorylation upon calcium binding, activating the Ras/MAPK pathway, which phosphorylates and inactivates transcription factors required for PTH gene expression in parathyroid chief cells","B":"CaSR is a ligand-gated cation channel that allows direct calcium influx upon opening; the resulting rise in intracellular calcium activates calcineurin, which dephosphorylates NFAT and represses the PTH gene promoter in the chief cell nucleus","D":"CaSR couples to Gs protein to activate adenylyl cyclase, generating cAMP that activates protein kinase A; PKA phosphorylates PTH secretory vesicles and tags them for lysosomal degradation rather than plasma membrane exocytosis when calcium is elevated","E":"CaSR functions as a nuclear receptor that translocates to the nucleus upon extracellular calcium binding and directly suppresses PTH transcription by competing with the vitamin D receptor for the negative VDRE in the PTH gene promoter region"},"explanation":"The calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) is a class C G protein–coupled receptor coupled to Gq/11. When extracellular calcium activates CaSR, Gq/11 stimulates phospholipase C (PLC), which cleaves PIP2 into IP3 and DAG. IP3 triggers calcium release from the endoplasmic reticulum, raising cytoplasmic calcium concentration. This elevated intracellular calcium suppresses PTH secretory granule exocytosis. Calcimimetic drugs (e.g., cinacalcet) allosterically potentiate CaSR, making it more sensitive to calcium and thus suppressing PTH in secondary hyperparathyroidism of CKD. Loss-of-function CaSR mutations cause familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH). The calcium-sensing receptor is a G protein–coupled receptor that activates phospholipase C, not a receptor tyrosine kinase. The calcium-sensing receptor is a G protein–coupled receptor acting through phospholipase C and IP3, not a ligand-gated cation channel. The calcium-sensing receptor activates phospholipase C to form IP3 and DAG, not adenylyl cyclase and cAMP. The calcium-sensing receptor is a G protein–coupled receptor in the parathyroid cell membrane, not a nuclear receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CaSR → Gq → PLC → IP3/DAG → ↑intracellular Ca → ↓PTH exocytosis. Cinacalcet potentiates CaSR — used in secondary hyperparathyroidism (CKD) and parathyroid carcinoma. Loss-of-function CaSR mutations → familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH): mild hypercalcemia with low urine calcium. COMLEX: FHH vs. primary hyperparathyroidism distinguished by urine Ca:Cr ratio.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism results from autonomous PTH oversecretion, typically from a parathyroid adenoma, causing hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The calcium-sensing receptor is a G protein–coupled receptor that activates phos","B":"The calcium-sensing receptor is a G protein–coupled receptor acting through phos","D":"The calcium-sensing receptor activates phospholipase C to fo","E":"The calcium-sensing receptor is a G protein–coupled receptor in the parathyroid "}},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the process of regulation of Parathyroid hormone secretion","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which increased vitamin D activity suppresses PTH secretion from parathyroid chief cells?","choices":{"E":"Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol) suppresses PTH secretion both directly, by binding to the vitamin D receptor (VDR) in parathyroid chief cells and acting on a negative vitamin D response element in the PTH gene promoter to reduce PTH transcription, and indirectly, by raising ECF calcium through increased intestinal absorption","A":"Elevated serum phosphate suppresses PTH secretion by directly binding to CaSR and mimicking the conformational change induced by calcium, triggering the same Gq/PLC/IP3 signaling cascade that elevated ionized calcium uses to inhibit PTH granule exocytosis","B":"Hypomagnesemia suppresses PTH secretion by impairing voltage-gated calcium channels required for parathyroid chief cell depolarization and PTH granule membrane fusion, reducing exocytosis below the tonic baseline secretory rate","C":"Estrogen directly suppresses PTH secretion by binding to estrogen response elements in the PTH gene promoter and repressing transcription; postmenopausal estrogen deficiency therefore causes secondary hyperparathyroidism as an early hormonal consequence","D":"Cortisol at physiological concentrations suppresses PTH by increasing renal calcium reabsorption, which raises ECF calcium above the CaSR set point and reduces parathyroid chief cell secretory activity through a glucocorticoid receptor-independent mechanism"},"explanation":"Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) suppresses PTH by two complementary mechanisms: (1) a direct nuclear effect — calcitriol binds to the vitamin D receptor (VDR) in parathyroid chief cells and acts on a negative vitamin D response element (nVDRE) in the PTH gene promoter, reducing PTH transcription; (2) an indirect effect — by increasing intestinal calcium absorption, calcitriol raises ECF calcium, which activates CaSR and further suppresses PTH secretion. Other suppressors of PTH include elevated ECF calcium (from any cause) and non-PTH-mediated bone resorption. Note: hypomagnesemia paradoxically can impair both PTH secretion and peripheral PTH action. High phosphate increases rather than suppresses PTH secretion. Severe hypomagnesemia does lower PTH secretion, but that is not how vitamin D acts; calcitriol gives feedback inhibition of PTH synthesis and raises calcium. Estrogen's described skeletal action is to stimulate osteoprotegerin production, not to repress PTH gene transcription.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin D directly suppresses PTH transcription via nVDRE — this is the pharmacological basis for using calcitriol/vitamin D analogs in secondary hyperparathyroidism. First Aid: ↑Calcitriol → ↑ECF Ca → ↓PTH (indirect), plus direct PTH gene suppression. COMLEX: Vitamin D deficiency is the most common nutritional cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism; screen in all patients with limited sun exposure, malabsorption, or dark skin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism results from autonomous PTH oversecretion, typically from a parathyroid adenoma, causing hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"High phosphate increases rather than suppresses PTH secretio","B":"Severe hypomagnesemia does lower PTH secretion, but that is ","C":"Estrogen's described skeletal action is to stimulate osteopr"}},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the physiological functions of calcitonin","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the anatomical source, peptide structure, and primary secretory stimulus for calcitonin?","choices":{"D":"Calcitonin is a 32-amino acid peptide hormone secreted by parafollicular C cells within the thyroid gland in response to elevated serum calcium, making its secretory stimulus physiologically opposite to that of PTH","A":"Calcitonin is a 32-amino acid peptide secreted by parathyroid chief cells in response to hypocalcemia; it acts synergistically with PTH to restore calcium levels by stimulating osteoblast-mediated bone formation and increasing renal calcium reabsorption in the distal nephron","B":"Calcitonin is an 84-amino acid glycoprotein secreted by thyroid follicular cells in response to elevated TSH; it is co-released with thyroglobulin and acts on osteoblasts to suppress alkaline phosphatase activity and reduce bone matrix mineralization","C":"Calcitonin is a 32-amino acid peptide secreted by ultimobranchial body remnants embedded within the parathyroid glands in response to hypocalcemia; it acts on the renal collecting duct to increase calcium reabsorption and restore ECF calcium toward normal","E":"Calcitonin is a 32-amino acid peptide synthesized by the hypothalamic paraventricular nucleus in response to hypercalcemia; it is transported to the posterior pituitary for storage and release, acting on osteoclasts to inhibit bone resorption and lower serum calcium"},"explanation":"Calcitonin is produced by parafollicular C cells (clear cells), which constitute approximately 0.1% of thyroid cell mass and are derived from the ultimobranchial body (neural crest origin). It is a 32-amino acid peptide with a molecular weight of approximately 3,400 Da. The primary stimulus is elevated ECF calcium — directly opposite to the hypocalcemic stimulus for PTH. Calcitonin is the physiological antagonist of PTH in bone and kidney, though its role is substantially weaker in adults compared to PTH. Calcitonin is made by thyroid parafollicular C cells, not parathyroid chief cells, and is stimulated by high, not low, calcium. Calcitonin is a 32-amino acid peptide from parafollicular C cells stimulated by elevated calcium; it is not an 84-amino acid glycoprotein from follicular cells released with TSH. Calcitonin comes from parafollicular C cells in the thyroid and is stimulated by elevated calcium, not hypocalcemia. Calcitonin is made by thyroid C cells in response to hypercalcemia, not by the hypothalamus and posterior pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C cells = parafollicular, neural crest–derived, scattered among follicular cells. Calcitonin is the tumor marker for medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) driven by RET proto-oncogene mutations. First Aid: MEN 2A and 2B both feature MTC with elevated calcitonin; serum calcitonin screens family members. COMLEX: A thyroid nodule with elevated calcitonin and diarrhea/flushing — think MTC; osteopathic somatic dysfunction at the thyroid reflex levels (C4–C6 and T2 with sympathetic supply T1–T4, OMM atlas Ch 15; head and neck T1–T5, OMM course map) may accompany thyroid pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from calcitonin-secreting parafollicular C cells, making serum calcitonin an important tumor marker","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Calcitonin is made by thyroid parafollicular C cells, not pa","B":"Calcitonin is a 32-amino acid peptide from parafollicular C ","C":"Calcitonin comes from parafollicular C cells in the thyroid ","E":"Calcitonin is made by thyroid C cells in response to hyperca"}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the physiological functions of calcitonin","batch":1,"stem":"Calcitonin exerts its anti-hypercalcemic effect through two temporally distinct mechanisms on osteoclasts. Which of the following correctly describes both the immediate and prolonged effects of calcitonin on bone?","choices":{"B":"Calcitonin immediately decreases the absorptive activity of existing mature osteoclasts; with prolonged exposure, it decreases the formation of new osteoclasts, although osteoblast activity is simultaneously reduced, resulting in minimal net effect on total bone calcium balance over time","A":"Calcitonin immediately stimulates osteoblast differentiation from mesenchymal precursors, increasing bone matrix synthesis within hours; with prolonged exposure, it activates caspase-dependent apoptosis in mature osteoclasts, causing net bone formation to exceed resorption for weeks","C":"Calcitonin immediately increases osteoclast motility, causing existing osteoclasts to migrate away from resorption lacunae into quiescent surface zones; with prolonged exposure, it increases sclerostin production by osteocytes, indirectly inhibiting osteoblast-driven RANKL expression","D":"Calcitonin immediately suppresses PTH secretion from the parathyroid gland through a paracrine mechanism, removing the major stimulus for osteoclast activation; with prolonged exposure, it stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity to increase calcitriol synthesis and shift balance toward calcium deposition","E":"Calcitonin immediately blocks calcium efflux from the bone canalicular system by inhibiting the osteocytic membrane pump, preventing calcium transfer from bone fluid to the ECF; with prolonged exposure, it permanently fuses osteocyte gap junctions, reducing the calcium-transporting canalicular network"},"explanation":"Calcitonin acts on calcitonin receptors (CTRs) expressed on mature osteoclasts. Immediately, it inhibits ruffled border activity and reduces the absorptive function of existing osteoclasts, causing a rapid drop in serum calcium during hypercalcemic states. With prolonged calcitonin exposure, new osteoclast formation is also reduced; however, osteoblast activity is simultaneously decreased, so the net long-term effect on calcium balance is minimal. This temporal dissociation explains why calcitonin's calcium-lowering effect is transient, and why it is not used for long-term hypercalcemia management in adults. Calcitonin acts on osteoclasts, inhibiting their activity and later reducing their formation, rather than stimulating osteoblast differentiation. Calcitonin immediately decreases the absorptive activity of existing osteoclasts rather than making them migrate away, and a sclerostin mechanism is not described. Rather than suppressing PTH, any fall in calcium caused by calcitonin powerfully stimulates PTH secretion within hours. Calcitonin's prolonged effect is fewer new osteoclasts and then fewer osteoblasts, not permanent fusion of osteocyte gap junctions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Calcitonin has direct receptors on osteoclasts (unlike PTH, which acts via osteoblasts). Immediate: inhibits osteoclast absorptive activity. Prolonged: reduces osteoclastogenesis but also osteoblastogenesis → net transient effect. COMLEX: Salmon calcitonin nasal spray is used for acute pain management in Paget's disease and osteoporotic vertebral compression fractures; COMLEX tests its analgesic and anti-resorptive mechanisms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from calcitonin-secreting parafollicular C cells, making serum calcitonin an important tumor marker","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Calcitonin acts on osteoclasts, inhibiting their activity an","C":"Calcitonin immediately decreases the absorptive activity of ","D":"Rather than suppressing PTH, any fall in calcium caused by c","E":"Calcitonin's prolonged effect is fewer new osteoclasts and t"}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the physiological functions of calcitonin","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why calcitonin deficiency following total thyroidectomy does not result in clinically significant hypercalcemia in adult patients?","choices":{"C":"After skeletal maturity, osteoclasts permanently downregulate calcitonin receptor expression, rendering them completely insensitive to calcitonin's inhibitory effects; therefore, calcitonin loss has no measurable impact on osteoclast activity at any point after puberty","A":"Calcitonin deficiency is fully compensated by upregulation of PTH secretion from the intact parathyroid glands; the resulting secondary hyperparathyroidism maintains serum calcium within the normal range through increased bone resorption and renal calcium retention with no clinical consequences","B":"Calcitonin plays only a minor role in adult calcium homeostasis because PTH is the dominant regulator, adult bone turnover is slow compared with that of growing children, and any hypercalcemic tendency from calcitonin loss is quickly corrected by CaSR-mediated PTH suppression","D":"Residual ultimobranchial body tissue invariably persists in the neck after thyroidectomy and provides sufficient calcitonin secretion to maintain bone calcium balance; complete calcitonin deficiency only occurs after combined thyroidectomy and radical central neck dissection","E":"Vitamin D–mediated intestinal calcium absorption is so precisely regulated by 1-alpha-hydroxylase that the loss of calcitonin's anti-resorptive signal is offset by a proportional decrease in intestinal calcium absorption, maintaining ECF calcium without any change in PTH levels"},"explanation":"In adults, calcitonin's physiological importance is limited compared to PTH. Adult bone remodeling is slow (unlike growing children or patients with Paget's disease, where calcitonin is more relevant), so calcitonin deficiency does not substantially elevate ECF calcium through bone resorption. Moreover, PTH is a far more rapid and powerful regulator — even a small rise in ECF calcium activates CaSR on parathyroid cells to suppress PTH, which reduces bone resorption and renal calcium reabsorption to normalize calcium. The clinical observation that thyroidectomy patients maintain normal serum calcium confirms this physiological redundancy. Calcitonin's role is minor in adults because bone turnover is slow and PTH dominates, not because osteoclasts permanently lose calcitonin receptors. Calcitonin deficiency is tolerated because calcitonin has a minor adult role, not because ultimobranchial remnants persist after thyroidectomy. The course attributes normal calcium to the minor role of calcitonin and the dominance of PTH, not to a proportional decrease in intestinal absorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Calcitonin is physiologically important in children and pregnant/lactating women (high bone turnover) and in Paget's disease. In adults, it is a \"weak\" hormone. First Aid: Calcitonin is clinically useful for Paget's disease and acute hypercalcemia (transient effect only). COMLEX: After thyroidectomy, monitor calcium to assess parathyroid viability (hypoparathyroidism is the real risk, not calcitonin deficiency).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from calcitonin-secreting parafollicular C cells, making serum calcitonin an important tumor marker","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Calcitonin's role is minor in adults because bone turnover i","D":"Calcitonin deficiency is tolerated because calcitonin has a ","E":"The course attributes normal calcium to the minor role of ca"}},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the Physiological Anatomy of the Parathyroid Glands","batch":2,"stem":"During a radical neck dissection for laryngeal cancer, three normal parathyroid glands are removed and the fourth is left intact with a good blood supply. On postoperative day 2 the patient has perioral tingling and a serum calcium of 7.6 mg/dL, and he is treated with calcium and calcitriol. Two months later, after the supplements have been stopped, his serum calcium and PTH are normal. Which of the following best explains his recovery?","choices":{"E":"The remaining parathyroid tissue hypertrophies and can take over the PTH secretion of all four of the glands","A":"Calcitonin secretion falls in response to the low calcium, and this fall by itself returns serum calcium to normal","B":"Oxyphil cells in the remaining gland convert into the main PTH-secreting cell population of the parathyroids","C":"The kidneys begin secreting a PTH-like peptide that replaces the hormone formerly made by the lost glands","D":"Exchangeable calcium in bone buffers plasma calcium indefinitely, so PTH is not needed after the acute phase"},"explanation":"Removal of half of the parathyroid glands usually causes no major physiological abnormality. Removal of three of the four normal glands causes transient hypoparathyroidism, but even a small quantity of remaining parathyroid tissue is usually capable of hypertrophying enough to perform the function of all the glands; a persistent fall in calcium is the stimulus for this growth. Calcitonin has a weak effect on plasma calcium in adults, and the PTH system overrides it. Oxyphil cells are thought to be modified or depleted chief cells that no longer secrete hormone; the chief cells secrete most, if not all, of the PTH. The kidneys do not make a PTH-like hormone. Exchangeable bone calcium is a rapid first line of defense, but in prolonged calcium deficiency only the PTH mechanism is really important in maintaining plasma calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Four parathyroid glands lie behind the upper and lower thyroid poles, and chief cells secrete PTH; removing three of four causes transient hypoparathyroidism until the remnant hypertrophies. COMLEX: Hypocalcemia after neck surgery presents with perioral tingling, tetany and Chvostek and Trousseau signs, and it reflects injury to the parathyroids or their blood supply.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism, pseudohypoparathyroidism, and hyperparathyroidism","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Calcitonin has a weak effect o","C":"The kidneys do not make a PTH-"}},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the Physiological Anatomy of the Parathyroid Glands","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has persistent primary hyperparathyroidism after a neck exploration in which three normal-sized parathyroid glands were found and the left inferior gland could not be located. A sestamibi scan now shows a focus of uptake in the anterior mediastinum next to the thymus. Which embryologic fact best explains the location of her adenoma?","choices":{"B":"The inferior parathyroids arise from the third pharyngeal pouch together with the thymus and can descend with it","A":"The superior parathyroids arise from the third pharyngeal pouch and descend the farthest along with the thyroid","C":"The inferior parathyroids arise from the fourth pharyngeal pouch alongside the precursors of thyroid C cells","D":"All four parathyroids arise from the thyroglossal duct and can be left anywhere along its path down into the chest","E":"The parathyroids arise from neural crest cells that migrate into the mediastinum with the cardiac outflow tract"},"explanation":"The third pharyngeal pouch gives rise to the thymus (ventral wings) and the inferior parathyroids (dorsal wings), and structures from the third pouch end up below those from the fourth pouch. The fourth pouch gives rise to the superior parathyroids (dorsal wings) and to the ultimopharyngeal body (ventral wings), which forms the parafollicular C cells. Because the parathyroids develop from pharyngeal pouches that also form the thymus, a gland may be found anywhere along the pathway of descent, including the carotid sheath, the thymus and elsewhere in the anterior mediastinum. The thyroglossal duct marks the descent of the thyroid from the foramen cecum and explains ectopic thyroid tissue, not ectopic parathyroids. The parathyroids are endodermal pouch derivatives, not neural crest.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Third pouch: inferior parathyroids and thymus; fourth pouch: superior parathyroids and ultimopharyngeal body (C cells); DiGeorge syndrome reflects third and fourth pouch maldevelopment. COMLEX: When a parathyroid adenoma cannot be found in the neck, look along the path of descent, including the thymus and anterior mediastinum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism, pseudohypoparathyroidism, and hyperparathyroidism","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"The fourth pouch gives rise to"}},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the Physiological Anatomy of the Parathyroid Glands","batch":2,"stem":"A surgical resident asks why parathyroid glands were often removed by accident during thyroid operations before their importance was recognized, and why surgeons still find them hard to identify. Which description of normal parathyroid glands best answers this question?","choices":{"D":"A single gland sits on the thyroid isthmus, where it blends into the overlying pretracheal fascia","A":"Each gland is about 6 mm long and looks like dark brown fat or another small lobule of the thyroid","B":"Each gland lies deep within the thyroid follicles and cannot be seen on the outer surface of the lobe","C":"Each gland is about 3 cm long, bright yellow and encapsulated, so it is mistaken for a lymph node","E":"The glands lie inside the carotid sheath beside the vagus nerve, outside the usual operative field"},"explanation":"Humans normally have four parathyroid glands located immediately behind the thyroid gland, one behind each upper and each lower pole. Each gland is about 6 mm long, 3 mm wide and 2 mm thick and has the macroscopic appearance of dark brown fat. The glands are difficult to locate during thyroid operations because they often look like just another lobule of the thyroid gland, and before their importance was recognized, total or subtotal thyroidectomy frequently removed them as well. They are not buried within the thyroid follicles, there are normally four rather than one, and the carotid sheath is an ectopic site along the path of descent rather than their usual location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Four parathyroid glands behind the upper and lower thyroid poles, each about 6 x 3 x 2 mm with a dark brown-fat appearance; chief cells secrete PTH, and oxyphil cells are thought to be depleted chief cells. COMLEX: Hypoparathyroidism after thyroid surgery comes from removal of the glands or injury to their blood supply and presents with tetany and hypocalcemia with hyperphosphatemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Hypoparathyroidism, pseudohypoparathyroidism, and hyperparathyroidism","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"They are not buried within the","C":"Each gland is about 6 mm long,","D":"They are not buried within the"}},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the Parathyroid Hormone Effects on Extracellular Fluid Calcium and Phosphate Concentrations","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has had untreated primary hyperparathyroidism for several years. A bone biopsy shows resorption cavities containing increased numbers of large, multinucleated osteoclasts, along with increased osteoblastic activity. Which mechanism best explains how PTH increases the number and activity of her osteoclasts?","choices":{"A":"PTH binds osteoblasts and osteocytes, which raise RANKL and lower osteoprotegerin, so preosteoclasts mature","B":"PTH binds PTH receptors on mature osteoclasts directly, so no other bone cell is needed to activate them","C":"PTH stimulates thyroid C cells to release calcitonin, which recruits new osteoclasts from marrow precursors","D":"PTH lowers bone fluid calcium through the osteocytic pump, and the low calcium fuses monocytes into osteoclasts","E":"PTH suppresses osteoblasts completely, so unopposed osteoclasts expand into the space the osteoblasts vacate"},"explanation":"The osteoclasts do not have membrane receptors for PTH. Instead, PTH binds receptors on osteoblasts and osteocytes, which send secondary signals to the osteoclast line; the major signal is RANKL, which activates receptors on preosteoclasts and transforms them into mature osteoclasts. PTH also decreases production of osteoprotegerin, which would otherwise bind RANKL and block this differentiation. This slow phase of bone resorption takes several days to become well developed and can continue for months, unlike the rapid phase, in which the osteocytic membrane pump releases calcium phosphate salts without resorbing the bone matrix. After months of excess PTH, the weakened bone secondarily stimulates the osteoblasts, so both osteoblastic and osteoclastic activity increase, although resorption still exceeds deposition. Calcitonin inhibits rather than recruits osteoclasts. Osteoclasts do not have membrane receptors for PTH; PTH acts on osteoblasts and osteocytes, which raise RANKL and lower osteoprotegerin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH acts on PTH receptors on osteoblasts and osteocytes, which increase RANKL and decrease osteoprotegerin to drive osteoclast differentiation; osteoclasts themselves lack PTH receptors. Rapid phase (minutes to hours) = osteocytic membrane pump; slow phase (days to months) = osteoclast proliferation. COMLEX: Continuous PTH excess resorbs bone, whereas pulsatile recombinant PTH (teriparatide) increases osteoblastic activity and is used for osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Calcium and phosphate distribution and regulation in ECF and plasma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Osteoclasts do not have membrane receptors for PTH; PTH acts","E":"After months of excess PTH, th"}},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the Parathyroid Hormone Effects on Extracellular Fluid Calcium and Phosphate Concentrations","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with primary hyperparathyroidism undergoes 24-hour urine collection. Results: urine calcium 420 mg/day (elevated), urine phosphate 1,500 mg/day (elevated). Serum calcium is 11.5 mg/dL and intact PTH is 290 pg/mL. The nephrologist notes that despite PTH normally promoting renal calcium reabsorption, this patient has hypercalciuria. Which of the following best explains this paradox?","choices":{"B":"PTH increases calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and distal tubule, but the markedly elevated filtered calcium load from PTH-driven hypercalcemia exceeds the tubular reabsorptive maximum, causing net hypercalciuria despite enhanced retention","A":"PTH decreases calcium reabsorption in the proximal tubule and the tubular loss from this segment exceeds the gain from PTH-stimulated distal calcium retention, so the net tubular balance of calcium in primary hyperparathyroidism is always negative and causes hypercalciuria at any PTH level","C":"Elevated PTH selectively upregulates the sodium-calcium exchanger NCX1 in the collecting duct, and the associated sodium-wasting effect of PTH creates an osmotic diuresis that carries calcium passively into the urine along with free water regardless of distal calcium reabsorption","D":"PTH increases phosphate reabsorption in the thick ascending limb, and the resulting hyperphosphaturia creates a strong osmotic gradient that pulls calcium passively across the tubular epithelium into the tubular lumen through paracellular calcium transport pathways","E":"PTH-induced calcitriol synthesis in the proximal tubule provides feedback that directly downregulates TRPV5 calcium channels in the distal nephron, overriding PTH’s direct reabsorptive signal and shifting the kidney toward net calcium wasting when calcitriol levels are high"},"explanation":"PTH promotes calcium retention in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and distal convoluted tubule via TRPV5 channel upregulation. However, in primary hyperparathyroidism, PTH simultaneously drives bone resorption (raising serum calcium) and intestinal calcium absorption (via calcitriol). The resulting marked hypercalcemia greatly increases the filtered calcium load; when this exceeds the reabsorptive capacity of the tubule (tubular maximum for calcium, Tm-Ca), spillover into the urine produces hypercalciuria despite intact tubular retention mechanisms. Hypercalciuria in primary hyperparathyroidism is the primary risk factor for nephrolithiasis (calcium oxalate stones). PTH increases tubular calcium reabsorption; the hypercalciuria reflects the extra calcium from bone and gut that the kidneys must excrete, not lost proximal reabsorption. The high urine calcium reflects excess calcium mobilized from bone and absorbed from the gut that must be excreted, not NCX1 upregulation with osmotic diuresis. PTH decreases proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption rather than increasing it in the thick ascending limb. Vitamin D itself weakly increases renal tubular calcium reabsorption, so calcitriol does not override PTH to cause calcium wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypercalciuria in primary hyperparathyroidism despite PTH’s calcium-retaining renal effect = filtered load overloads tubular Tm. Nephrolithiasis (kidney stones) is the most common symptomatic presentation of primary hyperparathyroidism. First Aid mnemonic: \"Stones, Bones, Groans, Psychic Moans.\" COMLEX: Recurrent calcium oxalate nephrolithiasis with hypercalcemia and low phosphate — always check intact PTH to rule out primary hyperparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Calcium and phosphate distribution and regulation in ECF and plasma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"PTH increases tubular calcium reabsorption; the hypercalciur","C":"The high urine calcium reflects excess calcium mobilized fro","D":"PTH decreases proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption rather","E":"Vitamin D itself weakly increases renal tubular calcium reab"}},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the Parathyroid Hormone Effects on Extracellular Fluid Calcium and Phosphate Concentrations","batch":2,"stem":"In an experiment, PTH is infused continuously into an animal at a moderate rate. Plasma phosphate falls to a depressed level within 1 to 2 hours, whereas plasma calcium rises more slowly and reaches a plateau at about 4 hours. PTH also releases phosphate from bone. Which of the following best explains why plasma phosphate falls nonetheless?","choices":{"E":"PTH sharply reduces proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption, and the urinary loss outweighs release from bone","A":"PTH drives phosphate into newly formed hydroxyapatite, so bone takes up more phosphate than it releases into plasma","B":"PTH lowers intestinal phosphate absorption by suppressing the renal formation of 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D","C":"PTH acts on skeletal muscle cells, which take up phosphate from plasma to build their creatine phosphate stores","D":"PTH shifts phosphate into cells by stimulating pancreatic insulin secretion within minutes of the infusion"},"explanation":"When PTH is infused, calcium begins to rise and reaches a plateau in about 4 hours, but phosphate falls more rapidly and is depressed within 1 or 2 hours. The calcium rises because PTH increases calcium and phosphate absorption from bone and rapidly decreases renal calcium excretion. The phosphate falls because PTH strongly increases renal phosphate excretion by diminishing proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption, an effect usually great enough to override the extra phosphate absorbed from bone. PTH increases, rather than suppresses, renal formation of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, which raises intestinal absorption of both calcium and phosphate. PTH mobilizes bone mineral rather than depositing it, and it does not lower phosphate by driving it into muscle or other cells. In the kidney PTH also increases magnesium and hydrogen ion reabsorption and decreases sodium, potassium and amino acid reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH raises serum calcium and lowers serum phosphate (Phosphate-Trashing Hormone): it increases calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubule, decreases proximal phosphate reabsorption and increases 1,25-(OH)2 vitamin D. COMLEX: Much of the effect of PTH is mediated by cAMP, which rises within minutes in osteocytes, osteoclasts and other target cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Calcium and phosphate distribution and regulation in ECF and plasma","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"PTH mobilizes bone mineral rat","C":"PTH mobilizes bone mineral rat","D":"PTH mobilizes bone mineral rat"}},{"id":"sdl37_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the process of regulation of Parathyroid hormone secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has been in skeletal traction for 6 weeks after a femoral shaft fracture. His serum calcium has risen to 11.0 mg/dL, and his PTH-related protein is undetectable. Which change in his parathyroid glands is expected during this period of immobilization?","choices":{"A":"Increased PTH secretion with chief cell hyperplasia, because PTH is needed to rebuild the unloaded bone","B":"Unchanged PTH secretion, because the calcium-sensing receptor responds to calcitriol rather than to calcium ions","C":"Increased PTH secretion, because unloaded osteocytes signal the chief cells directly to release hormone","D":"Decreased PTH secretion and gland activity, because the raised calcium activates the calcium-sensing receptor","E":"Conversion of chief cells into oxyphil cells that secrete calcitonin to bring the calcium back down"},"explanation":"Conditions that raise the extracellular calcium ion concentration decrease the activity and size of the parathyroid glands. They include excess calcium in the diet, increased vitamin D in the diet, and bone resorption caused by factors other than PTH, such as disuse of the bones. Extracellular calcium is detected by the calcium-sensing receptor, a G protein-coupled receptor that activates phospholipase C, increases inositol triphosphate and diacylglycerol, releases calcium from intracellular stores and thereby decreases PTH secretion. With calcium already high, PTH is suppressed rather than increased, and bone rebuilding does not drive PTH secretion. The calcium-sensing receptor responds to calcium ions; calcitriol suppresses PTH through a separate vitamin D receptor mechanism. Oxyphil cells are thought to be depleted chief cells, and calcitonin comes from thyroid C cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High ionized calcium activates the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor (Gq, phospholipase C, IP3 and DAG) and suppresses PTH; if sustained, the glands become smaller and less active. Causes include excess dietary calcium, excess vitamin D and bone resorption not driven by PTH, such as disuse. COMLEX: Hypercalcemia with a suppressed PTH points to a cause outside the parathyroid glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism from autonomous PTH oversecretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"C":"With calcium already high, PTH"}},{"id":"sdl37_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the process of regulation of Parathyroid hormone secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with Crohn’s disease and extensive small bowel resection presents for evaluation of recurrent muscle cramps and bone pain. Laboratory studies: serum calcium 6.8 mg/dL, phosphate 4.9 mg/dL, 25-hydroxyvitamin D 7 ng/mL (severely low), intact PTH 510 pg/mL. Neck ultrasound reveals four symmetrically enlarged parathyroid glands. Bone scan shows diffusely increased uptake consistent with increased bone turnover. Which of the following best explains the bilateral parathyroid gland enlargement in this patient?","choices":{"D":"Chronic hypocalcemia from vitamin D deficiency and calcium malabsorption provides a sustained stimulus to PTH secretion; over weeks to months, this persistent low-calcium signal drives compensatory parathyroid gland hyperplasia, which can increase total gland mass up to five-fold through chief cell proliferation","A":"Hypomagnesemia from intestinal malabsorption in Crohn’s disease directly stimulates parathyroid cell mitosis by activating a magnesium-sensing receptor distinct from CaSR, causing bilateral gland enlargement independent of serum calcium or vitamin D status","B":"Low vitamin D activity directly stimulates parathyroid chief cell DNA replication through a nuclear vitamin D receptor pathway that, paradoxically, promotes rather than suppresses cell proliferation when 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D levels are severely deficient","C":"Chronic hyperphosphatemia from impaired renal clearance in Crohn’s disease (due to associated glomerulonephritis) directly binds to a parathyroid phosphate-sensing receptor and upregulates chief cell division independent of calcium or vitamin D levels","E":"Malabsorption of dietary fat in Crohn’s disease leads to accumulation of long-chain fatty acids in parathyroid chief cells, which trigger mitochondrial oxidative stress and compensatory cellular hypertrophy that mimics the appearance of gland enlargement on ultrasound imaging"},"explanation":"In this patient with Crohn’s disease and small bowel resection, severe vitamin D malabsorption leads to markedly reduced 25-OH-D levels and therefore reduced calcitriol production, decreasing intestinal calcium absorption. The resulting chronic hypocalcemia provides a continuous stimulus to parathyroid chief cells via CaSR (calcium below set point → reduced Gq/PLC/IP3 signaling → increased PTH secretion). If this stimulus is sustained over weeks, the parathyroid glands undergo compensatory hyperplasia (secondary hyperparathyroidism) that can increase gland mass up to five-fold. This is the physiological basis of secondary hyperparathyroidism seen in CKD, vitamin D deficiency, and malabsorption syndromes. The course explains gland hypertrophy by persistently decreased calcium concentration, not a magnesium-sensing receptor. Gland enlargement in vitamin D deficiency reflects persistent low calcium driving PTH and gland hypertrophy, as in rickets, not a vitamin D receptor pathway promoting proliferation. Steatorrhea causes vitamin D and calcium malabsorption, and the resulting persistent low calcium drives gland hypertrophy, not hyperphosphatemia from glomerulonephritis. Fat malabsorption matters because vitamin D is fat-soluble and calcium forms insoluble soaps with fat; the low calcium then drives gland hypertrophy, not fatty-acid oxidative stress.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary hyperparathyroidism (all 4 glands enlarged) vs. primary hyperparathyroidism (single adenoma, 1 gland enlarged). Causes of secondary: CKD, vitamin D deficiency, malabsorption. Treatment is directed at the underlying cause. COMLEX: Osteoporosis and osteomalacia can coexist in Crohn’s disease due to vitamin D malabsorption and steroid use; bone mineral density screening is important in these patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism from autonomous PTH oversecretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"The course explains gland hypertrophy by persistently decrea","B":"Gland enlargement in vitamin D deficiency reflects persisten","C":"Steatorrhea causes vitamin D and calcium malabsorption, and ","E":"Fat malabsorption matters because vitamin D is fat-soluble a"}},{"id":"sdl37_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the process of regulation of Parathyroid hormone secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer presents with nausea, polyuria, and confusion. Laboratory values: serum calcium 14.8 mg/dL (severely elevated), intact PTH <5 pg/mL (undetectable), PTH-related protein (PTHrP) 11.2 pmol/L (elevated, normal <2). Serum phosphate is 2.1 mg/dL. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the suppressed PTH in this patient despite the hypercalcemia being driven by malignancy rather than primary hyperparathyroidism?","choices":{"C":"The severely elevated extracellular ionized calcium from malignancy activates CaSR on parathyroid chief cells, raising intracellular calcium through the Gq/PLC/IP3 pathway and suppressing PTH secretory granule exocytosis through normal feedback regulation; elevated ionized calcium from any cause suppresses PTH regardless of the source of hypercalcemia","A":"PTHrP directly binds to parathyroid chief cell PTH1R receptors and competitively inhibits PTH synthesis, causing transcriptional suppression of the PTH gene through a ligand-receptor feedback loop that does not operate through extracellular calcium or the CaSR","B":"Metastatic bone lesions release osteocalcin into the circulation, and elevated osteocalcin activates CaSR on parathyroid cells with higher affinity than calcium itself, causing profound suppression of PTH exocytosis even when extracellular calcium is only modestly elevated","D":"PTHrP stimulates pituitary release of calcitonin in large quantities, and elevated circulating calcitonin directly inhibits parathyroid chief cell secretory granule exocytosis through a G protein–coupled calcitonin receptor expressed on parathyroid cells","E":"Hypercalcemia from bone metastases depletes the ECF phosphate pool through calcium-phosphate precipitation, and the resulting severe hypophosphatemia is the primary CaSR-independent trigger for PTH suppression in malignancy-associated hypercalcemia"},"explanation":"The CaSR on parathyroid chief cells responds to extracellular ionized calcium regardless of the source of hypercalcemia. When serum calcium rises markedly (here due to PTHrP-driven humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy), CaSR is strongly activated, suppressing PTH secretion through the Gq/PLC/IP3/intracellular Ca pathway. PTHrP itself cannot suppress PTH, as there are no functional PTH1R receptors on parathyroid cells that would trigger negative feedback. The net result is suppressed PTH in the setting of hypercalcemia — a key distinguishing feature of malignancy-associated hypercalcemia compared to primary hyperparathyroidism (where PTH is elevated despite hypercalcemia). PTH falls because the raised calcium acts on the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor; osteocalcin is not described as an agonist there. Calcitonin is secreted by thyroid parafollicular C cells, not the pituitary, and PTH falls here because high calcium acts on the calcium-sensing receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key diagnostic distinction — Primary hyperparathyroidism: ↑PTH + ↑Ca. Malignancy/HHM: ↓PTH + ↑Ca + ↑PTHrP. CaSR suppresses PTH from any hypercalcemic cause. First Aid: PTHrP is the most common cause of hypercalcemia in inpatients; PTH is the most common cause in outpatients. COMLEX: Palpable lymph nodes or known malignancy + hypercalcemia — check PTHrP before attributing to primary hyperparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism from autonomous PTH oversecretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"PTHrP itself cannot suppress P","B":"PTH falls because the raised calcium acts on the parathyroid","D":"Calcitonin is secreted by thyroid parafollicular C cells, no"}},{"id":"sdl37_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the physiological functions of calcitonin","batch":2,"stem":"In a physiology study, healthy adults receive a pharmacologic dose of calcitonin. Besides inhibiting osteoclastic bone resorption, the hormone acts on the kidney. Which renal effect of calcitonin is expected?","choices":{"B":"Stimulation of renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase, raising calcitriol and with it intestinal calcium absorption","A":"Increased calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubule, mirroring the renal action of PTH","C":"Reduced tubular calcium reabsorption, so more calcium is excreted, a minor effect opposite to that of PTH","D":"No action on the kidney, because calcitonin receptors are found exclusively on the osteoclasts in bone","E":"Increased renal magnesium and sodium reabsorption, which secondarily traps more calcium in the tubule"},"explanation":"The principal effects of calcitonin are to lower serum calcium (and phosphate) by actions on bone and kidney. In the kidney, calcitonin reduces calcium reabsorption, and it also reduces the reabsorption of phosphate, sodium, potassium and magnesium (Katzung). The course text (Guyton) describes calcitonin's effects on calcium handling in the kidney tubules and intestines as minor and opposite to those of PTH, which increases calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubule. The sources agree on calcium; for phosphate, Katzung's statement that calcitonin reduces phosphate reabsorption is the same direction as PTH, so Guyton's general phrase 'opposite to PTH' should not be applied to phosphate. Stimulation of 1-alpha-hydroxylase is an action of PTH, not calcitonin. Calcitonin reduces, rather than increases, magnesium and sodium reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Calcitonin (32 amino acids, thyroid C cells, released by high calcium) inhibits osteoclastic resorption and reduces renal calcium reabsorption; its role in adult calcium balance is minor. COMLEX: Calcitonin is a tumor marker for medullary thyroid carcinoma, and pentagastrin, like hypercalcemia, is a potent stimulus of calcitonin secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from calcitonin-secreting C cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"In the kidney, calcitonin redu"}},{"id":"sdl37_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the physiological functions of calcitonin","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with Paget disease of bone is prescribed calcitonin. His pharmacist explains that the therapeutic product is salmon calcitonin rather than human calcitonin. Which pharmacokinetic property makes salmon calcitonin the more attractive therapeutic agent?","choices":{"A":"Its half-life of 40 to 50 minutes is several times longer than the half-life of about 10 minutes for human calcitonin","B":"It is absorbed intact when swallowed, so it can be given as an oral tablet, whereas human calcitonin must be injected","C":"It lacks the disulfide bond between positions 1 and 7, and losing this bond protects it from enzymatic degradation","D":"It is excreted intact in the urine, avoiding the renal metabolism that inactivates human calcitonin in the kidney","E":"It also binds PTH receptors on osteoblasts as well as calcitonin receptors, which doubles its antiresorptive action"},"explanation":"Human calcitonin monomer has a half-life of about 10 minutes, whereas salmon calcitonin has a longer half-life of 40 to 50 minutes, which makes it more attractive as a therapeutic agent. Calcitonin is a 32-amino acid peptide, and a disulfide bond between positions 1 and 7 is essential for its biologic activity, so a molecule lacking it would not be more effective. Much of its clearance occurs by metabolism in the kidney, and little intact calcitonin appears in the urine. Salmon calcitonin is available for parenteral and nasal administration, not as an oral tablet. Calcitonin acts through calcitonin receptors to inhibit osteoclastic bone resorption; it does not act on PTH receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Calcitonin inhibits osteoclastic bone resorption and lowers serum calcium; salmon calcitonin (half-life 40 to 50 minutes vs about 10 minutes for human) is given by injection or nasal spray. COMLEX: Calcitonin is used for Paget disease, hypercalcemia and osteoporosis, but it is less efficacious than bisphosphonates.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from calcitonin-secreting C cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl37_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the physiological functions of calcitonin","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer presents with confusion and a serum calcium of 14.6 mg/dL. She receives IV normal saline and subcutaneous salmon calcitonin every 12 hours. Her calcium falls to 12.9 mg/dL within 12 hours, but by day 3 it has drifted back toward 14 mg/dL although the calcitonin dose is unchanged. Which of the following best explains the waning effect of calcitonin?","choices":{"C":"Osteoclasts become refractory to calcitonin with continued exposure, so its rapid inhibition of bone resorption fades within days even though the dose is unchanged","A":"Neutralizing antibodies against salmon calcitonin form within 48 hours and bind the injected drug before it reaches bone, so the same dose no longer suppresses resorption","B":"Calcitonin lowers serum calcium mainly by increasing renal calcium excretion, and this effect stops once saline infusion has restored the glomerular filtration rate","D":"Calcitonin raises plasma calcitriol within 48 hours, and the resulting increase in intestinal calcium absorption offsets its inhibitory effect on bone resorption","E":"Calcitonin lowers calcium only in children with rapid bone turnover, so in adults its effect is limited to the first dose and is gone after about 24 hours"},"explanation":"Calcitonin binds calcitonin receptors on osteoclasts and inhibits bone resorption within minutes, and serum calcium begins to fall within 4–6 hours, which makes it a useful rapid bridge in severe hypercalcemia. With continued dosing, however, refractoriness (tachyphylaxis) frequently develops, so calcitonin by itself seldom restores serum calcium to normal and is combined with saline and a bisphosphonate. Katzung notes that this refractoriness is not correlated with antibody formation. Calcitonin's renal effect is a minor increase in calcium excretion, it does not raise calcitriol, and its acute anti-resorptive effect works in adults even though its physiologic role in adult calcium balance is small.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Calcitonin = rapid but transient calcium lowering (effect within hours, refractoriness within days); bisphosphonates act more slowly but last weeks. First Aid: calcitonin is used for acute hypercalcemia (transient effect) and Paget disease. COMLEX: Severe hypercalcemia of malignancy — IV saline first, calcitonin as a rapid bridge, and a bisphosphonate (e.g., zoledronic acid) for sustained control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from calcitonin-secreting C cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Katzung notes that this refrac","B":"Calcitonin's renal effect is a","D":"Calcitonin's renal effect is a","E":"Calcitonin's renal effect is a"}}],"38":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross/microscopic changes, complications and management of: Primary/Secondary/Tertiary hyperparathyroidism, Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, Hypoparathyroidism, Pseudohypoparathyroidism","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the most common underlying lesion in primary hyperparathyroidism and its defining morphologic characteristics on histopathology?","choices":{"B":"A solitary parathyroid adenoma, accounting for 85–95% of primary hyperparathyroidism, composed of sheets of uniform chief cells with minimal stromal fat and a rim of compressed normal tissue; the other glands are normal or shrunken from feedback inhibition by hypercalcemia","A":"Primary parathyroid hyperplasia, accounting for 85–95% of primary hyperparathyroidism cases, involving all four glands with symmetric chief cell enlargement; microscopically these glands preserve abundant stromal fat between lobules and show no rim of compressed nonneoplastic tissue at the gland periphery","C":"Parathyroid carcinoma, the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism, recognizable histologically by pleomorphic chief cells with high mitotic figures; cytologic atypia alone is the primary diagnostic criterion for malignancy and reliably distinguishes carcinoma from benign adenomas","D":"A sporadic parathyroid adenoma driven exclusively by MEN1 tumor suppressor gene mutations in all cases; biallelic loss of MEN1 on chromosome 17 is the required and sufficient molecular mechanism for adenoma formation regardless of familial or sporadic context","E":"Multiglandular chief cell hyperplasia involving all four glands with equal enlargement, accounting for 85–95% of primary hyperparathyroidism; microscopically the glands show abundant preserved stromal fat between enlarged chief cell nodules, and water-clear cell predominance is the most common histologic pattern"},"explanation":"Primary hyperparathyroidism results from autonomous PTH overproduction and is caused by a solitary parathyroid adenoma in 85–95% of cases. Adenomas are well-circumscribed, 0.5–5 g, and histologically show sheets of uniform polygonal chief cells with minimal adipose tissue; a characteristic rim of compressed nonneoplastic parathyroid tissue may be visible at the periphery. The uninvolved glands are shrunken because the hypercalcemia from the adenoma suppresses normal gland activity via CaSR feedback. Primary hyperplasia (5–10%) involves all four glands. Parathyroid carcinoma accounts for only ~1%; malignancy is diagnosed by invasive growth or metastasis, not cytology alone — cytologic atypia (\"endocrine atypia\") is not diagnostic of carcinoma. Only about 15% of sporadic parathyroid tumors carry somatic MEN1 mutations (MEN1 is on 11q13), and cyclin D1 overexpression is the other established defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 85–95% = solitary adenoma. 5–10% = hyperplasia (all 4 glands). <1% = carcinoma (only reliable criterion = invasion/metastasis). First Aid: parathyroid adenoma is monoclonal; CCND1 (cyclin D1) overexpression on chr 11 is a key molecular mechanism. MEN1 mutations found in ~15% of sporadic adenomas. COMLEX: Parathyroid adenoma localizes on sestamibi (Tc-99m) scan; single-gland vs. multiglandular disease determines surgical approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism is the most common manifestation of MEN-1, affecting 80–95% of patients; parathyroid adenomas and hyperplasia in MEN syndromes require genetic screening of family members","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Primary hyperplasia (5–10%) in","D":"Only about 15% of sporadic parathyroid tumors carry somatic ","E":"Primary hyperplasia (5–10%) in"}},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross/microscopic changes, complications and management of: Primary/Secondary/Tertiary hyperparathyroidism, Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, Hypoparathyroidism, Pseudohypoparathyroidism","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes secondary hyperparathyroidism from tertiary hyperparathyroidism in terms of underlying mechanism and serum calcium status?","choices":{"A":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is compensatory PTH hypersecretion in response to chronic hypocalcemia (usually from renal failure or vitamin D deficiency), with normal or low serum calcium; tertiary hyperparathyroidism is autonomous PTH secretion after prolonged secondary stimulation, causing hypercalcemia that persists after the original stimulus is corrected","B":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism results from a single autonomously secreting parathyroid adenoma causing hypercalcemia, with the term \"secondary\" indicating that it developed after a prior episode of primary hyperparathyroidism; tertiary hyperparathyroidism occurs when all four glands develop synchronous adenomas as a complication of surgically treated primary hyperparathyroidism","C":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism refers to hyperparathyroidism in which PTH is elevated but serum calcium remains normal, caused by a PTH-secreting parathyroid carcinoma that metastasizes to the liver and lungs; tertiary hyperparathyroidism describes diffuse parathyroid hyperplasia from dietary calcium deficiency in otherwise healthy adults","D":"Secondary and tertiary hyperparathyroidism are distinguished solely by serum PTH level rather than by mechanism or calcium status; secondary hyperparathyroidism is defined by PTH 65–200 pg/mL while tertiary hyperparathyroidism is defined by PTH >200 pg/mL regardless of whether the parathyroid glands show compensatory or autonomous activity","E":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is defined by familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia caused by heterozygous loss-of-function CASR mutations resulting in elevated PTH with mildly elevated serum calcium; tertiary hyperparathyroidism develops when these patients develop renal failure and their already-abnormal parathyroid glands undergo additional compensatory hyperplasia"},"explanation":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism = compensatory, reactive PTH excess driven by chronic hypocalcemia (most commonly renal failure or vitamin D deficiency). Serum calcium is low or low-normal. In CKD: decreased phosphate excretion → hyperphosphatemia → suppresses calcium → stimulates PTH; also decreased 1-alpha-hydroxylase → less calcitriol → less intestinal calcium absorption → further PTH stimulation. All four parathyroid glands enlarge (hyperplasia). Tertiary hyperparathyroidism occurs when these chronically stimulated glands eventually develop autonomous secretory capacity (like a secondary adenoma within hyperplastic tissue), resulting in hypercalcemia even after the original stimulus is removed (e.g., after renal transplant). Treatment is parathyroidectomy. Secondary hyperparathyroidism is a compensatory response to chronic hypocalcemia, not a single autonomous adenoma causing hypercalcemia. Secondary hyperparathyroidism is compensatory PTH hypersecretion from chronic hypocalcemia, usually renal failure or vitamin D deficiency, not a metastasizing PTH-secreting carcinoma. The two are distinguished by mechanism and calcium status (compensatory with low calcium versus autonomous with hypercalcemia), not by PTH level alone. Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia is a separate disorder of heterozygous loss-of-function CASR mutations; secondary hyperparathyroidism is compensatory PTH excess from chronic hypocalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary (1°) HPT: high PTH + high Ca, single adenoma usually. Secondary (2°) HPT: high PTH + low/normal Ca, CKD or Vit D deficiency, all 4 glands enlarged. Tertiary (3°) HPT: high PTH + high Ca after chronic 2° HPT — autonomous. First Aid: calciphylaxis (vascular calcification causing skin necrosis) is a feared complication of secondary HPT in dialysis patients. COMLEX: Renal osteodystrophy in CKD — osteitis fibrosa cystica, osteomalacia, mixed; treat underlying 2° HPT with phosphate binders, calcitriol, calcimimetics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism is the most common manifestation of MEN-1, affecting 80–95% of patients; parathyroid adenomas and hyperplasia in MEN syndromes require genetic screening of family members","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is a compensatory response to ","C":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is compensatory PTH hypersecre","D":"The two are distinguished by mechanism and calcium status (c","E":"Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia is a separate disorder "}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross/microscopic changes, complications and management of: Primary/Secondary/Tertiary hyperparathyroidism, Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, Hypoparathyroidism, Pseudohypoparathyroidism","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the hallmark neuromuscular manifestation of hypoparathyroidism, its underlying mechanism, and the two classic bedside signs used to detect subclinical disease?","choices":{"C":"Tetany is the hallmark manifestation, resulting from hypocalcemia-induced reduction in the threshold for nerve depolarization, causing neuromuscular hyperexcitability ranging from circumoral paresthesias and carpopedal spasm to laryngospasm and seizures; Chvostek sign (facial muscle contraction on tapping the facial nerve) and Trousseau sign (carpal spasm on blood pressure cuff inflation) detect subclinical latent tetany","A":"Nephrolithiasis is the hallmark manifestation of hypoparathyroidism, resulting from obligatory calcium phosphate crystal precipitation in the renal tubules; Chvostek sign (tap the femoral nerve to elicit hip flexion) and Trousseau sign (elicited by hyperextending the wrist against resistance) are the two bedside signs used to confirm renal calcium deposition","B":"Prolonged QT interval with ventricular tachycardia is the hallmark manifestation of hypoparathyroidism and the primary indication for emergency treatment; Chvostek sign (tap the neck to elicit laryngospasm) and Trousseau sign (produce tetanic arm flexion by applying pressure to the biceps tendon) identify life-threatening cardiac risk","D":"Psychosis with basal ganglia calcification is the hallmark manifestation of hypoparathyroidism; Chvostek sign (periorbital twitching after tapping over the supraorbital nerve) and Trousseau sign (inflation of a cuff over the forearm producing finger extension) are bedside tests for latent neuropsychiatric complications of chronic hypocalcemia","E":"Brown tumors with bone pain are the hallmark manifestations of hypoparathyroidism, resulting from upregulated osteoclastic activity caused by the absence of PTH; Chvostek sign (contraction of the orbicularis oculi after tapping the facial nerve) and Trousseau sign (extension of the toes on plantar stimulation) are used to confirm skeletal involvement"},"explanation":"Tetany is the hallmark of hypoparathyroidism and is caused by hypocalcemia, which lowers the threshold for nerve membrane depolarization (calcium stabilizes the resting potential). Symptoms range from mild circumoral numbness and paresthesias to carpopedal spasm, laryngospasm, and generalized seizures. Chvostek sign: tapping the facial nerve anterior to the ear elicits contraction of the ipsilateral facial muscles (mouth, nose, eyelid) — indicative of latent tetany. Trousseau sign: inflating a BP cuff above systolic for several minutes produces carpal spasm (thumb adduction, finger extension) due to ischemia-induced tetany of the forearm muscles. Additional complications include prolonged QT interval (arrhythmia risk), basal ganglia calcifications, cataracts, and dental abnormalities if hypocalcemia occurs during development. The hallmark of hypoparathyroidism is tetany from hypocalcemia, not nephrolithiasis, and Chvostek sign is tapping the facial nerve, not the femoral nerve. Brown tumors are a skeletal lesion of hyperparathyroidism; the hallmark of hypoparathyroidism is tetany from low blood calcium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoparathyroidism = hypocalcemia + hyperphosphatemia + LOW PTH. Causes: post-thyroidectomy (most common acquired), DiGeorge (22q11 deletion), APS-1 (AIRE mutations), autosomal dominant gain-of-function CASR mutations. ECG finding: prolonged QT interval. First Aid: distinguish from pseudohypoparathyroidism (high PTH with end-organ resistance). COMLEX: Post-thyroidectomy hypoparathyroidism is the highest-risk surgical complication for calcium homeostasis; OMM somatic dysfunction at the thyroid-region reflex levels (C4–C6 and T2 with sympathetic supply T1–T4, OMM atlas Ch 15; head and neck T1–T5, OMM course map) may accompany neck endocrine pathology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — Primary hyperparathyroidism is the most common manifestation of MEN-1, affecting 80–95% of patients; parathyroid adenomas and hyperplasia in MEN syndromes require genetic screening of family members","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The hallmark of hypoparathyroidism is tetany from hypocalcem","B":"Additional complications inclu","D":"Additional complications inclu","E":"Brown tumors are a skeletal lesion of hyperparathyroidism; t"}},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross/microscopic changes, complications and management of: Primary/Secondary/Tertiary hyperparathyroidism, Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, Hypoparathyroidism, Pseudohypoparathyroidism","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman is found to have serum calcium of 11.6 mg/dL on a routine metabolic panel obtained during a wellness visit. She denies bone pain but reports mild fatigue, constipation, and occasional flank discomfort. Vital signs are normal. Physical examination is unremarkable. Laboratory studies: intact PTH 195 pg/mL (elevated), calcium 11.6 mg/dL, phosphate 1.9 mg/dL, 24-hour urine calcium 410 mg/day (elevated), creatinine normal. Technetium-99m sestamibi scan localizes a focus of increased uptake to the left inferior parathyroid pole. Which of the following best describes the pathological diagnosis, the molecular event most commonly driving this lesion, and the classic clinical syndrome associated with severe untreated disease?","choices":{"D":"Parathyroid adenoma is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism; the most common molecular mechanism is Cyclin D1 (CCND1) overexpression, classically via a pericentromeric chromosome 11 inversion that juxtaposes the PTH gene promoter to CCND1; severe untreated disease classically presents with \"painful bones, renal stones, abdominal groans, and psychic moans\" from hypercalcemia","A":"Primary parathyroid hyperplasia is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism, with the most common molecular mechanism being biallelic MEN2A RET proto-oncogene activation on chromosome 10 that drives chief cell proliferation across all four glands; severe untreated disease presents with Trousseau sign, laryngospasm, and prolonged QT interval reflecting PTH-driven hypocalcemia","B":"Parathyroid carcinoma is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism; the most common molecular mechanism is BRAF V600E point mutation driving chief cell neoplastic transformation; diagnosis is confirmed by cytologic atypia and macronucleoli on fine needle aspiration, and severe disease presents with hypocalcemia and tetany due to excessive calcitonin co-secretion","C":"Parathyroid adenoma is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism; the most common molecular mechanism is gain-of-function mutation in the calcium-sensing receptor (CASR) gene that renders chief cells constitutively active regardless of ECF calcium concentration; severe untreated disease presents with hyperphosphatemia, bradycardia, and pathological humeral fractures due to PTH-independent bone resorption","E":"Primary parathyroid hyperplasia is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism, driven by somatic TP53 mutations causing loss of apoptotic regulation across all four glands; severe untreated disease presents with the clinical triad of hypercalcemia, elevated alkaline phosphatase, and symmetric bilateral knee effusions due to calcium pyrophosphate deposition in joint spaces"},"explanation":"Solitary parathyroid adenoma causes 85–95% of primary hyperparathyroidism. The most common molecular defect is cyclin D1 overexpression (up to 40% of adenomas, per Robbins), classically from a pericentromeric inversion on chromosome 11 that repositions the CCND1 gene (normally 11q) adjacent to the PTH gene promoter region (11p), causing cyclin D1 overexpression and unregulated chief cell proliferation. MEN1 mutations account for ~15% of sporadic adenomas. Classic symptomatic primary hyperparathyroidism is remembered as \"Stones, Bones, Groans, and Psychic Moans\": nephrolithiasis (from hypercalciuria), bone pain and osteitis fibrosa cystica/brown tumors (from PTH-driven bone resorption), GI symptoms (constipation, nausea, pancreatitis, peptic ulcers), and neuropsychiatric effects (depression, lethargy, confusion). As many as 80% of patients are now identified in the outpatient setting when hypercalcemia is found incidentally (Robbins Ch 24). Solitary adenoma, not primary hyperplasia, causes 85 to 95% of primary hyperparathyroidism, and the common molecular defect is cyclin D1 overexpression, not RET activation. Parathyroid carcinoma causes only about 1% of primary hyperparathyroidism, and cytologic detail is unreliable for its diagnosis; invasion and metastasis are the only reliable criteria. Gain-of-function CASR mutations suppress PTH and cause autosomal dominant hypoparathyroidism; adenomas are driven by cyclin D1 overexpression or MEN1 mutation. Adenoma (85–95%), not primary hyperplasia (5–10%), is the commonest cause, and its established molecular defects are cyclin D1 overexpression and MEN1 mutation, not TP53.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Labs in primary HPT: ↑Ca, ↓PO4, ↑PTH, ↑urine Ca, ↑urine cAMP, ↑1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Cyclin D1 overexpression (classically via the chr 11 CCND1 inversion) is the most common molecular defect; somatic MEN1 mutations are found in ~15%. Sestamibi scan localizes adenoma preoperatively. Asymptomatic primary HPT in older patients may be observed, while symptomatic disease is treated by parathyroidectomy. COMLEX: Calciphylaxis is a feared complication of secondary but also tertiary HPT in CKD; skin biopsy shows vascular calcification with ischemic necrosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — Regulation of PTH secretion: calcium-sensing receptor, CaSR signaling, and compensatory parathyroid gland hypertrophy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Solitary adenoma, not primary hyperplasia, causes 85 to 95% ","B":"Parathyroid carcinoma causes only about 1% of primary hyperp","C":"Gain-of-function CASR mutations suppress PTH and cause autos","E":"Adenoma (85–95%), not primary hyperplasia (5–10%), is the co"}},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross/microscopic changes, complications and management of: Primary/Secondary/Tertiary hyperparathyroidism, Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, Hypoparathyroidism, Pseudohypoparathyroidism","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with a 40-pack-year smoking history and a 40-lb weight loss over 6 months presents with confusion, constipation, and polyuria. He was recently diagnosed with a right upper lobe lung mass on chest X-ray; biopsy is pending. Laboratory studies: serum calcium 14.2 mg/dL, intact PTH <5 pg/mL (undetectable), PTH-related protein (PTHrP) 14 pmol/L (elevated, normal <2), phosphate 2.0 mg/dL, creatinine 1.4 mg/dL. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of this patient’s hypercalcemia, identifies the most likely cell type of origin of the lung mass, and explains why PTH is suppressed despite the severe hypercalcemia?","choices":{"A":"Humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy from PTHrP secreted by a squamous cell carcinoma of the lung; PTHrP binds PTH1R on bone and kidney with the same effects as PTH (osteoclast activation, reduced renal phosphate reabsorption), causing hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia, and PTH is suppressed because the high ionized calcium activates CaSR on chief cells through normal feedback","B":"Primary hyperparathyroidism from a parathyroid adenoma coincidentally discovered in a patient with a benign pulmonary hamartoma is the most likely cause; elevated ionized calcium from primary hyperparathyroidism paradoxically stimulates rather than suppresses PTH secretion through a positive feedback loop involving PTHrP cross-reactivity at the CaSR; the lung mass biopsy will show a carcinoid tumor co-secreting PTHrP and PTH","C":"Osteolytic metastases to bone causing calcium release are the most likely mechanism; the lung mass is most likely small cell carcinoma, which preferentially metastasizes to the axial skeleton; PTH is undetectable because small cell carcinoma directly destroys parathyroid gland tissue through a paraneoplastic autoimmune mechanism targeting parathyroid chief cells","D":"Vitamin D toxicity from sarcoidosis-associated granulomatous 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity is the most likely cause; the lung mass represents pulmonary sarcoidosis; PTH is suppressed because elevated calcitriol raises ECF calcium through intestinal absorption, which suppresses PTH via CaSR; PTHrP elevation in this patient reflects non-specific tumor marker elevation from activated pulmonary macrophages","E":"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism with autonomous PTH secretion in the setting of underlying CKD is the most likely cause; PTHrP elevation is a false-positive result from cross-reactivity of the elevated PTH on the PTHrP immunoassay; PTH appears suppressed on this report due to an assay interference, and a repeat PTH using a different immunoassay platform would return markedly elevated results"},"explanation":"Malignancy is the most common cause of symptomatic hypercalcemia in hospitalized patients (vs. primary hyperparathyroidism being more common in outpatients). ~80% of malignancy-associated hypercalcemia is caused by PTHrP (humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy, HHM), most commonly from squamous cell carcinomas (lung, head/neck, esophagus, cervix) and renal cell carcinoma. PTHrP shares the N-terminal domain homology with PTH and binds PTH1R on bone osteoblasts (inducing RANKL/osteoclast activation) and renal tubules (causing phosphaturia), mimicking PTH action. The resulting hypercalcemia suppresses parathyroid PTH secretion through normal CaSR-mediated feedback (elevated ionized Ca → Gq/PLC/IP3 → inhibits PTH exocytosis). Key lab distinction: primary HPT = ↑PTH + ↑Ca; HHM = ↓PTH + ↑PTHrP + ↑Ca. With PTH undetectable and PTHrP high, this is not primary hyperparathyroidism, in which PTH is elevated or inappropriately normal. The high PTHrP points to PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia (about 80% of malignancy cases) rather than bone metastases with cytokine-induced resorption. Granulomatous disease lowers PTH but would not raise PTHrP; a high PTHrP points to PTHrP-secreting malignancy. Tertiary hyperparathyroidism has increased PTH, and PTH- and PTHrP-specific immunoassays are what distinguish hyperparathyroidism from malignancy-associated hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypercalcemia causes mnemonic \"CHIMPANZEES\" or \"VITAMINS D\": primary HPT most common outpatient, malignancy most common inpatient. HHM: ↑PTHrP, ↓PTH, squamous cell carcinoma (lung most common). Osteolytic mets (breast, MM): ↓PTHrP, ↓PTH, cytokine-mediated. First Aid: multiple myeloma produces osteoclast-activating factors — PTH and PTHrP both low. COMLEX: Treating hypercalcemia of malignancy — IV fluids + loop diuretics (furosemide) acutely; bisphosphonates for sustained control; calcitonin for rapid temporary effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — Regulation of PTH secretion: calcium-sensing receptor, CaSR signaling, and compensatory parathyroid gland hypertrophy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"With PTH undetectable and PTHrP high, this is not primary hy","C":"The high PTHrP points to PTHrP-mediated hypercalcemia (about","D":"Granulomatous disease lowers PTH but would not raise PTHrP; ","E":"Tertiary hyperparathyroidism has increased PTH, and PTH- and"}},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply knowledge of clinical presentations, etiologies, pathogenesis, gross/microscopic changes, complications and management of: Primary/Secondary/Tertiary hyperparathyroidism, Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, Hypoparathyroidism, Pseudohypoparathyroidism","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl is evaluated for short stature, round facies, and shortened 4th and 5th metacarpals noted on hand X-ray. Her mother has the same physical features. Laboratory studies: serum calcium 6.8 mg/dL, phosphate 6.1 mg/dL (elevated), intact PTH 420 pg/mL (markedly elevated). She denies recent neck surgery or radiation. TSH is mildly elevated at 5.8 mIU/L (normal 0.45–4.12). Urinary cAMP does not rise after exogenous PTH infusion. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology, the responsible mutation, and the reason TSH is also mildly elevated in this patient?","choices":{"E":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism results from end-organ resistance to PTH due to inactivating GNAS mutations (Gs-alpha); because PTH, TSH, and FSH/LH all signal through Gs-coupled receptors, the response to all three is impaired; PTH resistance causes hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia with elevated PTH, TSH resistance causes mild hypothyroidism, and the short stature, round face, and short 4th/5th metacarpals are Albright hereditary osteodystrophy","A":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism results from end-organ overexpression of the calcium-sensing receptor (CASR), which constitutively suppresses PTH signaling in bone and kidney through excessive CaSR-mediated intracellular calcium release; because CASR is also expressed on TSH receptor-bearing thyrocytes, CASR overactivation simultaneously suppresses TSH signaling; the physical phenotype reflects CASR-driven growth plate premature fusion in long bones","B":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism results from autoimmune destruction of PTH receptors in bone and kidney by anti-PTH1R antibodies produced in the context of APS-1 associated with AIRE gene mutations; TSH elevation reflects concurrent autoimmune Hashimoto thyroiditis that develops as part of the same polyglandular autoimmune syndrome; PTH levels are elevated because the antibodies cross-react with the parathyroid gland CaSR and paradoxically stimulate PTH secretion","C":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism results from a gain-of-function mutation in the PTH gene itself, producing a structurally abnormal PTH peptide that binds PTH1R but fails to activate the receptor due to a defective N-terminal receptor-binding domain; TSH elevation reflects cross-reactivity of the abnormal PTH molecule with the TSH receptor on thyrocytes; the physical phenotype results from the abnormal PTH stimulating growth plate chondrocyte differentiation through PTHrP receptor signaling","D":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism results from biallelic deletion of the PTH1R gene on chromosome 3p encoding the PTH receptor; because PTH1R is also expressed in renal tubular cells and chondrocytes, its absence causes hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and growth plate dysplasia; TSH elevation reflects secondary thyroid suppression due to elevated PTH competing with TSH for the common beta-subunit of glycoprotein hormone receptors"},"explanation":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PHP) is defined by hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and markedly elevated PTH in the setting of end-organ PTH resistance — the parathyroid glands work normally and secrete PTH appropriately, but target organs (bone, kidney) cannot respond to it. PHP type 1a is caused by inactivating mutations in GNAS (Gsα subunit). Because PTH, TSH, and LH/FSH all couple to Gs-alpha to activate adenylyl cyclase, Gs-alpha deficiency impairs signaling of all three, producing: (1) PTH resistance → hypocalcemia + hyperphosphatemia + high PTH; (2) TSH resistance → mild hypothyroidism; (3) FSH/LH resistance → hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. The phenotype (Albright hereditary osteodystrophy, AHO): short stature, obesity, round facies, brachydactyly (shortened 4th/5th metacarpals), and intellectual disability. The failure of urinary cAMP to rise after PTH infusion confirms end-organ resistance. Pseudohypoparathyroidism is end-organ resistance from defects in shared G-protein signaling; CASR gain-of-function instead suppresses PTH secretion and lowers PTH. APS-1 from AIRE mutation causes autoimmune hypoparathyroidism with candidiasis and adrenal insufficiency, whereas this patient has high PTH from end-organ resistance. PTH gene mutations cause familial isolated hypoparathyroidism by impairing processing to active hormone, whereas this picture is end-organ resistance shared with TSH through G-protein signaling. Loss of PTH1R would not explain TSH resistance as well; combined PTH and TSH resistance reflects a defect in the G-protein pathway those receptors share.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PHP: ↑PTH + ↓Ca + ↑PO4 + GNAS mutation + AHO phenotype. Distinguish: hypoparathyroidism (↓PTH + ↓Ca + ↑PO4) vs. PHP (↑PTH + ↓Ca + ↑PO4). No rise in urinary cAMP after exogenous PTH = diagnostic of PHP. Albright hereditary osteodystrophy = short stature, obesity, round facies, shortened 4th/5th digits; the same phenotype with normal calcium and PTH (paternal GNAS mutation) is pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism. COMLEX: PHP patients have shortened 4th metacarpal — palpable on musculoskeletal exam; when patient makes a fist, the knuckle of the 4th ray is sunken.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 3 — Regulation of PTH secretion: calcium-sensing receptor, CaSR signaling, and compensatory parathyroid gland hypertrophy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism is end-organ resistance from defect","B":"APS-1 from AIRE mutation causes autoimmune hypoparathyroidis","C":"PTH gene mutations cause familial isolated hypoparathyroidis","D":"Loss of PTH1R would not explain TSH resistance as well; comb"}}],"39":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe physiology of male sexual organs including Leydig and Sertoli cell functions","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell type in the testis is primarily responsible for testosterone secretion?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cells, in the interstitium between seminiferous tubules, secrete testosterone in response to luteinizing hormone from the anterior pituitary","B":"Sertoli cells, located in the seminiferous tubule wall, secrete testosterone in response to follicle-stimulating hormone from the anterior pituitary gland","C":"Spermatogonia, the germinal stem cells at the base of the seminiferous tubule, produce testosterone during mitotic division at the onset of puberty","D":"Myoid cells, which surround the seminiferous tubule in a peritubular layer, synthesize testosterone from circulating low-density lipoprotein cholesterol precursors","E":"Chief cells clustered in the hilum of the testis secrete testosterone under the influence of human chorionic gonadotropin from the placenta during fetal development"},"explanation":"Leydig cells (interstitial cells) are located in the spaces between seminiferous tubules and comprise approximately 20% of adult testicular mass. They are stimulated by LH to synthesize and secrete testosterone. Sertoli cells are inside the seminiferous tubule and support spermatogenesis; they do not secrete testosterone directly. Spermatogonia are germ cell precursors. Myoid cells are contractile peritubular cells. Chief cells are parathyroid cells, not testicular.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leydig cells (interstitial) = testosterone via LH; Sertoli cells = spermatogenesis support via FSH. This LH→Leydig→testosterone vs FSH→Sertoli→spermatogenesis dichotomy is a classic board pairing. COMLEX: Androgen deficiency from Leydig cell dysfunction causes loss of libido, muscle mass, and bone density — CP3 male reproductive physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 1 — Monthly ovarian cycle includes granulosa and theca cells with functions analogous to Sertoli and Leydig cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Spermatogonia are germ cell pr","D":"Myoid cells are contractile pe","E":"Chief cells are parathyroid ce"}},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe physiology of male sexual organs including Leydig and Sertoli cell functions","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary functions of Sertoli cells in male reproductive physiology?","choices":{"C":"Sertoli cells are stimulated by LH to synthesize testosterone from cholesterol, providing intratubular androgen to drive spermatid maturation within the tubule lumen","A":"Sertoli cells, stimulated by FSH, convert testosterone to estradiol, secrete nutrient fluid supporting sperm maturation, and produce inhibin to regulate FSH secretion","B":"Sertoli cells form the outer fibromuscular capsule of each seminiferous tubule and secrete inhibin only in the absence of adequate spermatogenesis as a compensatory signal","D":"Sertoli cells produce androgen-binding protein in response to testosterone alone, and their primary function is to transport androgens across the blood-testis barrier to maturing sperm","E":"Sertoli cells are stimulated by LH to undergo hypertrophy, and their principal endocrine output is activin, which upregulates FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary"},"explanation":"Sertoli cells are large cells lining the seminiferous tubules. FSH stimulates them to: (1) convert testosterone to estradiol via aromatase, (2) secrete nutrient fluid for sperm maturation, and (3) produce inhibin, which provides negative feedback specifically on FSH. Sertoli cells are NOT stimulated by LH; that is Leydig cells. They do produce androgen-binding protein, but this is secondary to their FSH-driven functions. Inhibin is produced during active spermatogenesis, not in its absence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSH→Sertoli cells→inhibin (negative feedback on FSH specifically), spermiogenesis support, estradiol conversion. Classic board trap: inhibin inhibits FSH selectively, while testosterone inhibits both LH and FSH. COMLEX: FSH elevation in azoospermia reflects absent inhibin from dysfunctional Sertoli cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 1 — Monthly ovarian cycle includes granulosa and theca cells with functions analogous to Sertoli and Leydig cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Inhibin is produced during act","C":"Sertoli cells are NOT stimulat","E":"Sertoli cells are NOT stimulat"}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe physiology of male sexual organs including Leydig and Sertoli cell functions","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the relative contributions of male accessory glands to total semen volume?","choices":{"E":"The seminal vesicles contribute approximately 60% of semen volume, the prostate gland contributes approximately 30%, and the vas deferens contributes approximately 10%","A":"The prostate gland contributes approximately 60% of semen volume, the seminal vesicles contribute approximately 30%, and the vas deferens contributes approximately 10%","B":"The bulbourethral glands contribute approximately 60% of semen volume, the prostate contributes approximately 30%, and the seminal vesicles contribute approximately 10%","C":"The seminal vesicles and prostate contribute equally, each accounting for approximately 40% of semen volume, with the vas deferens contributing the remaining 20%","D":"The vas deferens contributes approximately 60% of semen volume, the seminal vesicles contribute approximately 30%, and the prostate gland contributes approximately 10%"},"explanation":"Semen composition by volume: seminal vesicles ~60% (fructose, citric acid, prostaglandins, fibrinogen), prostate ~30% (thin milky alkaline fluid with calcium, citrate, clotting enzyme, profibrinolysin), vas deferens ~10% (the sperm-containing fraction). Bulbourethral glands contribute mucus for lubrication but are not a significant volume contributor. Knowing these proportions is important because seminal vesicle dysfunction can abolish fructose in semen (azoospermia workup). The seminal vesicles contribute about 60% and the prostate about 30%, so these two are swapped. The seminal vesicles contribute about 60%, the prostate about 30% and the vas deferens about 10%; they are not equal at 40% each. The seminal vesicles contribute about 60% of semen volume, and the vas deferens only about 10%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Seminal vesicles provide fructose (energy for sperm), prostaglandins, and fibrinogen. Prostate provides alkaline secretion (neutralizes vaginal acidity). Absent seminal vesicles (as in CFTR mutations) → azoospermia without fructose. COMLEX: Semen alkalinity protects sperm viability in the acidic vaginal environment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 1 — Monthly ovarian cycle includes granulosa and theca cells with functions analogous to Sertoli and Leydig cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The seminal vesicles contribute about 60% and the prostate a","C":"The seminal vesicles contribute about 60%, the prostate abou","D":"The seminal vesicles contribute about 60% of semen volume, a"}},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Outline steps of spermatogenesis, sperm maturation, capacitation, and composition of semen","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following lists the stages of spermatogenesis in the correct sequential order from most primitive to most mature?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogonium, then primary spermatocyte, then secondary spermatocyte, then spermatid, then spermatozoon","B":"Primary spermatocyte, then spermatogonium, then secondary spermatocyte, then spermatozoon, then spermatid","C":"Spermatogonium, then spermatid, then primary spermatocyte, then secondary spermatocyte, then spermatozoon","D":"Spermatogonium, then secondary spermatocyte, then primary spermatocyte, then spermatid, then spermatozoon","E":"Primary spermatocyte, then spermatogonium, then spermatid, then secondary spermatocyte, then spermatozoon"},"explanation":"Spermatogenesis sequence: spermatogonia (mitosis) → primary spermatocytes (meiosis I) → two secondary spermatocytes (meiosis II) → four spermatids (differentiation/spermiogenesis) → spermatozoa, about 74 days in all (Guyton; First Aid gives about 2 months). One primary spermatocyte ultimately yields four spermatozoa via two meiotic divisions. Primary spermatocyte is the longest-lived stage. The sequence begins with the spermatogonium, then primary spermatocyte, not the reverse, and ends with the spermatozoon after the spermatid. The spermatid forms from the secondary spermatocyte, after the primary and secondary spermatocytes, not before them. The primary spermatocyte comes before the secondary spermatocyte, since meiosis I precedes meiosis II. The sequence begins with the spermatogonium and places the spermatid after the secondary spermatocyte.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary spermatocytes are diploid (2N, 4C); meiosis I yields haploid secondary spermatocytes with duplicated chromatids (1N, 2C); meiosis II yields spermatids (1N, 1C). The 74-day timeline explains why chemotherapy effects on fertility emerge 2-3 months after gonadotoxin exposure. COMLEX: Cryptorchidism causes tubular epithelium degeneration from heat — spermatogonia are most heat-sensitive.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — LH and FSH regulate oogenesis and ovulation in the female in parallel to their roles in spermatogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The sequence begins with the spermatogonium, then primary sp","C":"The spermatid forms from the secondary spermatocyte, after t","D":"The primary spermatocyte comes before the secondary spermato","E":"The sequence begins with the spermatogonium and places the s"}},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Outline steps of spermatogenesis, sperm maturation, capacitation, and composition of semen","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man and his partner present for infertility evaluation after 14 months of unprotected intercourse. Semen analysis is normal, but in vitro his sperm fail to fertilize oocytes unless they are first pre-incubated in a capacitating medium. The couple is told that full fertilizing capacity requires a process occurring after ejaculation that was not completed. Which of the following best describes this process?","choices":{"D":"Maturation in the epididymis over 18 to 24 hours, during which sperm acquire the capability to move but remain inhibited by epididymal secretions until they are removed at ejaculation","A":"Capacitation, in which inhibitory cholesterol factors are washed away over 1 to 10 hours in the female tract and calcium influx enables the powerful flagellar motion needed to fertilize","B":"Acrosome reaction, in which the acrosomal membrane fuses with the oocyte plasma membrane and releases hyaluronidase to dissolve the zona pellucida immediately upon ejaculation","C":"Sperm methylation in the female genital tract, in which epigenetic modifications to sperm DNA over 4 to 6 hours are required for normal paternal imprinting in the developing embryo","E":"Osmotic activation, in which the alkaline environment of the female genital tract dissolves the cholesterol glycocalyx and activates mitochondrial ATP synthase for enhanced forward motility"},"explanation":"Capacitation is a 1-10 hour process occurring in the female genital tract where inhibitory cholesterol-containing factors coating the sperm membrane are washed away. This allows calcium influx into the sperm, converting the normal flagellar beat into a powerful whiplash motion needed to penetrate the egg. Epididymal maturation occurs before ejaculation and confers baseline motility capability, not the hyperactivated motility of capacitation. The acrosome reaction is a separate downstream event triggered by zona pellucida contact. Capacitation is the washing away of inhibitory factors and loss of excess membrane cholesterol with calcium entry, not sperm DNA methylation. Capacitation involves washing away inhibitory factors, loss of excess cholesterol and calcium entry that gives a whiplash flagellar beat, not activation of mitochondrial ATP synthase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Capacitation = removal of inhibitory factors in female tract → calcium influx → hyperactivated motility. Acrosome reaction follows capacitation. COMLEX: A capacitation defect causes infertility despite a normal semen analysis, because capacitation occurs after ejaculation in the female tract and is not measured on a routine semen sample.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — LH and FSH regulate oogenesis and ovulation in the female in parallel to their roles in spermatogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Capacitation is the washing away of inhibitory factors and l","E":"Capacitation involves washing away inhibitory factors, loss "}},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Outline steps of spermatogenesis, sperm maturation, capacitation, and composition of semen","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the sperm count threshold for male infertility and the temperature requirements for spermatogenesis?","choices":{"C":"A sperm count below 20 million per milliliter is associated with infertility, and normal spermatogenesis requires testicular temperature maintained slightly below core body temperature","A":"A sperm count below 50 million per milliliter is considered pathologic, and scrotal temperature must be maintained at exactly core body temperature for normal spermatogenesis to proceed","B":"A sperm count below 40 million per milliliter is the standard threshold for infertility, and spermatogenesis requires a temperature at least 3 degrees above normal body temperature","D":"A sperm count below 10 million per milliliter is the threshold for infertility, and testicular temperature must equal core body temperature for normal tubular epithelium maintenance","E":"A sperm count below 5 million per milliliter defines infertility, and spermatogenesis can proceed normally at core body temperature if an adequate arterial blood supply is maintained"},"explanation":"A sperm count below 20 million/mL is associated with infertility (oligospermia). Normal spermatogenesis requires a temperature slightly below body temperature, which is maintained by the scrotal position and countercurrent heat exchange in the pampiniform plexus. Cryptorchidism (undescended testis) exposes the testis to abdominal temperature, causing tubular epithelial degeneration and infertility from heat. Varicoceles impair heat exchange and are a common cause of male factor infertility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Less than 20 million/mL = oligospermia = infertility threshold. Cryptorchidism → heat damage → infertility + increased testicular cancer risk. Varicocele = dilated pampiniform plexus → elevated scrotal temp → impaired spermatogenesis. COMLEX: Cryptorchidism managed with orchiopexy before age 2 to preserve fertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 2 — LH and FSH regulate oogenesis and ovulation in the female in parallel to their roles in spermatogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Normal spermatogenesis require","B":"Normal spermatogenesis require","E":"Normal spermatogenesis require"}},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe stages in the male sexual act","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the autonomic neural pathway responsible for penile erection?","choices":{"D":"Parasympathetic fibers from S2 to S4 release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, which increase cGMP in corporal smooth muscle to cause relaxation and vasodilation","A":"Sympathetic fibers from T12 to L2 release norepinephrine, which activates alpha-1 receptors on corporal smooth muscle to cause vasodilation and blood pooling in the cavernosa","B":"Somatic fibers in the pudendal nerve from S2 to S4 release acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors in the corpora cavernosa, causing direct smooth muscle contraction and rigidity","C":"Sympathetic fibers from T1 to T4 release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, which increase cGMP in corporal smooth muscle and cause relaxation and vasodilation","E":"Sensory fibers in the ilioinguinal nerve from L1 release substance P in the dorsal horn, triggering a spinal reflex that causes bilateral dilation of the pudendal arteries"},"explanation":"Erection is mediated by parasympathetic fibers from S2–S4 (pelvic nerves). These release nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP), which activate guanylyl cyclase to produce cGMP. Elevated cGMP relaxes smooth muscle of the corpora cavernosa and spongiosum, allowing vasodilation and engorgement. Sympathetic T12–L2 fibers mediate emission and detumescence. The somatic pudendal nerve carries sensation and controls the ejaculatory muscles. PDE-5 inhibitors work by blocking cGMP degradation. Erection is caused by parasympathetic impulses from the sacral cord through the pelvic nerves releasing nitric oxide and/or VIP, not an ilioinguinal substance P reflex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"Point, Squeeze, Shoot\": Point = erection (parasympathetic S2–S4), Squeeze = emission (sympathetic T12–L2), Shoot = ejaculation (somatic pudendal). NO→cGMP→smooth muscle relaxation→erection. PDE-5 inhibitors block cGMP degradation. COMLEX: Prostatectomy can damage cavernous nerves (parasympathetic) causing erectile dysfunction — nerve-sparing surgery preserves them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — cGMP second messenger signaling mediates smooth muscle relaxation and is the molecular target of PDE-5 inhibitors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"C":"Sympathetic T12–L2 fibers medi","E":"Erection is caused by parasympathetic impulses from the sacr"}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe stages in the male sexual act","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the process of emission during the male sexual act?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic nerves from T12 to L2 contract the vas deferens, ampulla, prostate, and seminal vesicles, propelling semen into the internal urethra prior to ejaculation","B":"Parasympathetic nerves from S2 to S4 coordinate sequential contraction of the epididymis, vas deferens, and prostate, delivering semen to the bladder neck for temporary storage","C":"Somatic motor fibers in the pudendal nerve drive rhythmic contractions of the ischiocavernosus and bulbocavernosus muscles, which compress the internal urethra and trigger emission","D":"Sympathetic fibers from T1 to T5 stimulate the seminal vesicles and prostate via alpha-2 adrenergic receptors to secrete their contents in coordinated waves into the posterior urethra","E":"Parasympathetic fibers from L1 to L3 relax the internal urethral sphincter while simultaneously causing peristaltic contractions of the ejaculatory ducts to propel semen forward"},"explanation":"Emission is the sympathetic phase of the male sexual act, mediated by fibers from T12–L2 via the hypogastric plexus. Sympathetic activation causes coordinated contraction of the vas deferens, ampulla, prostate, and seminal vesicles, propelling their contents into the posterior urethra. This is followed by ejaculation (somatic, rhythmic contractions of ischiocavernosus and bulbocavernosus muscles). The internal urethral sphincter also contracts during emission to prevent retrograde flow into the bladder. Erection, not emission, is the parasympathetic function (sacral fibers through the pelvic nerves); emission is the sympathetic phase, which expels sperm and prostatic and seminal fluid into the internal urethra. Emission is initiated by sympathetic impulses leaving the cord at T12 to L2 and passing through the hypogastric and pelvic sympathetic plexuses, not from T1 to T5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emission (sympathetic T12-L2) fills posterior urethra → Ejaculation (somatic pudendal) expels. Prostatectomy or retroperitoneal lymph node dissection can damage sympathetic fibers → retrograde ejaculation (semen enters bladder). COMLEX: Retrograde ejaculation identified by post-orgasm urine showing sperm — a named complication of pelvic surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — cGMP second messenger signaling mediates smooth muscle relaxation and is the molecular target of PDE-5 inhibitors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Erection, not emission, is the parasympathetic function (sac","D":"Emission is initiated by sympathetic impulses leaving the co","E":"The internal urethral sphincte"}},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe stages in the male sexual act","batch":1,"stem":"Which muscles are primarily responsible for propelling semen through the urethra during ejaculation?","choices":{"B":"The ischiocavernosus and bulbocavernosus muscles undergo rhythmic contractions driven by somatic motor fibers to propel semen through the urethra during ejaculation","A":"The cremaster and dartos muscles undergo rhythmic contractions coordinated by the hypogastric plexus to generate the propulsive force needed for semen expulsion through the urethra","C":"The detrusor muscle and external urethral sphincter contract sequentially in a somatic reflex arc from S3 to S4 to create the pressure gradient needed for normal ejaculation","D":"The internal urethral sphincter and levator ani muscle relax simultaneously while the prostate undergoes smooth muscle contraction to generate the ejaculatory pressure wave","E":"The perineal transverse muscles and membranous urethral compressor undergo voluntary contraction coordinated by cortical input from the medial prefrontal cortex during ejaculation"},"explanation":"Ejaculation is the somatic phase following emission. The ischiocavernosus and bulbocavernosus muscles undergo rhythmic contractions driven by somatic motor neurons in the pudendal nerve (S2–S4), propelling semen out of the urethra. The cremaster and dartos are thermoregulatory scrotal muscles. The detrusor is a bladder muscle. The internal urethral sphincter actually closes during ejaculation to prevent retrograde flow. Levator ani is a pelvic floor muscle unrelated to ejaculation propulsion. Ejaculation is the rhythmic contraction of the ischiocavernosus and bulbocavernosus muscles and internal genital organs, set off by sensory signals from the filled urethra; Guyton names no perineal transverse muscle or cortical control.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ischiocavernosus + bulbocavernosus = ejaculatory muscles (somatic/pudendal nerve). Distinct from emission (smooth muscle, sympathetic). COMLEX: Perineal body and pelvic floor integrity — bulbocavernosus reflex tests S2-S4 pudendal arc integrity, assessed in spinal cord injury evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — cGMP second messenger signaling mediates smooth muscle relaxation and is the molecular target of PDE-5 inhibitors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"The detrusor is a bladder musc","D":"The internal urethral sphincte","E":"Ejaculation is the rhythmic contraction of the ischiocaverno"}},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the chemistry, secretion, and metabolism of testosterone including conversion to DHT","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes testosterone transport in the circulation after secretion by Leydig cells?","choices":{"E":"Approximately 97% of testosterone is protein-bound in plasma, loosely to albumin or tightly to sex hormone-binding globulin, and circulates bound for 30 minutes to several hours","A":"Approximately 50% of secreted testosterone circulates freely in plasma, while the remaining 50% is bound to corticosteroid-binding globulin for transport to target tissues","B":"Testosterone is secreted as the inactive precursor dihydrotestosterone and is activated in the liver by 5-alpha-reductase before circulating bound to albumin for delivery to target organs","C":"Approximately 97% of testosterone circulates freely as an unbound fraction, which is immediately bioavailable for uptake by target tissues within minutes of secretion from Leydig cells","D":"Testosterone binds exclusively to sex hormone-binding globulin in the plasma, forming a tight complex that protects it from hepatic degradation for periods extending several days"},"explanation":"After secretion, approximately 97% of testosterone is protein-bound: loosely to albumin or more tightly to sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG). Only a small fraction circulates free (about 3% by Guyton's 97% bound; Katzung gives about 2% free, with about 65% on SHBG), which is the biologically active fraction. The protein-bound testosterone circulates for 30 minutes to several hours before being taken up by tissues or degraded. Testosterone is NOT secreted as DHT (DHT is formed in target tissues). Corticosteroid-binding globulin (CBG/transcortin) carries cortisol, not testosterone. Testosterone binds albumin loosely as well as SHBG tightly, and bound hormone circulates only 30 minutes to several hours before uptake or degradation, not days.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Only free testosterone is biologically active. SHBG increases with estrogen, hyperthyroidism, and liver disease → decreases free testosterone. SHBG decreases with obesity, insulin, androgens, and hypothyroidism. COMLEX: Elevated SHBG in liver cirrhosis reduces free testosterone, contributing to hypogonadism seen in chronic liver disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — Steroid hormones act via nuclear receptors to induce DNA-RNA transcription and drive protein synthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"Testosterone is NOT secreted a","C":"Only a small fraction circulat","D":"Testosterone binds albumin loosely as well as SHBG tightly, "}},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the chemistry, secretion, and metabolism of testosterone including conversion to DHT","batch":1,"stem":"In which tissues is testosterone most commonly converted to the more potent androgen dihydrotestosterone, and what enzyme performs this conversion?","choices":{"A":"In target tissues such as the prostate gland and the external genitalia of the male fetus, testosterone is converted by intracellular 5-alpha-reductase to dihydrotestosterone","B":"In Leydig cells of the testis, testosterone undergoes direct conversion to dihydrotestosterone via 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase before it is secreted into the systemic bloodstream","C":"In the anterior pituitary gland and hypothalamus, testosterone is aromatized to estradiol, while dihydrotestosterone is produced exclusively in the adrenal cortex from dehydroepiandrosterone","D":"In hepatocytes during first-pass metabolism, testosterone is converted to dihydrotestosterone by 5-alpha-reductase and this product is then secreted back into the systemic circulation","E":"In skeletal muscle and adipose tissue, testosterone is converted by 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase to dihydrotestosterone for local anabolic effects on protein synthesis"},"explanation":"In certain target organs — particularly the prostate gland in adults and the external genitalia of the male fetus — testosterone enters cells and is converted by intracellular 5-alpha-reductase to dihydrotestosterone (DHT). DHT binds androgen receptors with much higher affinity than testosterone and drives prostatic growth, external genital virilization, and male pattern baldness. 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride) block this conversion and are used to treat BPH and male pattern baldness. Leydig cells secrete testosterone itself; DHT is formed from it in target tissues by 5-alpha-reductase, and 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase is a steroid synthesis enzyme, not that converter. Estradiol is made from testosterone by Sertoli cells and other tissues, a different step; DHT is made from testosterone by intracellular 5α-reductase in target tissues such as the prostate. The liver converts testosterone to weaker androgens (androsterone, DHEA) for conjugation and excretion; DHT forms in target tissues such as the prostate and fetal external genitalia. 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase acts on cortisol/cortisone; testosterone is converted to DHT by 5-alpha-reductase in target tissues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DHT = 5-alpha-reductase product; more potent than testosterone; important in prostate, external genitalia, scalp. Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase → used for BPH and androgenic alopecia. 5-alpha-reductase deficiency → ambiguous genitalia at birth, virilization at puberty (testosterone still present). COMLEX: DHT excess drives benign prostatic hyperplasia — 5-AR inhibitors reduce prostate volume.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — Steroid hormones act via nuclear receptors to induce DNA-RNA transcription and drive protein synthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Leydig cells secrete testosterone itself; DHT is formed from","C":"Estradiol is made from testosterone by Sertoli cells and oth","D":"The liver converts testosterone to weaker androgens (androst","E":"11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase acts on cortisol/cortis"}},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the chemistry, secretion, and metabolism of testosterone including conversion to DHT","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the intracellular mechanism of testosterone or dihydrotestosterone action in target cells?","choices":{"B":"Testosterone or dihydrotestosterone binds a cytoplasmic receptor protein, and the complex migrates to the nucleus, binds a nuclear protein, and induces DNA-RNA transcription within 30 minutes","A":"Testosterone enters target cells and directly phosphorylates transcription factors without binding a cytoplasmic receptor, initiating RNA polymerase activation within 5 minutes of cellular entry","C":"Testosterone binds a membrane G protein-coupled receptor, activating adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP, which then activates protein kinase A to drive protein synthesis in target cells","D":"Testosterone binds the insulin receptor tyrosine kinase on the cell surface and triggers a phosphorylation cascade that leads to GLUT4 translocation and increased glucose uptake","E":"Testosterone binds heat shock proteins in the cytoplasm, which transport it directly to the endoplasmic reticulum where it is converted to active metabolites before entering the nucleus"},"explanation":"As a lipophilic steroid, testosterone crosses the cell membrane and binds a cytoplasmic receptor protein. The hormone-receptor complex then migrates to the nucleus, where it binds a nuclear protein and induces DNA-RNA transcription. RNA polymerase becomes activated within 30 minutes, increasing mRNA and subsequently protein synthesis. This is the classic nuclear receptor mechanism shared by all steroid hormones. cAMP is the second messenger for peptide hormones (LH, FSH), not for steroids. Testosterone acts through a cytoplasmic receptor whose complex goes to the nucleus and induces transcription (RNA polymerase activation within about 30 minutes); it does not directly phosphorylate transcription factors. The testosterone-receptor complex migrates to the nucleus and binds a nuclear protein to induce transcription; it is not routed through the endoplasmic reticulum for conversion first.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroid hormones use nuclear receptors and gene transcription. Latency of 30 min for RNA polymerase activation vs. seconds for cAMP-mediated peptide hormone effects. The same mechanism applies to cortisol, estrogen, and vitamin D. COMLEX: Androgen receptor mutations cause androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) — 46,XY phenotypically female, absent androgen response despite normal testosterone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — Steroid hormones act via nuclear receptors to induce DNA-RNA transcription and drive protein synthesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Testosterone acts through a cytoplasmic receptor whose compl","C":"This is the classic nuclear re","E":"The testosterone-receptor complex migrates to the nucleus an"}},{"id":"sdl39_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the degradation and excretion of testosterone","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary metabolic fate of circulating testosterone that is not taken up by target tissues?","choices":{"D":"Testosterone is rapidly converted mainly by the liver into androsterone and dehydroepiandrosterone, then conjugated as glucuronides or sulfates and excreted in bile or urine","A":"Unfixed testosterone is oxidized directly in hepatocyte mitochondria to acetyl-CoA, which enters the TCA cycle and is completely catabolized to carbon dioxide and water","B":"Testosterone that remains unbound is filtered freely at the glomerulus and excreted unchanged in the urine, with the majority cleared in the first pass through the renal circulation","C":"Unfixed testosterone is converted by the adrenal cortex into cortisol via 11-hydroxylation, representing the main pathway for testosterone recycling during physiologic stress states","E":"Testosterone is sequestered in adipose tissue as a long-term reservoir and slowly released back into the circulation, and it is never fully inactivated throughout normal reproductive life"},"explanation":"Testosterone not fixed to tissues is rapidly converted by the liver into androsterone and dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) — weaker androgens. These are then conjugated as glucuronides (primarily) or sulfates to increase water solubility. The conjugated metabolites are excreted into bile (via the gut) or filtered into urine via the kidneys. This hepatic degradation prevents accumulation of active androgens in the systemic circulation and is impaired in liver disease. Testosterone is not oxidized to acetyl-CoA for the TCA cycle; the liver converts it to the weaker androgens androsterone and DHEA, which are conjugated and excreted. Testosterone not taken up by tissues is converted by the liver and conjugated, not excreted unchanged; only the conjugated metabolites go out in urine or bile. The adrenal cortex does not turn testosterone into cortisol; its fate is hepatic conversion to androsterone and DHEA followed by conjugation. Testosterone not fixed to tissues is rapidly converted, mainly by the liver, and excreted; Guyton describes no adipose reservoir.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liver degrades testosterone → androsterone and DHEA → glucuronide/sulfate conjugation → bile or urine excretion. Liver failure impairs this → elevated estrogens + relative androgen excess (or deficiency) pattern. 17-ketosteroid urine assay measures androsterone/DHEA excretion. COMLEX: Glucuronide conjugation is the primary conjugation pathway for steroid clearance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — Steroid hormones are lipophilic and require glucuronide or sulfate conjugation for renal and biliary excretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Testosterone is not oxidized to acetyl-CoA for the TCA cycle","B":"Testosterone not taken up by tissues is converted by the liv","C":"The adrenal cortex does not turn testosterone into cortisol;","E":"Testosterone not fixed to tissues is rapidly converted, main"}},{"id":"sdl39_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the degradation and excretion of testosterone","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes estrogen production in the adult male?","choices":{"E":"Approximately 80% of male estrogen is produced by hepatic conversion of testosterone and androstanediol in the liver and other peripheral tissues, while Sertoli cells produce the remainder by converting testosterone to estradiol","A":"Males produce estrogens exclusively from adrenal dehydroepiandrosterone, and no estrogen is produced in the testis; all male estrogen originates from the adrenal cortex","B":"Estrogens in the male are produced exclusively by Leydig cells through intracellular aromatase activity, and this accounts for approximately 80% of total male estrogen production","C":"Males do not normally produce measurable estrogens; the small quantities detected in male urine reflect environmental contamination from exogenous estrogen sources","D":"Estrogen in the male is produced exclusively in the seminiferous tubule fluid by FSH-stimulated Sertoli cells and plays no role beyond local support of spermiogenesis"},"explanation":"The adult male produces about one-fifth the estrogen of the nonpregnant female. Approximately 80% comes from peripheral and hepatic conversion of testosterone and androstanediol via aromatase (primarily in the liver). FSH-stimulated Sertoli cells also convert testosterone to estradiol, and the high estradiol concentration in seminiferous tubule fluid supports spermiogenesis. Male estrogens are important for bone density, libido, and feedback regulation. Estrogen excess (from liver disease or obesity) causes gynecomastia. The adrenal is not the sole source: about 80% of male estrogen comes from peripheral and hepatic conversion of testosterone and androstanediol, and Sertoli cells also make estradiol. Leydig cells are not the main source; about 80% of male estrogen arises from peripheral and hepatic conversion of testosterone and androstanediol. Adult men do produce estrogen, about one-fifth the amount of a nonpregnant woman, and it matters for bone density, libido, and feedback regulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Male estrogen = primarily peripheral aromatization in liver/adipose (~80%), plus Sertoli cells converting testosterone to estradiol. Obesity → excess adipose aromatase → elevated estradiol → gynecomastia and suppressed HPG axis. Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole) used in male hypogonadism with elevated estradiol. COMLEX: Gynecomastia in liver disease = impaired estrogen clearance + increased aromatization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — Steroid hormones are lipophilic and require glucuronide or sulfate conjugation for renal and biliary excretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The adrenal is not the sole source: about 80% of male estrog","B":"Leydig cells are not the main source; about 80% of male estr","C":"Adult men do produce estrogen, about one-fifth the amount of"}},{"id":"sdl39_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the degradation and excretion of testosterone","batch":1,"stem":"What is the proposed physiological role of the high estrogen concentration found in seminiferous tubule fluid?","choices":{"B":"Estrogen in the tubule fluid suppresses FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary via negative feedback, calibrating the rate of spermatogenesis proportionally to the available LH signal","A":"The high estrogen in seminiferous tubule fluid, made by FSH-stimulated Sertoli cells that convert testosterone to estradiol, is believed to play an important role in spermiogenesis","C":"Estrogen in the tubule fluid acts as the primary mitogenic signal driving spermatogonial cell division, replacing the role of FSH in initiating the first round of meiosis at puberty","D":"Estrogen in the tubule fluid maintains the blood-testis barrier by stimulating tight junction protein expression in Sertoli cells, protecting haploid sperm from autoimmune attack","E":"Estrogen in the tubule fluid activates the capacitation reaction by binding zona pellucida glycoproteins, priming sperm for fertilization before they leave the seminiferous tubule"},"explanation":"The concentration of estrogen in seminiferous tubule fluid is quite high relative to systemic levels, produced locally by FSH-stimulated Sertoli cells via aromatase. This local estrogen is believed to play an important role in spermiogenesis — the final differentiation of spermatids into spermatozoa. Systemic feedback on FSH is mediated primarily by inhibin, not tubule estrogen. Spermatogonial division is driven by FSH and testosterone. Capacitation occurs in the female genital tract, not the seminiferous tubule. Tubule estrogen is not described as maintaining the blood-testis barrier; its proposed role is in spermiogenesis, the final differentiation of spermatids into spermatozoa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High intratesticular estradiol from Sertoli cell aromatase supports spermiogenesis locally. Aromatase excess syndromes can paradoxically impair spermatogenesis by suppressing HPG axis through estrogen feedback. COMLEX: Sertoli cell-only syndrome (Del Castillo) — Sertoli cells present but no germline cells → azoospermia, elevated FSH, normal testosterone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — Steroid hormones are lipophilic and require glucuronide or sulfate conjugation for renal and biliary excretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Systemic feedback on FSH is me","C":"Spermatogonial division is dri","D":"Tubule estrogen is not described as maintaining the blood-te","E":"Capacitation occurs in the fem"}},{"id":"sdl39_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best summarizes the anabolic and metabolic effects of testosterone on skeletal muscle and basal metabolism?","choices":{"A":"Testosterone causes approximately a 50% increase in skeletal muscle mass compared to females through increased protein synthesis, and raises the basal metabolic rate by 5 to 15%","B":"Testosterone causes approximately a 25% increase in skeletal muscle mass through increased glycogen synthesis and raises the basal metabolic rate by up to 30% via mitochondrial biogenesis","C":"Testosterone causes approximately a 50% increase in adipose tissue lipase activity, leading to fat mobilization rather than muscle gain, while raising the basal metabolic rate by 5 to 15%","D":"Testosterone causes skeletal muscle mass increases only in the presence of growth hormone, and its independent effect on basal metabolic rate does not exceed 3% above baseline values","E":"Testosterone raises basal metabolic rate primarily through activation of brown adipose tissue thermogenesis, with muscle mass increases being entirely secondary to this metabolic rate effect"},"explanation":"One of testosterone's most important functions is protein anabolism: it increases muscle mass approximately 50% above female levels through increased protein synthesis in muscle and other tissues. Testosterone raises the basal metabolic rate about 5–10% at normal adolescent levels and by as much as 15% with large injected doses, thought to be an indirect result of the increased protein content (especially enzymes) increasing cellular activity. Increases in skin thickness and voice deepening also partially result from this anabolic protein function. This is the basis for anabolic steroid abuse in athletes. Testosterone raises muscle mass about 50% above female levels through protein synthesis, not 25% through glycogen synthesis, and its BMR effect is at most about 15%, not 30%. Testosterone's anabolic effect is increased protein synthesis in muscle, not mobilization of fat through adipose lipase. Guyton attributes the muscle gain (about 50% above female levels) to testosterone's own protein anabolism, and BMR rises 5–10% at normal adolescent levels, up to 15% with large doses, more than 3%. The BMR rise is thought to be an indirect result of increased protein content (enzymes) raising cellular activity; muscle gain is a direct protein-anabolic effect, not a consequence of brown fat thermogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone = anabolic steroid → 50% more muscle mass than females, BMR up 5–10% (up to 15% with large doses). Anabolic steroids suppress HPG axis via negative feedback → testicular atrophy, oligospermia, hypogonadism on cessation. COMLEX: Exogenous androgen use causes suppression of endogenous LH and FSH, leading to Leydig cell atrophy and hypogonadism upon discontinuation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Cortisol produces catabolic protein effects in contrast to the anabolic protein synthesis driven by testosterone","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Testosterone raises muscle mass about 50% above female level","C":"Testosterone's anabolic effect is increased protein synthesi","D":"Guyton attributes the muscle gain (about 50% above female le","E":"The BMR rise is thought to be an indirect result of increase"}},{"id":"sdl39_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes testosterone's effects on the male skeleton?","choices":{"B":"Testosterone increases bone matrix and calcium retention, narrows the pelvic outlet into a funnel shape, and causes early epiphyseal closure that limits final adult height relative to castrated males","A":"Testosterone widens the pelvic outlet into a broad ovoid shape similar to the female pelvis and stimulates epiphyseal growth plate proliferation without causing premature epiphyseal closure","C":"Testosterone has no direct effect on bone remodeling but increases peak bone mass by stimulating intestinal calcium absorption via calcitriol, similar to the mechanism of parathyroid hormone","D":"Testosterone prevents epiphyseal closure, allowing the long bones to continue growing throughout adulthood, and increases bone mineral density by stimulating osteoblast activity via androgen receptors","E":"Testosterone increases cortical bone thickness by stimulating periosteal apposition while having no measurable effect on pelvic architecture or the rate of epiphyseal plate maturation"},"explanation":"Testosterone increases bone matrix thickness and causes calcium deposition, resulting in stronger, thicker bones. It specifically affects the pelvis by narrowing the outlet into a funnel shape (vs. the broad ovoid female pelvis). At puberty, testosterone also causes rapid bone growth but eventually causes the epiphyses to unite with the shaft (epiphyseal closure), limiting final height. Men castrated before puberty are actually slightly taller than normal males due to delayed epiphyseal closure from testosterone deficiency, allowing continued growth. Testosterone directly increases bone matrix thickness and calcium deposition and causes epiphyseal closure; it is not described as acting through calcitriol-mediated intestinal calcium absorption. Testosterone eventually causes the epiphyses to unite with the shaft, which limits final height; it does not prevent epiphyseal closure. Testosterone does narrow the pelvic outlet into a funnel shape and does cause epiphyseal closure, so claiming no effect on pelvic architecture or plate maturation is wrong.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone → epiphyseal closure → final height limited. Precocious puberty from Leydig cell tumor or CAH → tall child but short adult. Eunuchs taller than normal males (delayed closure). Male pelvis = narrow funnel outlet; female = broad ovoid. COMLEX: Osteoporosis risk in hypogonadal males managed with testosterone replacement to preserve bone density.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Cortisol produces catabolic protein effects in contrast to the anabolic protein synthesis driven by testosterone","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Testosterone directly increases bone matrix thickness and ca","D":"Testosterone eventually causes the epiphyses to unite with t","E":"Testosterone does narrow the pelvic outlet into a funnel sha"}},{"id":"sdl39_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes testosterone's effects on red blood cell production and extracellular fluid balance?","choices":{"E":"Testosterone increases red blood cell production by approximately 15 to 20%, contributing to men having roughly 700,000 more red blood cells per cubic millimeter than women, and causes minor sodium reabsorption increasing extracellular fluid volume by 5 to 10% after puberty","A":"Testosterone directly stimulates erythropoietin secretion from the kidney, increasing red blood cell production by 30 to 40% and causing a clinically significant rise in hematocrit above 60%","B":"Testosterone causes aldosterone-like sodium retention in the proximal tubule, increasing extracellular fluid volume by 20 to 30% after puberty in males compared to age-matched females","C":"Testosterone directly suppresses erythropoietin production, and the higher red blood cell count in males is entirely due to androgenic stimulation of hepatic erythropoietic factors","D":"Testosterone has no measurable effect on extracellular fluid volume and only modestly increases red blood cell count by approximately 5%, primarily through stimulation of splenic red pulp activity"},"explanation":"Testosterone increases RBC production approximately 15–20%, giving men about 700,000 more RBCs per mm³ than women. This effect is likely indirect — testosterone increases metabolic rate, which increases tissue oxygen demand, stimulating erythropoiesis. Testosterone does NOT appear to directly increase erythropoietin. For electrolytes: testosterone has weak sodium-retaining effects (minor compared to aldosterone), causing extracellular fluid volume to increase 5–10% after puberty, contributing to the higher blood volume in males. Testosterone does not suppress erythropoietin; it raises RBC production about 15–20%, probably indirectly through higher metabolic rate and oxygen demand. Testosterone raises RBC production about 15–20%, not 5%, and it does increase extracellular fluid volume by 5–10% after puberty through weak sodium retention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone → 15-20% more RBCs → higher hemoglobin/hematocrit in males vs females. Testosterone therapy in transgender males → polycythemia risk. Mechanism likely indirect via increased metabolic rate, not direct EPO stimulation. COMLEX: Hematocrit monitoring recommended during testosterone replacement therapy given erythropoietic effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Cortisol produces catabolic protein effects in contrast to the anabolic protein synthesis driven by testosterone","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Testosterone does NOT appear t","B":"For electrolytes: testosterone","C":"Testosterone does not suppress erythropoietin; it raises RBC","D":"Testosterone raises RBC production about 15–20%, not 5%, and"}},{"id":"sdl39_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand how male sexual hormones are regulated by hypothalamus and anterior pituitary; interpret labs to differentiate primary vs secondary hypogonadism","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the pattern of GnRH secretion and its physiological significance for gonadotropin release?","choices":{"A":"GnRH is secreted tonically from the median eminence directly into the systemic circulation, bypassing the portal system, and reaches the anterior pituitary via retrograde blood flow","B":"GnRH is secreted in pulses every 6 to 8 hours, and the pulsatile frequency is regulated primarily by inhibin from Sertoli cells, which acts directly on the arcuate nucleus to set the pulse interval","C":"GnRH is secreted continuously without pulsation in normal physiology, and it is only during states of pathologic stress that pulsatile secretion emerges to transiently suppress gonadotropin output","D":"GnRH pulses occur every 30 minutes in men and stimulate LH and FSH equally; the ratio of LH to FSH depends entirely on the amplitude of each pulse rather than the frequency of pulses","E":"GnRH is secreted in pulses every 1 to 3 hours, and this pattern is required for normal gonadotropin secretion, because continuous GnRH causes receptor downregulation and suppresses LH and FSH"},"explanation":"GnRH is a 10-amino acid peptide secreted in pulses every 1–3 hours by hypothalamic neurons in the arcuate nuclei, medial preoptic area, and paraventricular nuclei. KNDy neurons (expressing Kisspeptin, Neurokinin B, Dynorphin) coordinate this pulsatility. The pulsatile delivery is critical — continuous GnRH paradoxically downregulates GnRH receptors on gonadotropes, suppressing LH and FSH. This is the basis for GnRH agonist therapy (leuprolide) in prostate cancer and endometriosis. GnRH reaches the anterior pituitary through the hypophyseal portal system and is released in pulses by hypothalamic neurons, not tonically into the systemic circulation. GnRH pulses occur every 1–3 hours, not 6–8, and pulsatility is coordinated by KNDy neurons (kisspeptin, neurokinin B, dynorphin), not set by Sertoli inhibin acting on the arcuate nucleus. Continuous GnRH is not normal physiology; pulsatile delivery is required, and continuous GnRH downregulates gonadotrope receptors and suppresses LH and FSH. GnRH pulses occur every 1–3 hours, not every 30 minutes, and the pulsatile pattern itself is what normal gonadotropin secretion requires.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GnRH agonists (leuprolide) given continuously → downregulation → chemical castration. Used in prostate cancer, central precocious puberty, endometriosis. Pulsatile GnRH pump → treats hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. KNDy neurons drive pulsatility; kisspeptin triggers GnRH release. COMLEX: GnRH agonist therapy causes initial flare (about 7–10 days of elevated LH/FSH, Katzung Ch 37) before suppression begins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary gonadotropin secretion through the portal vascular system","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"GnRH reaches the anterior pituitary through the hypophyseal ","B":"GnRH pulses occur every 1–3 hours, not 6–8, and pulsatility ","C":"Continuous GnRH is not normal physiology; pulsatile delivery","D":"GnRH pulses occur every 1–3 hours, not every 30 minutes, and"}},{"id":"sdl39_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand how male sexual hormones are regulated by hypothalamus and anterior pituitary; interpret labs to differentiate primary vs secondary hypogonadism","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the distinct roles of LH and FSH in male reproductive physiology?","choices":{"D":"LH stimulates Leydig cells to make testosterone, and FSH stimulates Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis; both testosterone and FSH are needed to initiate it","A":"LH stimulates Sertoli cells to produce large quantities of testosterone, while FSH stimulates Leydig cells to drive spermatogenesis through androgen-binding protein secretion","B":"LH stimulates Sertoli cells to secrete inhibin and estradiol, while FSH acts exclusively on Leydig cells to maintain basal testosterone levels between LH pulses","C":"LH and FSH are functionally redundant in the male, both stimulating Leydig cells to produce testosterone; FSH is primarily important only for female reproductive function","E":"LH directly stimulates spermatogonia to undergo meiosis, while FSH acts on Leydig cells to maintain interstitial testosterone concentrations needed for sperm cell survival"},"explanation":"LH acts on Leydig cells via cAMP to stimulate testosterone synthesis. FSH acts on Sertoli cells via cAMP to support spermatogenesis and produce inhibin. Both are necessary for spermatogenesis: testosterone diffuses from Leydig cells into the seminiferous tubules providing local androgen drive, while FSH stimulates the Sertoli cells directly. Inhibin from Sertoli cells feeds back selectively to inhibit FSH (but not LH) from the anterior pituitary. LH acts on Leydig cells, not Sertoli cells, and FSH acts on Sertoli cells (supporting spermatogenesis and making inhibin), not exclusively on Leydig cells. LH and FSH are not redundant in men: LH drives Leydig testosterone and FSH drives Sertoli cells, and both are needed for spermatogenesis. LH acts on Leydig cells and FSH on Sertoli cells; neither hormone acts directly on spermatogonia or reverses these targets as described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LH→Leydig→testosterone→androgen effects; FSH→Sertoli→spermatogenesis+inhibin. Inhibin selectively inhibits FSH. Testosterone inhibits both LH and FSH (via hypothalamus). Isolated FSH deficiency → azoospermia with normal testosterone. COMLEX: Male infertility evaluation: FSH elevated → primary spermatogenic failure; LH elevated → primary Leydig cell failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary gonadotropin secretion through the portal vascular system","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"LH acts on Leydig cells, not Sertoli cells, and FSH acts on ","C":"LH and FSH are not redundant in men: LH drives Leydig testos","E":"LH acts on Leydig cells and FSH on Sertoli cells; neither ho"}},{"id":"sdl39_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand how male sexual hormones are regulated by hypothalamus and anterior pituitary; interpret labs to differentiate primary vs secondary hypogonadism","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following laboratory patterns best distinguishes primary hypogonadism from secondary hypogonadism in a male patient?","choices":{"B":"Primary hypogonadism shows low testosterone with elevated LH and FSH from loss of negative feedback, while secondary hypogonadism shows low testosterone with low or normal LH and FSH","A":"Primary hypogonadism presents with low testosterone and low LH with low FSH, as the absence of testicular function reduces the hypothalamic drive for gonadotropin secretion","C":"Secondary hypogonadism presents with elevated testosterone and elevated LH and FSH, reflecting autonomous testicular production that is uncoupled from hypothalamic-pituitary regulation","D":"Primary hypogonadism presents with normal testosterone and elevated FSH only, with LH remaining normal because residual Leydig cell activity sustains partial negative feedback on LH","E":"Secondary hypogonadism is distinguished by elevated testosterone with suppressed LH and FSH, reflecting enhanced peripheral androgen conversion from adrenal precursors in pituitary failure"},"explanation":"Primary hypogonadism = testicular failure (e.g., Klinefelter syndrome, bilateral orchitis, cryptorchidism). The testes fail to produce testosterone and inhibin, removing negative feedback → compensatory elevation of LH and FSH. Secondary hypogonadism = hypothalamic or pituitary failure (e.g., adiposogenital syndrome, pituitary tumor, Kallmann syndrome). Low GnRH or gonadotropins → low LH and FSH → low testosterone. In secondary hypogonadism, the testes are intact and can respond if given exogenous gonadotropins. In primary hypogonadism the failing testes no longer make testosterone and inhibin, which removes negative feedback, so LH and FSH are elevated, not low. Secondary hypogonadism is hypothalamic or pituitary failure with low GnRH or gonadotropins, so testosterone is low, not elevated. In primary hypogonadism the testes fail to make testosterone and inhibin, so testosterone is low and gonadotropins rise, not a normal testosterone with isolated FSH rise. Secondary hypogonadism features low LH and FSH with low testosterone because the hypothalamus or pituitary fails; testosterone is not elevated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary = high LH/FSH + low testosterone (testicular failure). Secondary = low LH/FSH + low testosterone (HPG failure). Klinefelter (47,XXY) = most common primary hypogonadism. Kallmann syndrome = GnRH deficiency + anosmia = secondary hypogonadism. COMLEX: HCG stimulation test distinguishes primary (no testosterone response) from secondary (testosterone rises with HCG) hypogonadism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormones control anterior pituitary gonadotropin secretion through the portal vascular system","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"In primary hypogonadism the failing testes no longer make te","C":"Secondary hypogonadism is hypothalamic or pituitary failure ","D":"In primary hypogonadism the testes fail to make testosterone","E":"Secondary hypogonadism features low LH and FSH with low test"}},{"id":"sdl39_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe physiology of male sexual organs including Leydig and Sertoli cell functions","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man undergoes evaluation for infertility after 18 months of unprotected intercourse. Semen analysis shows azoospermia. Serum testosterone is 480 ng/dL (normal). LH is 3.2 mIU/mL (normal). FSH is 28 mIU/mL (markedly elevated). Testicular biopsy reveals complete absence of germinal cells with preserved Sertoli cells. Which of the following best explains the elevated FSH in this patient?","choices":{"E":"Primary Leydig cell dysfunction causes low intratesticular testosterone with elevated LH as the compensatory pituitary response and cross-stimulates FSH through a shared receptor pathway","A":"Leydig cell failure from LH receptor mutation results in absent testosterone production with secondary loss of the spermatogenic stimulus and compensatory elevation of both LH and FSH","B":"Hypothalamic GnRH deficiency causes secondary absence of FSH stimulation, with the elevated FSH representing compensatory pituitary hypersecretion from loss of testosterone negative feedback","C":"Primary spermatogenic failure with loss of germinal cells leads to absent inhibin production from Sertoli cells, removing negative feedback specifically on FSH and causing its elevation","D":"Defective acrosome formation from a Sertoli cell structural mutation causes accumulation of immature spermatids that stimulate FSH hypersecretion through a paracrine signaling pathway"},"explanation":"Normal testosterone and normal LH indicate Leydig cell function is preserved. The absent germinal cells eliminate inhibin production from Sertoli cells — inhibin is the specific negative feedback signal for FSH. Without inhibin, FSH rises compensatorily. This is Sertoli cell-only syndrome (Del Castillo syndrome). The pattern of isolated FSH elevation with normal LH and testosterone in the setting of azoospermia is classic for primary spermatogenic failure with preserved Leydig cell function. In contrast, Leydig cell failure would elevate LH. GnRH deficiency would lower LH and FSH along with testosterone; this patient has normal LH and testosterone with markedly high FSH, pointing to the loss of Sertoli inhibin. FSH rises here because absent germinal cells remove inhibin from Sertoli cells; no paracrine signal from accumulating immature spermatids is described, and the biopsy shows no germinal cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhibin from Sertoli cells selectively inhibits FSH. Isolated FSH elevation + azoospermia + normal testosterone + normal LH = Sertoli cell-only syndrome (germinal cell aplasia). Klinefelter syndrome elevates both LH and FSH because both Leydig and Sertoli cells are dysfunctional. COMLEX: Testicular biopsy differentiates obstructive azoospermia (normal spermatogenesis, blocked transport) from spermatogenic failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — FSH regulation of oogenesis and inhibin feedback in the female reproductive axis parallels Sertoli-cell inhibin feedback in males","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"In contrast, Leydig cell failu","B":"GnRH deficiency would lower LH and FSH along with testostero","D":"FSH rises here because absent germinal cells remove inhibin ","E":"In contrast, Leydig cell failu"}},{"id":"sdl39_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe physiology of male sexual organs including Leydig and Sertoli cell functions","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer and severe bone pain is started on androgen deprivation therapy using a GnRH agonist. His PSA falls significantly after 3 months and his bone pain improves. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which androgen removal improves outcomes in this patient?","choices":{"C":"Prostate cancer cells proliferate more rapidly in response to testosterone acting through androgen receptors, so removing this trophic signal slows tumor growth and reduces bone pain","A":"Testosterone activates p53-independent apoptotic pathways in prostate cells; removing it restores normal p53-mediated tumor suppression and leads to spontaneous regression of metastatic deposits","B":"Testosterone maintains VEGF-independent tumor angiogenesis in the prostate stroma; androgen deprivation collapses the tumor vasculature and triggers ischemic necrosis of cancer cells","D":"Testosterone upregulates PSA secretion from prostatic cells, which directly promotes basement membrane invasion; androgen deprivation suppresses PSA and thereby halts the invasive phenotype","E":"Testosterone maintains the blood-tumor barrier in prostate cancer metastases; its removal disrupts this barrier and enhances penetration of the GnRH agonist itself into the tumor mass"},"explanation":"Prostate cancer cells retain androgen receptors and are stimulated by testosterone and DHT to grow. Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) — via GnRH agonists, GnRH antagonists, or surgical castration — removes this trophic signal, slowing tumor progression and reducing bone metastasis pain. This is a foundational principle of prostate cancer management. Note the initial LH/FSH flare with GnRH agonists before suppression, which can transiently worsen pain — this is countered with initial anti-androgen therapy. Prostate cancer cells keep androgen receptors and are stimulated by testosterone and DHT to grow; androgen removal works by withdrawing that growth signal, not by restoring p53. Androgen deprivation works by removing the trophic androgen-receptor signal that drives growth; collapse of tumor vasculature is not the mechanism. Guyton says prostate cancer cells are stimulated to grow by testosterone and that drugs lowering testosterone slow the cancer and ease bone pain; it describes no PSA-driven invasion mechanism. The GnRH agonist lowers testosterone; benefit comes from withdrawing the androgen-receptor growth signal, not from a testosterone-maintained blood-tumor barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prostate cancer cells stimulated by testosterone (via AR) to proliferate. ADT = chemical castration via GnRH agonists (leuprolide, goserelin) or antagonists (degarelix). GnRH agonist flare phenomenon: initial LH surge worsens symptoms before downregulation. COMLEX: ADT side effects include osteoporosis, gynecomastia, hot flashes, and metabolic syndrome — all from androgen deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 1 — Granulosa and Sertoli cell functions parallel Leydig/Sertoli interplay; androgen receptor signaling in prostate mirrors estrogen receptor signaling in breast cancer management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Prostate cancer cells keep androgen receptors and are stimul","B":"Androgen deprivation works by removing the trophic androgen-","D":"Guyton says prostate cancer cells are stimulated to grow by ","E":"The GnRH agonist lowers testosterone; benefit comes from wit"}},{"id":"sdl39_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe physiology of male sexual organs including Leydig and Sertoli cell functions","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy is noted on routine physical exam to have a non-palpable right testis. His parents are counseled about the risks of leaving this untreated. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which cryptorchidism impairs fertility?","choices":{"D":"The higher temperature of the abdominal or inguinal position causes degeneration of the seminiferous tubular epithelium, impairing spermatogenesis and raising malignancy risk","A":"The undescended testis is exposed to lower portal venous pressure, impairing Leydig cell blood supply and reducing testosterone secretion from the affected side prior to puberty","B":"Abdominal temperature suppresses Leydig cell LH receptor expression, causing irreversible androgen deficiency limited to the affected side without impairing spermatogenesis","C":"The undescended testis undergoes lymphatic obstruction from the inguinal canal, causing progressive tubular edema and eventual fibrosis of the interstitial cells over several years","E":"Failure to descend prevents exposure of spermatogonia to epididymal maturation signals that are required for the initial meiotic divisions to proceed at the onset of puberty"},"explanation":"Spermatogenesis requires a temperature slightly below core body temperature, maintained by the scrotal position and pampiniform plexus heat exchange. In cryptorchidism, the testis remains in the abdomen or inguinal canal at 37°C, which causes degeneration of the seminiferous tubular epithelium. The Leydig cells are more heat-resistant, so testosterone production may be preserved. Cryptorchidism also significantly increases testicular cancer risk (seminoma). Orchiopexy before age 2 is recommended to preserve fertility and allow better cancer surveillance. Cryptorchidism harms fertility through the higher abdominal or inguinal temperature; Leydig cells are more heat-resistant, so testosterone production may be preserved. The mechanism is heat-induced degeneration of the seminiferous tubular epithelium at body temperature, not lymphatic obstruction with fibrosis of interstitial cells. Spermatogenesis fails because the testis sits at core temperature rather than the slightly lower temperature it requires, not because of missing epididymal maturation signals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryptorchidism → heat-induced tubular degeneration → infertility + 4x increased seminoma risk. Testosterone (via HCG or GnRH stimulation) drives descent. Orchiopexy before age 2 preserves fertility and enables cancer monitoring. COMLEX: Bilateral cryptorchidism in a newborn with bilateral non-palpable testes — must rule out virilized genetic female (CAH) before orchiopexy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 1 — Temperature requirements for spermatogenesis parallel the need for controlled follicular microenvironment in ovarian physiology","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Cryptorchidism harms fertility through the higher abdominal ","B":"The Leydig cells are more heat","C":"The mechanism is heat-induced degeneration of the seminifero","E":"Spermatogenesis fails because the testis sits at core temper"}},{"id":"sdl39_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Outline steps of spermatogenesis, sperm maturation, capacitation, and composition of semen","batch":2,"stem":"A semen sample collected for an infertility evaluation forms a weak gel within a minute of ejaculation, and the sperm in it are relatively immobile. After about 20 minutes the gel liquefies and the sperm become highly motile. Which interaction between accessory gland secretions best explains this sequence?","choices":{"B":"A prostatic clotting enzyme clots seminal vesicle fibrinogen, and fibrinolysin from prostatic profibrinolysin lyses it","A":"Bulbourethral mucus polymerizes on contact with air, and fructose from the seminal vesicles later digests the gel","C":"Calcium from the vas deferens fluid cross-links the sperm tails, and the alkaline prostatic fluid later releases them","D":"Seminal vesicle prostaglandins aggregate the sperm, and inhibitory epididymal proteins later disperse the sperm clumps","E":"Fibrinogen leaking from inflamed prostatic capillaries clots the sample, a finding that indicates chronic prostatitis"},"explanation":"A clotting enzyme in the prostatic fluid causes the fibrinogen of the seminal vesicle fluid to form a weak fibrin coagulum that holds the semen in the deeper vagina near the cervix. The coagulum dissolves during the next 15 to 30 minutes through lysis by fibrinolysin formed from prostatic profibrinolysin. In the early minutes after ejaculation the sperm remain relatively immobile, possibly because of the viscosity of the coagulum, and they become highly motile as it dissolves. This is normal and does not indicate inflammation. Seminal vesicle fluid supplies fructose and other nutrients for the sperm, and its prostaglandins are thought to make cervical mucus more receptive and to cause reverse peristalsis in the uterus and fallopian tubes. The slightly alkaline prostatic fluid helps neutralize the acidity of the vas deferens fluid and the vagina. Inhibitory proteins in the epididymal and ductal fluid keep stored sperm suppressed before ejaculation. Bulbourethral mucus and seminal vesicle fructose do not form or dissolve the gel; fructose nourishes sperm, while the coagulum comes from a prostatic clotting enzyme acting on seminal vesicle fibrinogen and is lysed by fibrinolysin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Semen: seminal vesicles about 60% (fructose, citric acid, prostaglandins, fibrinogen), prostate about 30% (thin, milky, slightly alkaline; calcium, citrate, phosphate, clotting enzyme, profibrinolysin), vas deferens about 10%. COMLEX: Normal semen coagulates and then liquefies within 15 to 30 minutes, and the sperm become fully motile as the coagulum dissolves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — LH surge and ovulation timing require coordination with sperm capacitation for successful fertilization","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Bulbourethral mucus and seminal vesicle fructose do not form","E":"This is normal and does not in"}},{"id":"sdl39_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Outline steps of spermatogenesis, sperm maturation, capacitation, and composition of semen","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student studying human reproduction asks about the mechanism by which a capacitated sperm penetrates the zona pellucida. Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of events in the acrosome reaction?","choices":{"E":"Hyaluronidase released from the acrosome dissolves the granulosa cell layer around the oocyte, proteolytic enzymes then penetrate the zona pellucida, and the sperm fuses with the oocyte membrane","A":"The acrosome reaction begins with the sperm tail releasing dynein motor proteins that mechanically cleave the zona pellucida glycoprotein ZP3 without requiring enzymatic activity","B":"Acrosomal enzymes are pre-released during capacitation to pre-digest the cumulus oophorus, so the sperm arrives at the zona pellucida already armed without requiring a separate acrosome reaction","C":"The acrosome reaction begins with exocytosis of cortical granules that harden the zona pellucida to create a temporary scaffold through which the sperm enzymatically bores a channel","D":"The acrosome reaction is triggered by zona pellucida contact and consists entirely of calcium-dependent membrane fusion between the outer acrosomal and inner oocyte membranes"},"explanation":"After capacitation, sperm reach the zona pellucida. ZP3 contact triggers the acrosome reaction — exocytosis of acrosomal enzymes. Hyaluronidase first dissolves the hyaluronic acid matrix between granulosa cells of the cumulus oophorus. Proteolytic enzymes then cleave zona pellucida glycoproteins, allowing sperm to penetrate and fuse with the oocyte plasma membrane. The cortical granule reaction (not acrosomal) occurs after fertilization to prevent polyspermy by hardening the zona. Capacitation and acrosome reaction are separate sequential events. At the zona pellucida the sperm membrane binds receptor proteins, the acrosome dissolves, and its released hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes digest a path; the reaction is enzymatic, not mechanical cleavage. The acrosome releases hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes that open a path through the granulosa cells and zona pellucida, and only afterward do the sperm and oocyte membranes fuse, so it is more than membrane fusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acrosome reaction = ZP3-triggered exocytosis of hyaluronidase + proteolytic enzymes. Cortical granule reaction (polyspermy block) occurs AFTER fertilization. ICSI (intracytoplasmic sperm injection) bypasses the need for acrosome reaction entirely — used in severe oligospermia or acrosomal defects. COMLEX: Globozoospermia = round-headed sperm with absent acrosome → complete infertility from absent acrosome reaction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — LH surge triggers ovulation and zona pellucida softening; acrosomal enzymes and zona interaction determine fertilization success","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"At the zona pellucida the sperm membrane binds receptor prot","B":"Capacitation and acrosome reac","D":"The acrosome releases hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes "}},{"id":"sdl39_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Outline steps of spermatogenesis, sperm maturation, capacitation, and composition of semen","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man receives cyclophosphamide for non-Hodgkin lymphoma. His oncologist counsels him that semen quality will deteriorate over the coming weeks and that he should bank sperm now. Which of the following best explains the approximately 74-day timeline of spermatogenesis and its clinical relevance?","choices":{"C":"Spermatogenesis takes approximately 74 days from spermatogonium to mature spermatozoon, so a gonadotoxin exposure may not change semen quality for 2 to 3 months after exposure begins","A":"Spermatogenesis takes approximately 30 days total, with meiosis I being the rate-limiting step, explaining why gonadotoxin effects on semen quality appear within 2 weeks of first exposure","B":"Spermatogenesis takes approximately 120 days total because the 60-day epididymal transit is required before sperm acquire fertilizing capacity after leaving the seminiferous tubule","D":"Spermatogenesis takes approximately 14 days because only one meiotic division is required to generate spermatids; the extended timelines cited in older literature included post-ejaculatory survival","E":"Spermatogenesis takes approximately 45 days, and cytotoxic drug effects appear in semen within 7 to 10 days because rapidly dividing spermatogonia are destroyed first and are immediately reflected in the ejaculate"},"explanation":"Spermatogenesis takes approximately 74 days from spermatogonium to spermatozoon (Guyton; First Aid gives about 2 months). Sperm then spend 18–24 hours in the epididymis. Because sperm in the ejaculate were formed ~74 days earlier, the effects of a gonadotoxin (chemotherapy, radiation, heat) on spermatogenesis appear 2–3 months later in semen analysis. Conversely, after stopping a gonadotoxin, recovery of fertility may also take 2–3 months or longer. The entire period of spermatogenesis takes about 74 days, not 30. Spermatogenesis takes about 74 days, and sperm gain the capability of motility after only 18 to 24 hours in the epididymis, not 60 days. Spermatogenesis from spermatogonia to spermatozoa takes about 74 days, not 14. Spermatogenesis from spermatogonia to spermatozoa takes about 74 days, not 45.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 74 days spermatogenesis + 18-24 hrs epididymal transit. Chemotherapy effects on semen lag ~2-3 months. Semen banking before chemotherapy is standard of care. Recovery after cessation takes at least one full cycle and can be much longer after severe gonadotoxins. COMLEX: Sperm banking before gonadotoxic therapy is a preventive medicine principle applicable to COMLEX patient management objectives.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 1 — Oogenesis timeline and primordial follicle pool differ fundamentally from continuous spermatogenesis; males produce 120 million sperm daily vs. fixed female egg reserve","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The entire period of spermatogenesis takes about 74 days, no","B":"Spermatogenesis takes about 74 days, and sperm gain the capa","D":"Spermatogenesis from spermatogonia to spermatozoa takes about 74 days, not 14.","E":"Spermatogenesis from spermatogonia to spermatozoa takes about 74 days, not 45."}},{"id":"sdl39_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe stages in the male sexual act","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with a 2-year history of erectile dysfunction. His cardiologist clears him for sexual activity. He is started on sildenafil. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which sildenafil restores penile erection?","choices":{"E":"Sildenafil inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, which normally degrades cGMP; by blocking cGMP breakdown in corporal smooth muscle, it prolongs the vasodilatory effect of nitric oxide and enhances erection","A":"Sildenafil activates guanylyl cyclase directly to increase cGMP production in corporal smooth muscle, bypassing the need for nitric oxide and correcting the deficient NO signaling caused by vascular disease","B":"Sildenafil blocks alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on corporal smooth muscle, preventing sympathetic vasoconstriction and allowing basal parasympathetic tone to produce vasodilation without NO involvement","C":"Sildenafil increases nitric oxide synthase expression in penile endothelial cells over 4 to 6 weeks, restoring the ability to generate NO in response to sexual stimulation and correcting the vascular defect","D":"Sildenafil blocks muscarinic receptors in the pudendal nerve terminal, prolonging the acetylcholine dwell time in the synaptic cleft and amplifying the parasympathetic signal for penile erection"},"explanation":"During sexual arousal, parasympathetic S2–S4 stimulation causes endothelial cells to release NO. NO activates guanylyl cyclase, producing cGMP, which relaxes corporal smooth muscle. Phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE-5) normally degrades cGMP. In vascular disease (hypertension, diabetes, atherosclerosis), impaired NO release reduces cGMP. Sildenafil (Viagra), vardenafil, and tadalafil are PDE-5 inhibitors that prevent cGMP degradation, amplifying the residual NO signal. They do not activate guanylyl cyclase directly and require some level of sexual stimulation. Sildenafil is a PDE-5 inhibitor that prevents breakdown of the cyclic GMP made after nitric oxide release; it is not an alpha-1 blocker. Sildenafil inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, which degrades cyclic GMP, to prolong the effect of cyclic GMP; it is not described as raising nitric oxide synthase expression. Sildenafil acts by inhibiting PDE-5 so cGMP is not degraded, not by blocking muscarinic receptors or prolonging acetylcholine action.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Erection pathway: S2-S4 parasympathetic → NO → guanylyl cyclase → cGMP → corporal smooth muscle relaxation → vasodilation → erection. PDE-5 degrades cGMP. PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil) block this. Contraindicated with nitrates (additive hypotension). COMLEX: ED in men >40 is often vascular disease until proven otherwise — full cardiovascular risk assessment before prescribing PDE-5 inhibitors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — cGMP is a second messenger that mediates smooth muscle relaxation; PDE-5 inhibitor pharmacology is a direct application of this signaling pathway","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"They do not activate guanylyl ","B":"Sildenafil is a PDE-5 inhibitor that prevents breakdown of t","C":"Sildenafil inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, which degrades cycl","D":"Sildenafil acts by inhibiting PDE-5 so cGMP is not degraded,"}},{"id":"sdl39_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe stages in the male sexual act","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes open repair of an abdominal aortic aneurysm with extensive dissection anterior to the lower aorta and across its bifurcation. Postoperatively he reports that during orgasm he experiences pleasurable sensations but no visible ejaculate, and his urine is cloudy after orgasm. Which component of the male sexual act is most specifically disrupted, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"C":"Emission is disrupted by injury to the sympathetic hypogastric plexus fibers that close the bladder neck and internal sphincter, allowing semen to flow retrograde into the bladder","A":"Erection is disrupted by injury to the parasympathetic cavernous nerves from S2 to S4 that travel along the posterolateral surface of the prostate to reach the corpora cavernosa of the penis","B":"Lubrication is disrupted by damage to the bulbourethral gland ducts in the deep perineal pouch, impairing the mucus secretion that normally precedes semen expulsion","D":"Sensory transduction is disrupted by transection of the dorsal penile nerve branches, impairing tactile feedback from the glans penis to the sacral spinal cord","E":"Ejaculation is disrupted by injury to the ischiocavernosus and bulbocavernosus muscles of the perineum, preventing the rhythmic contractions required for propulsive expulsion of semen"},"explanation":"No visible ejaculate with cloudy urine after orgasm means semen is passing back into the bladder: retrograde ejaculation. Sympathetic impulses leave the cord at T12–L2 and pass through the hypogastric and pelvic sympathetic plexuses to initiate emission (Guyton Ch 81, course SDL 39 PDF p18), and the same sympathetic innervation contracts the internal urethral sphincter at the bladder neck to prevent reflux of semen into the bladder (Moore Ch 6). The superior hypogastric plexus lies anterior to the inferior abdominal aorta and extends across its bifurcation (Moore Ch 5), so dissection there can injure these fibers. Cavernous-nerve injury would impair erection, not send semen into the bladder. Bulbourethral mucus is a minor part of semen and its loss would not explain semen in the urine. Loss of glans sensation would blunt the sensory drive to orgasm, yet he reports pleasurable sensations. Weak bulbocavernosus and ischiocavernosus contractions would reduce propulsion but would not redirect semen into the bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Retrograde ejaculation = semen in post-orgasm urine because the sympathetically contracted internal urethral sphincter fails to close (Moore Ch 6). Kaplan notes it always has an organic cause, including genitourinary surgery and drugs such as thioridazine and α-adrenergic antagonists; Katzung lists guanethidine sympathoplegia as a cause. COMLEX: emission and ejaculation are sympathetic functions (T12–L2), whereas erection is parasympathetic (Guyton Ch 81).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous system coordination via distinct second messenger systems governs each phase of the male sexual response","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Cavernous-nerve injury would i","B":"Bulbourethral mucus is a minor","D":"Loss of glans sensation would "}},{"id":"sdl39_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe stages in the male sexual act","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man sustains a complete spinal cord injury at the T8 level in a motor vehicle accident. During rehabilitation, he is noted to have reflex erections in response to direct genital stimulation but is unable to achieve psychogenic erections from visual or cognitive stimuli. Which of the following best explains this dissociation?","choices":{"A":"Reflex erections use an intact sacral parasympathetic arc (S2 to S4) that needs no supraspinal input, while psychogenic erections need descending pathways from the brain that the thoracic injury interrupts","B":"Psychogenic erections are mediated by the sacral parasympathetic system at S2 to S4, while reflex erections require intact thoracic sympathetic outflow from T10 to L2 that is preserved below this injury","C":"Reflex erections depend on intact somatic sensation of the dorsal penile nerve with spinal integration at T8 to T10; the injury eliminates this pathway while the psychogenic route through the frontal lobe remains intact","D":"Psychogenic erections are mediated by thoracic sympathetic fibers at T1 to T4 that are intact above the T8 lesion; reflex erections depend on spinal interneurons at T6 to T8 that are damaged","E":"Both reflex and psychogenic erections use the same sacral S2 to S4 pathway; the dissociation described reflects incomplete injury with selective preservation of touch sensation below the lesion"},"explanation":"Reflex erections are mediated by a complete spinal reflex arc at S2–S4: genital tactile stimulation → pudendal nerve afferents → sacral spinal cord → parasympathetic efferents → NO release → erection. This reflex is intact below a T8 injury because the sacral cord is preserved. Psychogenic erections require intact descending pathways from the brain (via limbic and cortical centers) through the thoracic cord to the sacral parasympathetic neurons — this pathway is severed at T8. This distinction is important in spinal cord injury rehabilitation. Guyton places the reflex mechanisms of the male sexual act in the sacral and lumbar cord, initiated by genital stimulation or psychic input from the brain, so the choice reverses which route needs the brain. Genital sensory signals travel by the pudendal nerve to the sacral cord, and the reflex mechanisms are integrated in the sacral and lumbar cord without needing brain function; psychic stimulation is the part that comes from the brain. Erection is caused by parasympathetic impulses from the sacral cord through the pelvic nerves, not by thoracic sympathetic fibers at T1 to T4. Guyton says the act can be initiated by psychic stimulation from the brain or by genital stimulation acting through sacral and lumbar cord reflexes, so these are separate inputs rather than one S2 to S4 pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reflex erection = intact sacral S2-S4 arc (below T8 injury, sacral cord intact). Psychogenic erection = descending cortical input, lost above T8. A complete lesion that spares the sacral cord preserves reflex erections; destruction of the S2–S4 segments abolishes them. COMLEX: Spinal cord injury sexual rehabilitation — preserving reflex erections enables penile prosthesis planning; bladder and bowel dysfunction co-occur from same sacral cord involvement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Parasympathetic NO-cGMP signaling drives reflex erection; understanding second messenger pathways allows prediction of which pharmacologic agents enhance or impair erectile function","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Guyton places the reflex mechanisms of the male sexual act i","C":"Genital sensory signals travel by the pudendal nerve to the ","D":"Erection is caused by parasympathetic impulses from the sacr","E":"Guyton says the act can be initiated by psychic stimulation "}},{"id":"sdl39_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the chemistry, secretion, and metabolism of testosterone including conversion to DHT","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn with 46,XY karyotype is found to have ambiguous genitalia: a small phallus, bifid scrotum, and perineal hypospadias. Serum testosterone level is normal. Serum dihydrotestosterone is undetectable. The father reports that a paternal uncle had a similar presentation and virilized at puberty. Which enzyme deficiency best explains this presentation?","choices":{"E":"5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency, which blocks conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone in the external genitalia and prostate, impairing their development despite normal testosterone","A":"Aromatase deficiency, which prevents conversion of testosterone to estradiol in the fetal testis and causes masculinization of a genetic female fetus through unopposed androgen excess","B":"21-hydroxylase deficiency, which blocks cortisol synthesis and diverts adrenal precursors toward androgen overproduction, causing virilization of a genetic female rather than ambiguous genitalia in a 46,XY male","C":"11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, which impairs aldosterone and cortisol synthesis while causing excess androgen accumulation due to shunting of steroid precursors in the adrenal cortex","D":"17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency, which reduces both cortisol and sex steroid synthesis, causing ambiguous genitalia in a 46,XY fetus through failure of adequate testosterone production in the testes"},"explanation":"5-alpha-reductase type 2 deficiency is autosomal recessive, explaining the positive family history. DHT is required for development of the external genitalia (penis, scrotum, prostate) and the urogenital sinus derivatives in the male fetus, while the internal reproductive organs (vas deferens, epididymis, seminal vesicles) are testosterone-dependent and thus normal. At puberty, rising testosterone levels can cause significant virilization in the classic Guevedoces phenotype. Normal testosterone rules out 17-OH deficiency; the 46,XY karyotype rules out CAH presentations. Aromatase converts testosterone to estradiol; with normal testosterone and undetectable DHT, the block is in testosterone-to-DHT conversion, not estrogen synthesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase-2 deficiency → normal testosterone, absent DHT → female or ambiguous external genitalia at birth → virilization at puberty (testosterone rises). Classic board: \"raised as female, virilizes at puberty.\" Compare to complete AIS: XY female at birth AND at puberty due to absent androgen receptor. COMLEX: Distinguish: AIS (receptor absent), 5-AR2 deficiency (DHT absent), 17-OH deficiency (testosterone absent) by hormone levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — Nuclear receptor mechanism of steroid action: DHT binds androgen receptor with higher affinity than testosterone, explaining the specific external genital virilization defect when 5-AR2 is absent","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Aromatase converts testosterone to estradiol; with normal te","B":"Normal testosterone rules out ","C":"Normal testosterone rules out ","D":"Normal testosterone rules out "}},{"id":"sdl39_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the chemistry, secretion, and metabolism of testosterone including conversion to DHT","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis presents with bilateral gynecomastia and decreased libido. Serum total testosterone is 280 ng/dL (low-normal). Serum estradiol is markedly elevated. His doctor explains that impaired liver function shifts the androgen-to-estrogen balance. Which of the following best explains the elevated estradiol in the setting of low-normal testosterone?","choices":{"B":"Hepatic impairment reduces the liver's ability to degrade testosterone and convert it to inactive metabolites; meanwhile, increased peripheral aromatase activity in adipose tissue converts available androgens to estradiol, raising the estrogen-to-androgen ratio","A":"Cirrhosis causes direct Leydig cell stimulation via elevated portal ammonia, increasing testicular aromatase activity and overproducing estradiol from local testosterone within the testis","C":"Cirrhosis causes elevated FSH due to hepatic clearance failure, which overstimulates Sertoli cell aromatase and drives excessive testicular estradiol synthesis despite low-normal total testosterone","D":"Cirrhosis elevates portal estrogens derived from the gut microbiome, which directly suppress Leydig cell testosterone synthesis through androgen receptor downregulation in the testicular interstitium","E":"Cirrhosis dramatically elevates sex hormone-binding globulin production in damaged hepatocytes, increasing total testosterone while reducing free androgens and simultaneously elevating bioavailable estradiol"},"explanation":"In the male, approximately 80% of estrogen comes from peripheral aromatization of testosterone in the liver and adipose tissue. Cirrhosis impairs hepatic degradation of estrogens (normally cleared via glucuronide conjugation) and reduces conversion of testosterone to androsterone/DHEA. Additionally, increased adipose aromatase activity (exacerbated by obesity common in alcoholic liver disease) converts testosterone to estradiol. Raised SHBG does contribute in cirrhosis by lowering free testosterone, but it would raise rather than lower total testosterone and does not by itself produce the high estradiol here. The result is elevated estradiol with relatively low testosterone → feminization, gynecomastia, and gonadotropin suppression through estrogen feedback.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cirrhosis → impaired estrogen clearance + increased peripheral aromatization → elevated estradiol → gynecomastia. Also: cirrhosis → elevated SHBG → reduced free testosterone. Spider angiomata, palmar erythema, gynecomastia in cirrhosis = excess estrogen. COMLEX: Alcoholic liver disease causes testicular atrophy from direct ethanol toxicity AND from elevated estrogens suppressing the HPG axis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — Steroid hormone metabolism via nuclear receptor pathways; hepatic conjugation and clearance of sex steroids determines the systemic free hormone concentration available for receptor binding","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"E":"Raised SHBG does contribute in"}},{"id":"sdl39_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the chemistry, secretion, and metabolism of testosterone including conversion to DHT","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old man with advanced prostate cancer undergoes bilateral orchiectomy. Afterward his serum testosterone falls to a small fraction of its previous level but remains measurable. Which source accounts for the testosterone that remains?","choices":{"D":"The adrenal glands, which normally supply about 5% of male testosterone along with weaker androgens","A":"The prostatic epithelium, which synthesizes testosterone from cholesterol and converts it to dihydrotestosterone","B":"Hepatocytes, which rebuild testosterone from the androsterone and etiocholanolone that return in the bile","C":"Adipose tissue, whose aromatase enzyme converts circulating estradiol back into testosterone in the fat","E":"Skeletal muscle, which produces testosterone locally in proportion to its greater mass in adult men"},"explanation":"Men produce about 8 mg of testosterone daily; about 95% comes from the Leydig cells and only about 5% from the adrenal glands. The adrenals also produce most of the weak androgens androstenedione, dehydroepiandrosterone and DHEA sulfate. Removing both testes therefore removes most testosterone, which is why orchiectomy has been used to reduce endogenous androgen in advanced prostatic carcinoma. The prostate converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone with 5-alpha-reductase but does not synthesize it. The liver degrades testosterone to inactive products such as androsterone and etiocholanolone, which are conjugated and excreted in the urine. Aromatase converts testosterone to estradiol in tissues such as adipose tissue, liver and hypothalamus, not the reverse. Skeletal muscle is a target of testosterone, not a source.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone is about 95% from LH-stimulated Leydig cells and about 5% from the adrenals; androgen potency DHT > testosterone > androstenedione > DHEA. COMLEX: Androgen suppression for advanced prostate cancer (orchiectomy, or a GnRH analog) removes the testicular testosterone supply.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — Three zones of the adrenal cortex and their products, including adrenal androgens from the zona reticularis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl39_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the degradation and excretion of testosterone","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old competitive weightlifter has taken high doses of oral methyltestosterone for 4 months. He now has jaundice and itching. Total bilirubin is 6.2 mg/dL, mostly conjugated, alkaline phosphatase is elevated, aminotransferases are mildly elevated, and ultrasound shows no bile duct dilation. Which of the following best explains both why this steroid works when swallowed and why his liver is affected?","choices":{"C":"Its 17-alkyl group resists the hepatic inactivation that destroys most swallowed testosterone, and such steroids cause cholestasis","A":"It is a 5-alpha-reduced androgen that the liver cannot break down, so it builds up inside hepatocytes and causes their necrosis","B":"It is absorbed into the lymph and so bypasses the liver, and his jaundice comes from hemolysis of the extra red cells it produces","D":"It is changed in the gut wall to dihydrotestosterone that is absorbed intact, and dihydrotestosterone narrows the small bile ducts","E":"It binds sex hormone-binding globulin so tightly that liver enzymes cannot reach it, and the bound hormone clogs the canaliculi"},"explanation":"Swallowed testosterone is rapidly absorbed but largely converted to inactive metabolites, so only about one sixth of the dose is available in active form. The main pathway of degradation is in the liver: reduction of the A ring yields inactive products that are conjugated and excreted (androsterone and etiocholanolone in Katzung; androsterone and dehydroepiandrosterone in Guyton). Testosterone derivatives alkylated at the 17 position, such as methyltestosterone and fluoxymesterone, are active when given by mouth; Katzung states this without naming the mechanism, and the contrast with plain testosterone implies that the 17-alkyl group protects the molecule from this hepatic inactivation. Most synthetic androgens and anabolic agents are 17-alkyl-substituted, and they often cause hepatic dysfunction early in treatment, in proportion to the dose. Bilirubin can rise until cholestatic jaundice appears, which reverses when the drug is stopped. High doses can also cause peliosis hepatis, hepatic failure, and hepatic adenomas or carcinomas. Hemolysis would raise unconjugated, not conjugated, bilirubin. 5-alpha-reduction to dihydrotestosterone takes place in target tissues such as the prostate and skin and does not explain oral activity or cholestasis. Only about 2% of testosterone circulates free; binding to sex hormone-binding globulin does not cause cholestasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 17-alpha-alkylated androgens (methyltestosterone, fluoxymesterone) are orally active but hepatotoxic: dose-related, reversible cholestatic jaundice, and at high doses peliosis hepatis and hepatic adenomas or carcinomas. COMLEX: Anabolic steroid abuse also causes azoospermia, testicular atrophy, gynecomastia, erythrocytosis, lower HDL and aggressive behavior.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 5 — Hepatic inactivation of testosterone and why 17-alkylated oral androgens escape it at the cost of cholestatic liver injury","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"5-alpha-reduction to dihydrote","B":"Hemolysis would raise unconjug","D":"5-alpha-reduction to dihydrote"}},{"id":"sdl39_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the degradation and excretion of testosterone","batch":2,"stem":"A pharmacology lecturer explains why testosterone metabolites must be conjugated before excretion. Which of the following best explains the physiological necessity of glucuronide or sulfate conjugation of steroid metabolites?","choices":{"D":"Glucuronide and sulfate conjugation increases the water solubility of lipophilic steroid metabolites, enabling their excretion into bile and urine since unconjugated steroids are too lipophilic to be efficiently cleared by the kidneys or biliary system","A":"Glucuronide conjugation activates testosterone metabolites for receptor binding in peripheral tissues, allowing them to exert weak androgenic effects in organs distant from the testis and adrenal gland","B":"Sulfate conjugation converts inactive androsterone to a potent neurosteroid that modulates GABA-A receptor activity in the CNS, facilitating neurological effects before renal filtration occurs","C":"Glucuronide and sulfate conjugation prevents tubular secretion of testosterone metabolites in the proximal nephron by masking the organic anion transporter recognition sequences","E":"Conjugation as glucuronides prevents enterohepatic recirculation of testosterone metabolites by inactivating intestinal beta-glucuronidase, which would otherwise reabsorb the deconjugated free steroid"},"explanation":"Steroids are lipophilic molecules that would otherwise be reabsorbed in the renal tubule or intestine rather than excreted. Conjugation with glucuronic acid (primarily) or sulfate dramatically increases the polarity and water solubility of these molecules, allowing them to be filtered at the glomerulus and excreted in urine, or secreted into bile. This is phase II metabolism. The same principle applies to all lipophilic drugs and hormones: conjugation = increased polarity = enhanced excretion. This is not an activation step — it inactivates the steroid. Conjugation with glucuronic acid or sulfate prepares testosterone breakdown products such as androsterone for excretion in bile or urine; Guyton describes no conversion to a neurosteroid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Phase II conjugation = inactivation + water solubilization for excretion. Glucuronidation by UGT enzymes in liver. Products are more polar = filtered at glomerulus = urine, or secreted into bile. COMLEX: Enterohepatic recirculation occurs when gut bacteria hydrolyze glucuronides (deconjugation by beta-glucuronidase) → reabsorption of free steroid → prolonged steroid exposure; antibiotics that reduce gut flora can disrupt this cycle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — Lipophilic steroid hormones require conjugation for excretion; the same chemical property that allows membrane permeability and nuclear receptor access also necessitates metabolic modification for clearance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"This is not an activation step","B":"Conjugation with glucuronic acid or sulfate prepares testost","C":"This is not an activation step"}},{"id":"sdl39_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the degradation and excretion of testosterone","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with hypogonadism asks why he cannot simply swallow tablets of plain testosterone and is instead offered testosterone cypionate injections. Which statement best explains this choice?","choices":{"B":"Swallowed testosterone is absorbed but mostly converted to inactive metabolites, leaving about one sixth active","A":"Testosterone is a peptide hormone that gastric pepsin digests before any of the dose can be absorbed","C":"Testosterone is too water-soluble to cross the intestinal epithelium, so almost none of an oral dose is absorbed","D":"Oral testosterone binds SHBG so tightly in portal blood that no free hormone reaches androgen-sensitive tissues","E":"Intestinal 5-alpha-reductase converts the entire oral dose to dihydrotestosterone, overstimulating the prostate"},"explanation":"Testosterone given by mouth is rapidly absorbed, but it is largely converted to inactive metabolites, and only about one sixth of the dose is available in active form. The major pathway of testosterone degradation is in the liver, where reduction of the A ring produces inactive products that are conjugated and excreted. Katzung names these products androsterone and etiocholanolone, while the course text (Guyton) names androsterone and dehydroepiandrosterone, conjugated as glucuronides or sulfates and excreted in bile or urine. Parenteral ester forms such as the propionate, enanthate, undecanoate and cypionate are absorbed slowly and are hydrolyzed to free testosterone at the injection site, and testosterone derivatives alkylated at the 17 position are active by mouth. Testosterone is a lipid-soluble steroid, not a peptide. About 65% of circulating testosterone is bound to SHBG and about 2% is free, so SHBG binding still leaves a free fraction available to target cells. 5-alpha-reductase works in target tissues such as the prostate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone is degraded mainly in the liver (A-ring reduction to androsterone and related products), then conjugated as glucuronides or sulfates and excreted in urine or bile. COMLEX: Because oral testosterone is largely inactivated, replacement uses injected esters (cypionate, enanthate) or 17-alkylated oral derivatives such as methyltestosterone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — Steroid hormone degradation requires hepatic enzymatic conversion followed by phase II conjugation; impaired hepatic clearance disrupts the hormonal balance between androgens and estrogens","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Testosterone is a lipid-solubl","C":"Testosterone is a lipid-solubl","D":"About 65% of circulating testo"}},{"id":"sdl39_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had both testes removed at age 30 after a crush injury and has declined testosterone replacement. Which of the following best describes the changes expected from castration after puberty?","choices":{"C":"His voice stays almost as deep and erection still occurs, but masculine hair, thick bones and the virile musculature are lost","A":"His voice rises back to a childlike pitch, and his penis and scrotum shrink back to their prepubertal size within a few years","B":"His masculine features stay unchanged, because adrenal androgens take over the full work of the testes that were removed","D":"He grows taller, because his epiphyses reopen without testosterone and long-bone growth resumes during early adulthood","E":"He develops progressive scalp baldness, because testosterone protects the hair on the top of the head from shedding"},"explanation":"Guyton describes castration after puberty: some male secondary sexual characteristics revert to those of a child and others remain adult. The sexual organs regress slightly in size but not to a childlike state, erection can still occur although with less ease, and the voice regresses from its bass quality only slightly; masculine hair production, the thick masculine bones and the musculature of the virile male are lost. A childlike voice and infantile sex organs describe eunuchism, the loss of the testes before puberty. About 95% of testosterone comes from the Leydig cells and only about 5% from the adrenals, so adrenal androgens cannot keep masculine features intact. The prepubertal eunuch ends up slightly taller than a normal man because his epiphyses are slow to unite, but closed epiphyses in an adult do not reopen. Testosterone decreases hair growth on the top of the head, and a man without functional testes does not become bald.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prepubertal loss of the testes (eunuchism): infantile genitalia, childlike voice, slightly taller stature from slow epiphyseal union, thin bones, weak muscles and no male-pattern baldness. Postpubertal castration: genitalia regress only slightly and the voice stays deep, but masculine hair, bone thickness and musculature are lost. COMLEX: Androgen replacement in hypogonadal men increases lean body mass and decreases bone turnover.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — Testosterone-dependent male characteristics and which of them revert after castration","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"A childlike voice and infantil"}},{"id":"sdl39_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy is noted to have Tanner stage IV development at his annual well-child visit. His voice has deepened significantly in the past year, he has developed facial hair, and his muscle mass has increased substantially. His parents ask which hormone is primarily driving these changes. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which testosterone produces these secondary sexual characteristics?","choices":{"A":"FSH secreted from the anterior pituitary acts directly on muscle satellite cells to drive protein synthesis and hypertrophy, while LH independently drives laryngeal growth through a separate androgen-independent pathway","B":"Dihydrotestosterone secreted directly by Leydig cells acts on hypothalamic androgen receptors to amplify GnRH pulsatility, secondarily driving laryngeal and musculoskeletal changes through IGF-1 stimulation","C":"Growth hormone surges triggered by sleep-associated GHRH secretion drive the majority of musculoskeletal changes at puberty, with testosterone playing only a secondary permissive role limited to hair follicle stimulation","D":"Testosterone secreted from Leydig cells causes laryngeal mucosa hypertrophy and laryngeal enlargement producing voice deepening, stimulates androgen-sensitive hair follicles for facial and body hair growth, and drives skeletal muscle protein synthesis increasing muscle mass by approximately 50%","E":"Adrenal dehydroepiandrosterone converted to active androgens in peripheral tissues is the primary driver of all secondary sexual characteristics in males, with testicular testosterone contributing only to genital growth"},"explanation":"Testosterone from Leydig cells (stimulated by LH) drives pubertal secondary sexual characteristics: (1) laryngeal hypertrophy → voice deepening (\"cracking\" voice then adult masculine voice); (2) stimulation of androgen-sensitive follicles → pubic, axillary, facial, and chest hair; (3) protein anabolism → ~50% muscle mass increase vs females; (4) skin thickening; (5) sebaceous gland stimulation → acne. DHT is responsible specifically for external genital growth, prostate growth, and scalp hair loss (not for voice or muscle). Adrenal androgens (DHEA) cause pubic/axillary hair (adrenarche) but are too weak for full virilization. Testosterone from LH-stimulated Leydig cells drives the pubertal voice, hair, and muscle changes; FSH does not act on muscle, and LH acts through testosterone, not directly on the larynx. Leydig cells secrete testosterone, which is partly converted to DHT in target tissues such as the prostate and fetal external genitalia; testosterone itself causes the laryngeal, hair, and muscle changes. Testosterone itself drives the voice deepening, body hair, and about 50% greater muscle mass through protein anabolism; growth hormone surges are not the primary driver.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone drives: voice deepening, facial/body hair, muscle mass, bone growth. DHT drives: external genital growth, prostate growth, scalp hair loss (MPB), acne. Adrenal DHEA drives: pubic/axillary hair (adrenarche precedes gonadarche). Classic: 5-AR-2 deficiency → absent DHT → no external genital virilization but normal testosterone-driven features at puberty. COMLEX: Pubertal gynecomastia reflects transient estrogen-testosterone imbalance during early puberty.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Testosterone anabolic effects on protein synthesis parallel cortisol catabolic effects; both act via nuclear receptor-mediated gene transcription but produce opposing protein metabolic outcomes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Testosterone from LH-stimulated Leydig cells drives the pube","B":"Leydig cells secrete testosterone, which is partly converted","C":"Testosterone itself drives the voice deepening, body hair, a","E":"Adrenal androgens (DHEA) cause"}},{"id":"sdl39_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism begins intramuscular testosterone injections. Over the next 3 months his beard thickens, and he develops oily facial skin with acne. Which effect of testosterone best explains his skin changes?","choices":{"D":"Increased secretion by the sebaceous glands, especially on the face, together with thickening of the skin","A":"Aromatization of testosterone to estradiol in the skin, with the estrogen excess plugging the sebaceous ducts","B":"Suppression of cutaneous immunity, allowing a bacterial skin infection that mimics acne across the face","C":"Stimulation of adrenal cortisol output through ACTH, with the cortisol excess producing a steroid acne","E":"Thinning of the epidermis and dermis, which leaves the hair follicles exposed to occlusion and infection"},"explanation":"Testosterone increases the thickness of the skin over the entire body and the ruggedness of the subcutaneous tissues, and it increases the rate of secretion of the sebaceous glands. Excessive secretion by the sebaceous glands of the face can result in acne, which is why acne is so common in male adolescence, when the body is first exposed to increased testosterone; after several years of testosterone secretion the skin normally adapts and the acne subsides. Katzung likewise lists more active sebaceous glands and thicker, oilier skin among the pubertal effects of androgens. Testosterone thickens rather than thins the skin. His skin changes follow the direct androgen effect on the sebaceous glands; nothing in the history points to estrogen excess, cortisol excess or infection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone effects: male hair distribution and male pattern baldness (with genetic predisposition), laryngeal growth and a deeper voice, thicker skin and more sebaceous secretion (acne), about 50% more muscle mass, increased bone matrix with earlier epiphyseal closure, and increased red cell production. COMLEX: Acne is common when testosterone first rises, at puberty or with replacement, and usually improves as the skin adapts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — Testosterone anabolic effects on protein synthesis and bone matrix formation parallel cortisol catabolic effects; excess anabolism leads to premature bone maturation through accelerated chondrocyte differentiation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"His skin changes follow the di","B":"His skin changes follow the di","E":"Testosterone thickens rather t"}},{"id":"sdl39_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand how male sexual hormones are regulated by hypothalamus and anterior pituitary; interpret labs to differentiate primary vs secondary hypogonadism","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old male presents to a reproductive endocrinologist with inability to smell since birth and failure to develop secondary sexual characteristics. Serum testosterone is 45 ng/dL (severely low). LH is 0.4 mIU/mL (low). FSH is 0.3 mIU/mL (low). MRI shows absent olfactory bulbs. Which of the following best explains both the anosmia and the hypogonadism?","choices":{"C":"GnRH neurons originate in the olfactory placode and migrate to the hypothalamus during embryogenesis; failure of this migration due to mutations in KAL1 or FGFR1 causes both absent olfactory structures and absent hypothalamic GnRH secretion, resulting in low LH, FSH, and testosterone","A":"The anosmia results from a mutation in the androgen receptor that is also expressed in olfactory neurons; absent androgen signaling impairs olfactory bulb development and simultaneously causes testicular resistance to LH stimulation","B":"Absent olfactory bulbs cause retrograde degeneration of the medial temporal lobe, impairing hippocampal GnRH pulsatility and disrupting the hypothalamic-pituitary axis through limbic pathway damage","D":"Absent olfactory input eliminates a major excitatory drive to hypothalamic kisspeptin neurons, progressively reducing GnRH pulse frequency and eventually causing failure of the HPG axis in early adulthood","E":"The olfactory bulb normally secretes neurokinin B that reaches the pituitary portal system via the cribriform plate; its absence reduces FSH and LH directly without affecting hypothalamic GnRH neuron number"},"explanation":"Kallmann syndrome: GnRH neurons originate from the olfactory placode during embryogenesis and migrate along olfactory nerves to the hypothalamus. Mutations in KAL1 (anosmin-1) or FGFR1 impair this migration, causing GnRH neurons to fail to reach the hypothalamus → absent GnRH secretion → absent LH/FSH stimulation → severe hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. The olfactory nerves/bulbs also fail to develop → anosmia. This explains the unique combination. Treatment: exogenous GnRH pulses or gonadotropin therapy. FA describes Kallmann syndrome as failure of GnRH-producing neurons to migrate from the olfactory pit, not an androgen receptor defect. Kallmann syndrome is failed migration of GnRH-producing neurons from the olfactory pit, not retrograde degeneration of the temporal lobe. Kallmann syndrome is failed migration of GnRH-producing neurons from the olfactory pit, not loss of olfactory drive to kisspeptin neurons. Kallmann syndrome is failed migration of GnRH-producing neurons from the olfactory pit, so hypothalamic GnRH is absent, not an olfactory bulb hormone acting on the pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kallmann syndrome = hypogonadotropic hypogonadism + anosmia. Mechanism: GnRH neuron migration failure from olfactory placode. Mutations: KAL1 (X-linked), FGFR1, PROKR2, others. Lab: low LH, FSH, testosterone. Distinguish from other causes of secondary hypogonadism by anosmia. COMLEX: Kallmann treatment with pulsatile GnRH can restore fertility — demonstrates that the pituitary and gonads are intact and capable of responding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus controls anterior pituitary LH and FSH secretion; the embryological origin of GnRH neurons from the olfactory placode explains why Kallmann syndrome combines anosmia with hypogonadism","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"FA describes Kallmann syndrome as failure of GnRH-producing ","B":"Kallmann syndrome is failed migration of GnRH-producing neurons from the olfactory pit, not retrogra","D":"Kallmann syndrome is failed migration of GnRH-producing neurons from the olfactory pit, not loss of ","E":"Kallmann syndrome is failed migration of GnRH-producing neurons from the olfactory pit, so hypothala"}},{"id":"sdl39_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand how male sexual hormones are regulated by hypothalamus and anterior pituitary; interpret labs to differentiate primary vs secondary hypogonadism","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old male is brought in by his parents for evaluation of obesity and failure to develop secondary sexual characteristics. BMI is 39. Physical exam reveals prepubertal-sized testes and penis, sparse pubic hair, and no facial hair. Serum testosterone is 38 ng/dL (severely low). LH is 0.3 mIU/mL (low). FSH is 0.2 mIU/mL (low). MRI of the brain shows a hypothalamic mass. Which of the following diagnoses does this presentation most suggest?","choices":{"A":"Klinefelter syndrome, in which 47,XXY primary testicular failure causes gonadotropin elevation; the obesity reflects metabolic consequences of androgen deficiency, and LH and FSH would be markedly elevated rather than suppressed","B":"Adiposogenital syndrome, in which hypothalamic damage impairs GnRH secretion and simultaneously injures the adjacent satiety center, causing secondary hypogonadism with low LH and FSH alongside morbid obesity","C":"Primary hypogonadism from bilateral anorchia, in which absent testicular testosterone eliminates negative feedback on the pituitary, causing elevated LH and FSH rather than the suppressed values present here","D":"Cushing syndrome from a corticotroph adenoma suppressing GnRH through hypercortisolemia, with the obesity and gonadotropin suppression as the primary manifestations; cortisol levels would be markedly elevated","E":"Constitutional delay of puberty, a benign condition in which hypothalamic maturation is delayed without structural lesion; the hypothalamic mass seen on MRI represents an incidental finding unrelated to the pubertal delay"},"explanation":"Adiposogenital syndrome (Fröhlich syndrome / hypothalamic eunuchism): a hypothalamic lesion (tumor, infiltration, or injury) damages both the GnRH-secreting neurons → secondary hypogonadism (low LH/FSH/testosterone) AND the adjacent satiety center (ventromedial hypothalamus) → hyperphagia → morbid obesity. The combination of obesity + secondary hypogonadism + hypothalamic lesion on MRI is classic. Klinefelter would have elevated gonadotropins (primary hypogonadism). Constitutional delay has no structural lesion. This is a secondary (not primary) cause of hypogonadism. Bilateral anorchia is primary hypogonadism and would raise LH and FSH through lost negative feedback; this patient's gonadotropins are low and a hypothalamic mass is present.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fröhlich syndrome = hypothalamic lesion → GnRH deficiency (secondary hypogonadism) + satiety center damage (obesity). Key: low LH/FSH/testosterone + structural hypothalamic lesion. Distinguish from simple obesity, which can suppress HPG axis via elevated estrogen from peripheral aromatization. COMLEX: Viscerosomatic reflexes from hypothalamic dysfunction connect to pituitary-gonadal axis — CP3 clinical presentation of hypothalamic-pituitary axis disorders.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — The hypothalamus controls multiple anterior pituitary functions; structural hypothalamic damage can simultaneously impair GnRH secretion (hypogonadism) and disrupt adjacent regulatory centers (satiety) through shared anatomical proximity","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Klinefelter would have elevate","C":"Bilateral anorchia is primary hypogonadism and would raise L"}},{"id":"sdl39_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Understand how male sexual hormones are regulated by hypothalamus and anterior pituitary; interpret labs to differentiate primary vs secondary hypogonadism","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents to a fertility clinic with infertility. Labs reveal testosterone 105 ng/dL (low), LH 0.6 mIU/mL (low), FSH 0.4 mIU/mL (low). He takes no medications. Physical exam shows small soft testes (8 mL bilaterally), decreased axillary and pubic hair, and no gynecomastia. Which of the following best describes the next steps in evaluation to localize the lesion to the hypothalamus versus the pituitary?","choices":{"B":"MRI of the brain and pituitary with gadolinium should be performed, and the other pituitary axes (prolactin, IGF-1, TSH, and morning cortisol) measured, to identify a structural lesion or pan-hypopituitarism","A":"Serum prolactin should be measured as the sole next step, since hyperprolactinemia from a prolactinoma is the only pituitary cause of secondary hypogonadism and no further evaluation is needed if prolactin is normal","C":"Exogenous testosterone should be started immediately to restore virilization and fertility, deferring evaluation of the underlying etiology since testosterone replacement is equally effective regardless of the cause","D":"Testicular biopsy should be performed as the next step to confirm the presence of spermatogonia, which would confirm primary testicular failure as the cause of infertility despite the paradoxically low gonadotropins","E":"A single-dose GnRH stimulation test should be performed and LH measured at 30 and 60 minutes; a brisk LH response confirms the diagnosis of primary hypogonadism since the pituitary is capable of secreting LH"},"explanation":"This patient has secondary (hypogonadotropic) hypogonadism: low testosterone WITH low LH and FSH. The next step is to determine the cause: structural pituitary lesion (prolactinoma, craniopharyngioma, other adenoma), infiltrative disease, or isolated GnRH deficiency (Kallmann). MRI with gadolinium identifies structural lesions. Measuring other pituitary axes (prolactin — most common pituitary tumor; IGF-1 for GH deficiency; TSH for central hypothyroidism; morning cortisol for central adrenal insufficiency) evaluates for pan-hypopituitarism. Testosterone replacement before diagnosis may mask the cause and impair subsequent fertility treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secondary hypogonadism workup: MRI pituitary + prolactin + other pituitary hormones. Prolactinoma most common pituitary tumor → hyperprolactinemia suppresses GnRH pulsatility → secondary hypogonadism. GnRH stimulation test distinguishes hypothalamic (LH rises) from pituitary (LH does not rise) cause. COMLEX: Pan-hypopituitarism from pituitary apoplexy or Sheehan syndrome — all anterior pituitary hormones deficient simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — Anterior pituitary control by hypothalamic releasing hormones: GnRH deficiency is one of several possible HPG axis lesions; systematic evaluation of all pituitary axes localizes the defect and guides treatment selection","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Testosterone replacement befor"}},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":1,"stem":"In addition to maintaining the corpus luteum, human chorionic gonadotropin exerts an interstitial cell-stimulating effect on the testes of a male fetus. What is the primary physiologic consequence of this hCG action during fetal development?","choices":{"B":"hCG stimulates fetal Leydig cell testosterone secretion, driving differentiation of male sex organs and causing the testes to descend into the scrotum near the end of pregnancy","A":"hCG suppresses testosterone secretion by fetal Leydig cells to prevent premature virilization of the external genitalia before the appropriate developmental window","C":"hCG activates fetal adrenal androgen synthesis to provide DHEA precursors for placental estrogen synthesis during the second trimester of pregnancy","D":"hCG promotes Sertoli cell proliferation to establish the blood-testis barrier that is necessary for future spermatogenesis after puberty begins","E":"hCG induces fetal pituitary LH secretion through a short-loop positive feedback mechanism that amplifies fetal testosterone output throughout gestation"},"explanation":"hCG exerts an interstitial (Leydig) cell-stimulating effect on the testes of male fetuses. This small but critical amount of testosterone causes the fetus to develop male sex organs rather than female organs. Near the end of pregnancy, testosterone secreted by the fetal testes also causes the testes to descend into the scrotum. This hCG-driven mechanism is distinct from adrenal androgen synthesis. If Leydig cell stimulation fails, the result is incomplete virilization of the male genitalia and, classically, cryptorchidism. hCG stimulates fetal Leydig cells to secrete testosterone, which drives male organ development; it does not suppress them. hCG's action on the male fetal testis is Leydig cell stimulation, not Sertoli cell proliferation or blood-testis barrier formation. hCG itself stimulates the fetal Leydig cells as an LH-like hormone; it does not work by inducing fetal pituitary LH secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryptorchidism and incomplete male genital development can result from failure of hCG-driven fetal testosterone secretion. COMLEX: Male fetal sexual differentiation requires intact hCG → Leydig cell → testosterone signaling — a high-yield CP3 concept in reproductive endocrinology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — LH and hCG share nearly identical molecular structure and receptor binding; hCG acts as a surrogate LH signal to rescue the corpus luteum from involution and prevent menstruation in early pregnancy.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"hCG stimulates fetal Leydig cells to secrete testosterone, w","D":"hCG's action on the male fetal testis is Leydig cell stimula","E":"hCG itself stimulates the fetal Leydig cells as an LH-like h"}},{"id":"sdl36_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Outline the functions of testosterone","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has a long-standing androgen-secreting ovarian tumor. Over several years she has developed a deeper voice, coarse facial hair and hair loss over the top of the scalp. Her father and two brothers became bald in early adulthood, while her sister, who has normal androgen levels, has a full head of hair. Which statement best explains her scalp hair loss?","choices":{"A":"Baldness needs an inherited tendency plus large amounts of androgen, and her tumor supplies the androgen","B":"Testosterone stimulates scalp hair growth, so her hair loss must come from estrogen secreted by the tumor","C":"Sustained androgen excess makes every woman bald, so her family history adds nothing to the explanation","D":"Androgen excess suppresses thyroid hormone secretion, and the resulting hypothyroidism thins scalp hair","E":"Her hair loss is an inherited trait of aging that would have developed in the same way without the tumor"},"explanation":"Testosterone decreases the growth of hair on the top of the head, and a man without functional testes does not become bald. Many virile men never become bald, however, because baldness results from two factors: a genetic background for baldness and, superimposed on it, large quantities of androgenic hormones. When a long-sustained androgenic tumor develops in a woman with the appropriate genetic background, she becomes bald in the same manner as a man. Her family history supplies the genetic background, and the tumor supplies the androgen; her sister, without the androgen excess, has kept her hair. Testosterone reduces rather than stimulates hair growth on the top of the head, genetic background alone is not enough without androgen, and androgen alone does not produce baldness in everyone. No thyroid mechanism is involved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Male pattern baldness = genetic predisposition plus high androgen; testosterone reduces hair growth on the top of the head while increasing pubic, linea alba, facial and chest hair. COMLEX: Virilization in a woman (deeper voice, hirsutism, scalp hair loss) suggests a long-standing androgen-secreting tumor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 6 — Functions of testosterone, including its effects on hair and skin","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Testosterone reduces rather th","D":"No thyroid mechanism is involv","E":"Testosterone reduces rather th"}}],"40":[{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the monthly ovarian cycle (follicular and luteal phases, corpus luteum formation)","batch":1,"stem":"During the first trimester of pregnancy, progesterone is primarily secreted by which organ before the placenta assumes full steroidogenic responsibility after approximately the 13th week of gestation?","choices":{"A":"The corpus luteum of the ovary, maintained by hCG stimulation, which secretes estrogen and progesterone for the first 13 to 17 weeks","B":"The anterior pituitary gland, under continuous stimulation by GnRH pulses that are paradoxically preserved during the first trimester","C":"The maternal adrenal cortex, which upregulates progesterone synthesis in response to increasing ACTH levels in the first trimester","D":"The syncytial trophoblast cells of the placenta, which begin de novo progesterone synthesis immediately at the time of implantation","E":"The endometrial decidual cells, which autonomously synthesize progesterone in large quantities to sustain the implantation site"},"explanation":"In early pregnancy, hCG maintains the corpus luteum, which continues secreting progesterone and estrogens. If the corpus luteum is removed before approximately week 7, spontaneous abortion almost always occurs. The placenta gradually assumes steroidogenic function and the corpus luteum involutes between weeks 13 and 17. The pituitary actually suppresses FSH and LH during pregnancy; the adrenal cortex does not significantly produce progesterone; trophoblast cells do eventually produce progesterone but not immediately at implantation. Decidual cells develop under progesterone's influence for early embryo nutrition and are not the progesterone source; the corpus luteum, maintained by hCG, supplies it until the placenta takes over.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The corpus luteum is absolutely essential before week 7 of pregnancy; lutectomy before this point causes abortion. COMLEX: This is a key OB physiology concept — \"hCG rescues the corpus luteum\" is a classic phrasiology tested in CP3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — The six anterior pituitary hormones include FSH and LH; their near-complete suppression by placental estrogens and progesterone during pregnancy is central to understanding which organ systems sustain gestation.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The pituitary actually suppres","E":"Decidual cells develop under progesterone's influence for ea"}},{"id":"sdl36_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the monthly ovarian cycle (follicular and luteal phases, corpus luteum formation)","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman (G2P1) at 10 weeks of gestation develops bilateral ovarian torsion that requires emergency bilateral oophorectomy. Postoperatively, her obstetrician counsels her that the pregnancy may still continue successfully. Which of the following best explains why the pregnancy can potentially be maintained after loss of both ovaries at 10 weeks of gestation?","choices":{"E":"By approximately 10 weeks, the placenta can secrete sufficient quantities of progesterone and estrogens to sustain the pregnancy independently of the ovaries, though the transition is not yet fully complete","A":"The maternal adrenal cortex upregulates de novo progesterone synthesis under ACTH stimulation to fully compensate for ovarian progesterone loss at 10 weeks of gestation","B":"The fetal adrenal gland begins secreting progesterone by 10 weeks in quantities sufficient to maintain the uterine decidua and prevent spontaneous abortion after oophorectomy","C":"The anterior pituitary responds to falling progesterone by secreting FSH, which drives de novo steroidogenesis within the endometrial decidual stromal cells of the uterus","D":"Prolactin secreted by the enlarged anterior pituitary acts as a luteotropic hormone and binds progesterone receptors on the endometrium to compensate for corpus luteum loss"},"explanation":"Although removal of the corpus luteum before approximately week 7 almost invariably causes spontaneous abortion, the placenta then develops enough steroidogenic capacity to maintain the pregnancy; Guyton notes abortion can occur sometimes up to week 12, while First Aid places completion of the luteal-placental shift at 8–10 weeks. The placenta progressively takes over progesterone and estrogen production from the corpus luteum during the first trimester, and by weeks 13–17 the corpus luteum involutes naturally without affecting pregnancy. At 10 weeks, the transition is nearly complete, so bilateral oophorectomy at this gestational age has a reasonable chance of sparing the pregnancy, particularly if progesterone supplementation is provided. Guyton has the placenta secrete enough progesterone and estrogens to maintain pregnancy after the first trimester, with adrenal glands supplying DHEA precursors for placental estrogen, not progesterone. The pituitary suppresses FSH and LH during pregnancy, so it does not secrete FSH to drive decidual steroidogenesis; the placenta takes over progesterone and estrogen production. Guyton lists increased prolactin in pregnancy but gives it no role in maintaining the pregnancy; after the first trimester the placenta secretes progesterone and estrogens.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Before week 7 = corpus luteum essential; after weeks 13–17 = placenta fully autonomous. At 10 weeks the transition is ongoing. COMLEX: This is a clinical application of the luteal-placental shift tested in CP3 OB physiology scenarios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary hormones and their target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Guyton has the placenta secrete enough progesterone and estr","C":"The pituitary suppresses FSH and LH during pregnancy, so it ","D":"Guyton lists increased prolactin in pregnancy but gives it n"}},{"id":"sdl36_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the monthly ovarian cycle (follicular and luteal phases, corpus luteum formation)","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman (G3P0Ab2) presents for evaluation of recurrent early pregnancy loss. Both prior losses occurred at 5 to 6 weeks of gestation. Workup reveals normal uterine anatomy and normal thyroid function. Her obstetrician explains that corpus luteum maintenance is critical in early pregnancy and that spontaneous abortion almost always occurs if the corpus luteum is removed before a specific gestational week. What is that critical week?","choices":{"C":"Removal of the corpus luteum before approximately week 7 of pregnancy almost always causes spontaneous abortion because the placenta cannot yet sustain steroidogenesis independently","A":"Removal of the corpus luteum before approximately week 4 of pregnancy almost always causes spontaneous abortion because the blastocyst depends entirely on hCG for implantation signals","B":"Removal of the corpus luteum before approximately week 5 of pregnancy almost always causes spontaneous abortion because fetal cardiac activity has not yet begun to sustain placental growth","D":"Removal of the corpus luteum before approximately week 10 of pregnancy almost always causes spontaneous abortion because placental progesterone synthesis is not fully established until week 10","E":"Removal of the corpus luteum before approximately week 14 of pregnancy almost always causes spontaneous abortion because placental autonomy is not achieved until the late first trimester"},"explanation":"According to Guyton and Hall, if the corpus luteum is removed before approximately the seventh week of pregnancy, spontaneous abortion almost always occurs. This is because the placenta has not yet developed sufficient steroidogenic capacity at this early stage. After week 7, the risk of abortion from lutectomy decreases, and after weeks 13–17 the corpus luteum involutes naturally without risk to the pregnancy. The corpus luteum can cause abortion even up to week 12 if removed, but week 7 is the point below which it almost invariably causes loss. This is the specific Guyton-sourced number to know for boards. Week 4 is too early; Guyton gives about week 7 as the point before which corpus luteum removal almost always causes abortion because the placenta cannot yet sustain steroidogenesis. Week 5 is too early; Guyton gives about week 7 as the threshold before which removing the corpus luteum almost always causes spontaneous abortion. Week 10 is too late; Guyton gives about week 7 as the point below which removal almost invariably causes loss, though abortion can occur up to about week 12. The corpus luteum involutes naturally by weeks 13–17 without harming the pregnancy, so week 14 is not the critical point; Guyton gives about week 7.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corpus luteum essential before week 7; placenta takes over by weeks 13–17. \"Before week 7 = almost certain abortion\" is a board-level fact from Guyton. COMLEX: Recurrent first-trimester loss workup should include evaluation of luteal phase function and progesterone levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary hormones and their target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Week 4 is too early; Guyton gives about week 7 as the point ","B":"Week 5 is too early; Guyton gives about week 7 as the thresh","D":"Week 10 is too late; Guyton gives about week 7 as the point ","E":"The corpus luteum involutes naturally by weeks 13–17 without"}},{"id":"sdl40_n01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the monthly ovarian cycle (follicular and luteal phases, corpus luteum formation)","batch":1,"stem":"Each month several primary follicles begin accelerated growth, but usually only one reaches ovulation. Which of the following best explains why the others undergo atresia?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen from the leading follicle suppresses FSH, while that follicle's own feedback keeps it growing","B":"LH from the pituitary binds only one follicle each month, so all of the others receive no trophic support","C":"Progesterone from the leading follicle directly induces apoptosis in neighboring granulosa cells","D":"Inhibin from the previous corpus luteum destroys all but one of the developing follicles each month","E":"The two ovaries alternate each month, and every follicle in the resting ovary degenerates by default"},"explanation":"After a week or more of growth, one follicle outgrows the others and the remaining follicles involute. The proposed mechanism is that the large amounts of estrogen from the most rapidly growing follicle act on the hypothalamus to depress further FSH secretion, which halts growth of the less developed follicles, while the largest follicle keeps growing through its own positive feedback: estrogen increases its FSH receptors, and FSH with estrogen adds LH receptors to its granulosa cells. LH receptors appear on the granulosa cells of growing follicles through this local mechanism rather than through pituitary targeting of one follicle. Progesterone secretion by the follicle begins only around the time of the LH surge. The previous corpus luteum has already involuted, and the fall in its inhibin is what allows FSH to rise and start the new cycle. Selection is attributed to estrogen-mediated FSH suppression, not to alternation between ovaries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen up-regulates FSH and LH receptors on granulosa cells of the dominant follicle while feeding back negatively on pituitary FSH. COMLEX: Excess exogenous gonadotropin-like stimulation can override this selection, causing ovulation of many follicles and multiple births.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — Negative feedback of estrogen on pituitary FSH limits how many follicles mature","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"LH receptors appear on the gra","C":"Progesterone secretion by the "}},{"id":"sdl40_n02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the monthly ovarian cycle (follicular and luteal phases, corpus luteum formation)","batch":1,"stem":"In a cycle in which fertilization does not occur, which of the following best describes the corpus luteum?","choices":{"A":"It peaks at ovulation, secretes mainly estrogen, and persists for about 3 months before it involutes","B":"It peaks about 7 to 8 days after ovulation, secretes mainly androgens, and persists until menopause","C":"It peaks about 7 to 8 days after ovulation, secretes mainly progesterone, and involutes after about 12 days","D":"It peaks about 2 days before ovulation, secretes mainly inhibin, and involutes within about 24 hours","E":"It peaks about 14 days after ovulation, secretes mainly FSH, and is replaced by the next follicle"},"explanation":"After the ovum is expelled, the remaining granulosa and theca interna cells luteinize under the influence of LH to form the corpus luteum. It grows to about 1.5 cm by 7 to 8 days after ovulation, secretes large amounts of progesterone and estrogen, with more progesterone than estrogen, plus small amounts of inhibin, and then involutes, losing its secretory function about 12 days after ovulation and becoming the corpus albicans. A corpus luteum that persists for months does so only when hCG from a pregnancy maintains it. Theca cells form androgens, but most are converted to estrogens, and the corpus luteum is not maintained until menopause. The corpus luteum forms after ovulation, not before it, and FSH is a pituitary hormone rather than a product of the corpus luteum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The luteal phase lasts a fixed 14 days; corpus luteum progesterone raises basal body temperature, and the corpus albicans is its scar. COMLEX: A rise in progesterone in the second half of the cycle indicates that ovulation occurred.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — Theca and granulosa cells are the ovarian counterparts of Leydig and Sertoli cells","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"The corpus luteum forms after ","E":"The corpus luteum forms after "}},{"id":"sdl40_n13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the monthly ovarian cycle (follicular and luteal phases, corpus luteum formation)","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman who is trying to conceive has very regular menstrual cycles that last 35 days. She asks when in her cycle she is most likely to ovulate. Counting the first day of menses as day 1, which of the following is the best estimate?","choices":{"A":"About day 14, because ovulation occurs on day 14 whatever the cycle length","B":"About day 17, because the two phases lengthen in equal proportion to the cycle","C":"About day 21, because the luteal phase lasts a fairly constant 14 days","D":"About day 28, because a longer cycle reflects a luteal phase of about 21 days","E":"About day 7, because a longer cycle shortens the follicular phase to 1 week"},"explanation":"The interval from ovulation to the next onset of menstruation is almost always 13 to 15 days. Guyton's examples: in a regular 28-day cycle ovulation occurs within a day of day 14, in a 40-day cycle near day 26, and in a 21-day cycle near day 7. First Aid likewise describes the luteal phase as a fixed 14 days, with variability in cycle length coming from the follicular phase. For a 35-day cycle, ovulation is therefore expected near day 35 minus 14, about day 21. Day 14 applies only to a 28-day cycle. Splitting the cycle in half to day 17 assumes both phases stretch equally, which they do not. A 21-day luteal phase contradicts the fixed luteal length. A longer cycle lengthens, rather than shortens, the follicular phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cycle-length variability comes from the follicular phase; the luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days by the lifespan of the corpus luteum. COMLEX: The fertile window runs from about 4 to 5 days before ovulation to a few hours after it, because the ovum survives about 24 hours and sperm up to 5 days.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Sperm survival and capacitation in the female tract define the fertile window","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"A 21-day luteal phase contradi"}},{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the functions of LH and FSH (including the LH surge, ovulation, and corpus luteum maintenance)","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following hormones is secreted by the syncytial trophoblast cells of the early embryo and is first detectable in maternal blood approximately 8 to 9 days after ovulation?","choices":{"D":"Human chorionic gonadotropin, secreted by syncytial trophoblast cells and detectable in blood within days of blastocyst implantation","A":"Progesterone, secreted by the maternal anterior pituitary gland and detectable in blood within the first 24 hours of fertilization","B":"Follicle-stimulating hormone, released directly from the corpus luteum of the ovary after ovulation to sustain early embryo development","C":"Growth hormone, released from syncytial trophoblast cells of the blastocyst to drive protein synthesis in early embryonic tissues","E":"Relaxin, secreted exclusively by the anterior pituitary gland during the first days of gestation before placentation begins"},"explanation":"hCG is a glycoprotein (MW ~39,000) secreted by syncytial trophoblast cells shortly after blastocyst implantation. It becomes detectable in blood 8–9 days after ovulation and rises rapidly to peak at 10–12 weeks (Guyton; First Aid gives 8–10 weeks). Its primary early function is maintaining the corpus luteum to prevent menstruation. FSH and growth hormone are anterior pituitary products; relaxin is secreted by the corpus luteum and placenta; progesterone in early pregnancy comes from the corpus luteum, not the pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCG is the basis of home pregnancy tests; it shares the same alpha subunit as LH, FSH, and TSH and can cause hyperthyroidism at very high levels (e.g., molar pregnancy). COMLEX: hCG peaks at 10–12 weeks (Guyton) or 8–10 weeks (First Aid) and then declines — a classic COMLEX CP3 OB timeline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — The six anterior pituitary hormones include FSH and LH; their near-complete suppression by placental estrogens and progesterone during pregnancy is central to understanding which organ systems sustain gestation.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"B":"FSH and growth hormone are ant","C":"FSH and growth hormone are ant"}},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the functions of LH and FSH (including the LH surge, ovulation, and corpus luteum maintenance)","batch":1,"stem":"The molecular structure of human chorionic gonadotropin closely resembles that of which anterior pituitary hormone, explaining its ability to bind the same receptor and rescue the corpus luteum from involution during early pregnancy?","choices":{"C":"Luteinizing hormone, whose receptor hCG binds with high affinity to maintain corpus luteum steroidogenesis and prevent endometrial shedding","A":"Follicle-stimulating hormone, which shares the identical alpha subunit and a nearly identical beta subunit with hCG, allowing cross-receptor activation at the corpus luteum","B":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone, whose structural similarity to hCG fully explains all thyroid-related changes seen in the first trimester of normal pregnancy","D":"Adrenocorticotropic hormone, whose shared pro-opiomelanocortin precursor explains hCG cross-reactivity at adrenal cortex receptors in early pregnancy","E":"Prolactin, whose shared structural domain with hCG allows dual stimulation of both the corpus luteum and mammary gland ductal tissue in early gestation"},"explanation":"hCG is a glycoprotein with MW ~39,000 that has nearly the same molecular structure and function as LH. It binds LH receptors on the corpus luteum with high affinity, causing the corpus luteum to continue secreting progesterone and estrogens. This prevents luteolysis and endometrial shedding (menstruation). hCG also has weak thyrotropic activity — it shares the same alpha subunit as TSH — but its primary structural/functional homolog is LH. FSH shares the alpha subunit but its receptor is distinct and targets granulosa cells/Sertoli cells, not the corpus luteum. hCG has much the same molecular structure and function as LH, not ACTH. hCG has much the same molecular structure and function as LH, not prolactin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCG, LH, FSH, and TSH all share the same alpha subunit; they differ only in their beta subunits. Very high hCG (hydatidiform mole, choriocarcinoma) can cause hyperthyroidism via TSH receptor cross-activation. COMLEX: The hCG-LH structural homology is a classic mechanism question in CP3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — LH and hCG share nearly identical molecular structure and receptor binding; hCG acts as a surrogate LH signal to rescue the corpus luteum from involution and prevent menstruation in early pregnancy.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"B":"This prevents luteolysis and e","D":"hCG has much the same molecular structure and function as LH, not ACTH.","E":"hCG has much the same molecular structure and function as LH, not prolactin."}},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the functions of LH and FSH (including the LH surge, ovulation, and corpus luteum maintenance)","batch":1,"stem":"Human chorionic gonadotropin secretion follows a characteristic temporal pattern. Which of the following correctly describes when hCG peaks and what happens to its level for the remainder of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"hCG peaks at about 8 to 12 weeks of pregnancy and then falls to a lower plateau by 16 to 20 weeks, staying there for the rest of gestation","B":"hCG peaks at 6 to 8 weeks and then decreases sharply by the end of the first trimester to nearly undetectable levels by 16 weeks of pregnancy","C":"hCG rises steadily throughout all three trimesters, reaching its maximum concentration just before parturition as the placenta reaches peak mass","D":"hCG peaks at 20 weeks when placental mass is greatest and then decreases linearly toward term as placental estrogen takes over corpus luteum maintenance","E":"hCG rises in two distinct waves at 8 and 28 weeks, corresponding to first-trimester corpus luteum rescue and third-trimester fetal organ maturation"},"explanation":"hCG is first measurable in maternal blood 8 to 9 days after ovulation, rises rapidly, and peaks at about 8 to 12 weeks of pregnancy (First Aid 2025 gives 8–10 weeks; Guyton Ch 83 gives 10–12 weeks). It then falls to a lower value by 16 to 20 weeks and stays at that level for the rest of pregnancy (Guyton Ch 83). hCG falls to a lower plateau after its peak and remains measurable, not nearly undetectable, by 16 weeks; its peak is at 8 to 12 weeks (First Aid 8–10, Guyton 10–12), not 6 to 8. hCG does not rise steadily to term; it peaks in the first trimester and then holds at a lower level. hCG peaks in the first trimester, not at 20 weeks, and has already fallen to its plateau by 16 to 20 weeks. hCG has a single first-trimester peak, not two waves at 8 and 28 weeks.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The hCG curve (rises → peaks ~8–12 wks → plateaus) is frequently tested in relation to prenatal screening. Down syndrome has elevated hCG on second-trimester triple screen. COMLEX: hCG timing is a CP3 OB physiology essential; know the curve shape.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — LH and hCG share nearly identical molecular structure and receptor binding; hCG acts as a surrogate LH signal to rescue the corpus luteum from involution and prevent menstruation in early pregnancy.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"hCG falls to a lower plateau after","C":"hCG does not rise steadily to term","D":"hCG peaks in the first trimester, not","E":"hCG has a single first-trimester peak"}},{"id":"sdl36_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the functions of LH and FSH (including the LH surge, ovulation, and corpus luteum maintenance)","batch":2,"stem":"Daily blood samples are collected across a woman's menstrual cycle. Estradiol peaks late in the follicular phase and then begins to fall about 1 day before ovulation, while progesterone starts to rise before the follicle ruptures. Which action of the preovulatory gonadotropin surge best explains this hormonal switch?","choices":{"E":"LH acts on the granulosa and theca cells of the mature follicle, converting them mainly to progesterone-secreting cells before ovulation","A":"FSH acts on granulosa aromatase, shifting it from estradiol synthesis to progesterone synthesis as the follicle enlarges before rupture","B":"LH acts on the endometrium, converting its glands to secrete progesterone in preparation for implantation of a fertilized ovum","C":"Inhibin from the follicle blocks estradiol synthesis in theca cells, and the falling estradiol releases progesterone from storage granules","D":"Prolactin rises with the LH surge and switches the corpus luteum from estradiol to progesterone secretion before the follicle has ruptured"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 82) states that about 2 days before ovulation LH secretion rises 6- to 10-fold, peaking about 16 hours before ovulation, while FSH rises 2- to 3-fold. LH has a specific effect on the granulosa and theca cells, converting them mainly to progesterone-secreting cells; therefore estrogen secretion begins to fall about 1 day before ovulation while increasing amounts of progesterone begin to be secreted. LH also triggers proteolytic enzyme release from the theca externa and prostaglandin secretion, leading to follicle rupture, and after ovulation drives luteinization. FSH stimulates granulosa growth and aromatase, which converts theca androgens to estrogens; it does not switch the cells to progesterone. The endometrium responds to progesterone; it does not secrete it. Steroid hormones are not stored in granules, and inhibin acts on FSH secretion. Prolactin does not drive this switch, and the corpus luteum forms only after ovulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LH is necessary for final follicular growth, ovulation, luteinization, and corpus luteum secretion; the surge peaks about 16 hours before ovulation, and estrogen begins to fall about 1 day before ovulation as progesterone rises (Guyton Ch 82). COMLEX: hCG has nearly the same actions as LH and can stand in for the LH surge to trigger ovulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 4 — estrogen positive feedback produces the preovulatory LH surge","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"The endometrium responds to pr","C":"Steroid hormones are not store"}},{"id":"sdl40_n03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the functions of LH and FSH (including the LH surge, ovulation, and corpus luteum maintenance)","batch":1,"stem":"In the developing follicle, estrogen production requires cooperation between two cell types. Which of the following correctly describes this arrangement?","choices":{"A":"FSH drives theca cells to make estrogens, which LH-stimulated granulosa cells convert to androgens","B":"LH drives theca cells to make androgens, which FSH-stimulated granulosa aromatase converts to estrogens","C":"LH drives granulosa cells to make androgens, which theca cell aromatase converts to estrogens","D":"FSH drives granulosa cells to make cholesterol, which theca cells convert directly to estrogens","E":"LH drives theca cells to make progesterone, which the oocyte converts to estrogen by aromatase"},"explanation":"Under the control of LH, theca cells produce androgens, but they lack aromatase and cannot convert them to estrogens. The androgens diffuse into the adjacent granulosa cells, where aromatase, whose activity is stimulated by FSH, converts them to estrogens. Reversing the hormones or the cell types reverses this two-cell arrangement: theca cells do not have the aromatase step, and granulosa cells are where androgens are aromatized, not made. Ovarian steroids are synthesized mainly from cholesterol taken up from the blood, and the oocyte is not the site of aromatization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two-cell model: LH acts on theca cells (desmolase) to make androstenedione; FSH acts on granulosa cells (aromatase) to make estradiol. In PCOS, a high LH to FSH ratio drives theca androgen excess. COMLEX: Aromatase inhibitors such as anastrozole and letrozole block estrogen synthesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — LH acts on Leydig cells and FSH on Sertoli cells, paralleling theca and granulosa roles","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Reversing the hormones or the ","C":"Reversing the hormones or the "}},{"id":"sdl40_n04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the functions of LH and FSH (including the LH surge, ovulation, and corpus luteum maintenance)","batch":1,"stem":"A woman has normal FSH secretion during her cycle, but her midcycle LH rise is blunted. Which of the following best describes the consequence for her ovarian cycle?","choices":{"A":"The follicle does not rupture and no corpus luteum forms, so luteal progesterone stays low","B":"The follicle ruptures normally, but the corpus luteum secretes estrogen without progesterone","C":"The follicle ruptures early, because FSH alone is enough to break down the follicle wall","D":"Several follicles rupture together, because unopposed FSH removes the selection of one","E":"The follicle ruptures normally, but the endometrium cannot proliferate in the next cycle"},"explanation":"LH is necessary for final follicular growth and ovulation; without it, even large quantities of FSH cannot bring the follicle to ovulation. The LH surge makes the theca externa release proteolytic enzymes that weaken the follicle wall, stimulates new vessel growth and prostaglandin secretion, and converts granulosa and theca cells into progesterone-secreting cells. Without an adequate surge the cycle is anovulatory: no corpus luteum develops, there is almost no progesterone in the latter part of the cycle, and the cycle is shortened by several days. Luteinization itself depends on LH and on extrusion of the ovum, so a corpus luteum does not form normally without the surge. FSH cannot trigger rupture on its own. Selection of a single follicle depends on estrogen feedback, and endometrial proliferation in the next cycle depends on estrogen, not on the previous LH surge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The LH surge is a positive-feedback response to high midcycle estrogen and triggers ovulation; anovulatory cycles are normal near menarche and menopause. COMLEX: Absence of a luteal rise in progesterone or in basal body temperature indicates anovulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — The midcycle LH surge is the classic example of positive feedback","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"FSH cannot trigger rupture on ","D":"Selection of a single follicle"}},{"id":"sdl40_n14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the functions of LH and FSH (including the LH surge, ovulation, and corpus luteum maintenance)","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with anovulatory infertility caused by low gonadotropin secretion undergoes ovulation induction. After her follicles have grown in response to gonadotropin therapy, she receives a single injection of human chorionic gonadotropin. Which of the following best explains the purpose of this injection?","choices":{"A":"hCG has nearly the same actions as FSH, so it recruits a fresh group of primary follicles","B":"hCG has nearly the same actions as LH, so it stands in for the LH surge and triggers ovulation","C":"hCG suppresses GnRH release, so a premature LH surge cannot occur before the follicles mature","D":"hCG converts theca cell androgens to estrogens, raising estradiol to the level needed for growth","E":"hCG causes the endometrium to shed, so implantation can occur in a freshly proliferated lining"},"explanation":"Lack of ovulation caused by hyposecretion of pituitary gonadotropins can be treated with appropriately timed hCG, which, although secreted by the placenta, has almost the same effects as LH and is a powerful stimulator of ovulation; excessive use can cause ovulation from many follicles and multiple births. hCG acts like LH, not FSH, so it does not recruit a new group of follicles. It does not suppress GnRH; it replaces the missing LH signal directly at the ovary. Conversion of androgens to estrogens is carried out by granulosa cell aromatase under FSH stimulation. hCG maintains the corpus luteum and its progesterone, which preserves rather than sheds the endometrium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCG shares its alpha subunit with TSH, LH, and FSH and acts like LH; it maintains the corpus luteum, and thus progesterone, for the first 8 to 10 weeks of pregnancy. COMLEX: Ovulation induction carries a risk of multiple ovulation and multiple gestation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — LH, FSH, TSH, and hCG are glycoprotein hormones that share an alpha subunit","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"The placenta cannot synthesize estrogens de novo from basic substrates and relies entirely on androgenic precursors supplied by the adrenal glands. Which precursor molecules are used by placental syncytial trophoblast aromatase to produce estrogens?","choices":{"B":"Dehydroepiandrosterone and its 16-hydroxylated form, transported from fetal and maternal adrenal glands to the placenta for aromatization","A":"Aldosterone and cortisol, which are converted to estradiol by placental CYP enzymes acting on glucocorticoid ring structures","C":"Pregnenolone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone, transported from the corpus luteum to the placenta and converted to estriol by trophoblast enzymes","D":"Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone, aromatized to estrone within the placental decidual stromal cells lining the maternal surface","E":"Androstenedione secreted by the maternal ovary under residual LH stimulation, which alone supplies all placental estrogen precursors"},"explanation":"Placental estrogen synthesis is unique: the syncytial trophoblast cannot form estrogens de novo. Instead, it relies on androgenic precursors DHEA and 16-OH-DHEA from both the maternal adrenal glands and the fetal adrenal glands (whose cortices are very large with a \"fetal zone\" devoted mainly to DHEA secretion). These androgens are transported in the blood to the placenta and aromatized to estradiol, estrone, and estriol. By term, daily placental estrogen production is about 30 times the mother's normal level. Aldosterone and cortisol are not the precursors; the syncytial trophoblast aromatizes DHEA and 16-OH-DHEA from the maternal and fetal adrenal glands into estrogens. The placental estrogen precursors are the adrenal androgens DHEA and 16-OH-DHEA, not pregnenolone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone from the corpus luteum. The precursors are adrenal DHEA and 16-OH-DHEA, not testosterone and DHT, and aromatization happens in the syncytial trophoblast. The precursors come from both the maternal and fetal adrenal glands, mainly DHEA and 16-OH-DHEA, not from maternal ovarian androstenedione alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The fetal-placental unit for estrogen synthesis is a classic concept — the fetal adrenal \"fetal zone\" produces massive amounts of DHEA during pregnancy. COMLEX: Placental estrogen depends on fetal adrenal function; fetal adrenal insufficiency causes low maternal estriol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — The six anterior pituitary hormones include FSH and LH; their near-complete suppression by placental estrogens and progesterone during pregnancy is central to understanding which organ systems sustain gestation.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Aldosterone and cortisol are not the precursors; the syncyti","C":"The placental estrogen precursors are the adrenal androgens ","D":"The precursors are adrenal DHEA and 16-OH-DHEA, not testoste","E":"The precursors come from both the maternal and fetal adrenal"}},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"By the end of pregnancy, daily placental estrogen production increases to approximately how many times the mother's normal non-pregnant level of estrogen production?","choices":{"D":"Approximately 30 times the normal non-pregnant daily estrogen production rate, reflecting the massive synthetic capacity of the term placenta","A":"Approximately 3 to 5 times the normal non-pregnant daily estrogen production rate, reflecting modest placental contribution to the maternal estrogen pool","B":"Approximately 8 to 10 times the normal non-pregnant daily estrogen production rate, sufficient to maintain uterine growth and ductal breast development","C":"Approximately 15 to 20 times the normal non-pregnant daily estrogen production rate, driven by increasing placental mass in the third trimester","E":"More than 50 times the normal non-pregnant daily estrogen production rate, predominantly in the form of estriol secreted by syncytial trophoblast cells"},"explanation":"Toward the end of pregnancy, the placenta produces estrogens at a rate approximately 30 times the mother's normal non-pregnant level. This enormous output is made possible by the two-gland system: the fetal and maternal adrenal glands supply DHEA and 16-OH-DHEA precursors, which placental syncytial trophoblast aromatase converts to estradiol, estrone, and estriol. Estrogens promote uterine enlargement (50 to 1100 grams), breast ductal growth, and relaxation of pelvic ligaments. The 30-times figure from Guyton is the high-yield board number. By term the placenta makes estrogens at about 30 times the mother's normal non-pregnant level, far more than 3 to 5 times. By term the placenta makes estrogens at about 30 times the mother's normal non-pregnant level, not 15 to 20 times.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maternal serum estriol rises throughout pregnancy; a low estriol on the triple screen suggests fetal adrenal insufficiency or trisomy 18. COMLEX: The 30-times increase in placental estrogen by term is a quantitative fact tested in COMLEX CP3 OB physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — Placental estrogens are lipophilic steroids that act through intracellular receptors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"By term the placenta makes estrogens at about 30 times the mother's normal non-pregnant level, far m","C":"By term the placenta makes estrogens at about 30 times the mother's normal non-pregnant level, not 1"}},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"Progesterone secreted by the placenta during pregnancy has several essential effects on uterine function. Which of the following best describes the key protective role of placental progesterone in preventing premature expulsion of the fetus?","choices":{"C":"Progesterone decreases uterine contractility by suppressing myometrial excitability, preventing premature uterine contractions throughout the gestational period","A":"Progesterone stimulates myometrial gap junction formation to synchronize uterine contractions so that they occur only at the correct time near term parturition","B":"Progesterone promotes uterine estrogen receptor upregulation, preparing the myometrium to respond maximally to the oxytocin surge that occurs at term parturition","D":"Progesterone induces cervical ripening and effacement beginning in the second trimester to prepare the birth canal for eventual parturition at term","E":"Progesterone activates prostaglandin synthesis by the fetal amnion membranes, providing a coordinated uterotonic signal that is balanced against progesterone withdrawal at term"},"explanation":"Placental progesterone has four essential effects: (1) it causes decidual cells to develop in the endometrium for early embryo nutrition; (2) it decreases uterine contractility, preventing premature labor — this is its most critical protective effect against spontaneous abortion; (3) it increases fallopian tube and uterine secretions to nourish the morula and blastocyst before implantation; and (4) it helps prepare the breasts for lactation. Near term, the estrogen-to-progesterone ratio shifts upward, increasing myometrial excitability and facilitating labor onset. Progesterone decreases uterine contractility rather than synchronizing it; near term a rising estrogen-to-progesterone ratio increases myometrial excitability. Progesterone's protective role is to reduce uterine contractility; it is the shift toward a higher estrogen-to-progesterone ratio near term that increases myometrial excitability. Progesterone's listed effects are decidual cell development, reduced uterine contractility, increased tubal and uterine secretions, and breast preparation; it protects the fetus by reducing contractility, not by ripening the cervix. Progesterone reduces uterine contractility and is not described as activating amnion prostaglandin synthesis; its listed effects are decidual development, secretions, and breast preparation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Progesterone maintains pregnancy by inhibiting uterine contractions; progesterone withdrawal near term increases the estrogen/progesterone ratio, promoting labor. COMLEX: This is a key uterine physiology mechanism — progesterone is the \"quiet hormone\" of pregnancy, keeping the uterus relaxed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 4 — Oxytocin stimulates uterine contraction, while progesterone restrains myometrial excitability","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Progesterone decreases uterine contractility rather than syn","B":"Progesterone's protective role is to reduce uterine contract","D":"Progesterone's listed effects are decidual cell development,","E":"Progesterone reduces uterine contractility and is not descri"}},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"Relaxin is a polypeptide hormone secreted during pregnancy that affects connective tissue. Which of the following correctly identifies both the secretory sources of relaxin during pregnancy and its primary physiologic targets?","choices":{"E":"Relaxin is secreted by the corpus luteum and placental tissues; it is thought to act as a vasodilator, including in the kidneys, and has been claimed to soften the cervix at delivery","A":"Relaxin is secreted by the anterior pituitary and the decidua; it primarily acts on uterine smooth muscle to prevent premature myometrial contractions during gestation","B":"Relaxin is secreted by the fetal adrenal gland and the syncytial trophoblast; it primarily promotes breast lobulo-alveolar development alongside progesterone in late pregnancy","C":"Relaxin is secreted by the maternal liver and the chorionic villi; it primarily acts on cervical smooth muscle to ripen the cervix independently of prostaglandin activity","D":"Relaxin is secreted by the maternal thyroid gland and the decidual cells; it primarily relaxes the myometrial fundus and promotes placental diffusion capacity in late pregnancy"},"explanation":"Guyton Ch 83 states that relaxin is secreted by the corpus luteum of the ovary and by placental tissues, with secretion increased by hCG. Its classic effect, relaxation of the ligaments of the symphysis pubis, was shown in the estrous rat and guinea pig; Guyton calls this effect weak or possibly absent in pregnant women, where the estrogens probably relax the pelvic ligaments instead. Guyton adds that relaxin has been claimed to soften the cervix at delivery and is thought to act as a vasodilator, increasing blood flow in tissues including the kidneys and raising venous return and cardiac output; Costanzo Ch 10 likewise lists relaxin from the corpus luteum among the vasodilators of pregnancy and gives it a role in parturition. Relaxin is not secreted by the anterior pituitary or decidua, and neither source gives it a role in preventing myometrial contractions. Relaxin is not secreted by the fetal adrenal, and neither source gives it a role in breast lobulo-alveolar development. Relaxin is not secreted by the maternal liver, and Guyton reports cervical softening only as a claimed effect, not an independent primary action. Relaxin is not secreted by the maternal thyroid, and neither source describes relaxation of the myometrial fundus or a change in placental diffusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Relaxin comes from the corpus luteum and placenta (secretion increased by hCG); Guyton describes it as a vasodilator and a claimed cervical softener, with the pubic-symphysis effect weak or absent in women. COMLEX: pelvic joint laxity in pregnancy matters in OMM; Guyton attributes pelvic ligament relaxation mainly to estrogens.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — Polypeptide hormones such as relaxin act through cell-surface receptors","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Relaxin is not secreted by the anterior pituitary","B":"Relaxin is not secreted by the fetal adrenal","C":"Relaxin is not secreted by the maternal liver","D":"Relaxin is not secreted by the maternal thyroid"}},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"Human chorionic somatomammotropin begins placental secretion at approximately which week of pregnancy, and how does its secretion level change as the pregnancy progresses toward term?","choices":{"D":"hCS begins secretion at approximately week 5 of pregnancy and increases progressively throughout all of gestation in direct proportion to placental weight","A":"hCS begins secretion at approximately week 10 and then reaches a plateau level at week 20, remaining constant through the remainder of the third trimester","B":"hCS begins secretion at approximately week 8 and increases rapidly until week 16, then decreases sharply as the placenta reaches its final mature functional state","C":"hCS begins secretion at approximately week 12 and rises proportionally to fetal weight rather than placental weight as the fetus grows in the third trimester","E":"hCS begins secretion at approximately week 2 alongside hCG and follows the same temporal curve, peaking at 10 to 12 weeks and declining through the second trimester"},"explanation":"hCS is a protein hormone (MW ~22,000) that begins to be secreted by the placenta at about the fifth week of pregnancy. Its secretion increases progressively throughout the remainder of pregnancy in direct proportion to the weight of the placenta, reflecting the growth of syncytial trophoblast tissue. This means hCS levels are highest near term when the placenta is largest. This is in contrast to hCG, which peaks at 10–12 weeks then declines. Because hCS is secreted in quantities several times greater than all other pregnancy hormones combined, it is considered the dominant metabolic hormone of pregnancy. hCS starts at about week 5 and rises with placental weight to term; it does not plateau at week 20. hCS starts at about week 5 and keeps increasing with placental weight; the early peak followed by a decline is hCG's pattern. hCS secretion begins at about week 5 and tracks placental weight, not fetal weight.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCS (formerly called human placental lactogen or hPL) is proportional to placental mass and is used as a marker of placental function in some clinical settings. COMLEX: Knowing that hCS begins at week 5 and is proportional to placental weight distinguishes it from the hCG curve — a classic COMLEX timeline comparison.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone increases protein synthesis, stimulates fat mobilization, and decreases glucose utilization; hCS replicates these same three metabolic effects at approximately 1/100th the potency to divert maternal nutrients toward the fetus.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"hCS starts at about week 5 and rises with placental weight t","B":"hCS starts at about week 5 and keeps increasing with placent","C":"hCS secretion begins at about week 5 and tracks placental we"}},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"Human chorionic somatomammotropin exerts metabolic effects on the mother that parallel those of growth hormone. Which of the following best describes hCS's primary effect on maternal glucose metabolism during pregnancy?","choices":{"B":"hCS decreases maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization, creating a state of relative insulin resistance that leaves more glucose available for fetal growth","A":"hCS increases maternal insulin sensitivity and peripheral glucose uptake to ensure equal glucose sharing between maternal tissues and the fetus during gestation","C":"hCS stimulates maternal pancreatic beta-cell proliferation, increasing insulin secretion to compensate for the increased metabolic demands of fetal growth in late pregnancy","D":"hCS upregulates maternal hepatic glycogen synthase activity, storing excess maternal glucose in the liver as glycogen to buffer fetal glucose demand during fasting periods","E":"hCS activates maternal gluconeogenesis by directly stimulating PEPCK expression in pericentral hepatocytes to elevate fasting maternal blood glucose above the normal range"},"explanation":"hCS has three key metabolic effects analogous to growth hormone: (1) weak tissue protein anabolic effect (promoting protein synthesis); (2) enhanced maternal fat mobilization, releasing free fatty acids as an alternative energy source for the mother; and (3) decreased maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization — the insulin resistance effect. By making the mother insulin-resistant, hCS ensures that glucose is spared from maternal metabolism and diverted to the fetus, which uses glucose as its primary substrate for growth. This mechanism directly explains the predisposition to gestational diabetes mellitus. hCS decreases maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization (an anti-insulin effect); it does not increase insulin sensitivity. hCS's listed metabolic effects are weak protein anabolism, fat mobilization, and decreased insulin sensitivity; beta-cell proliferation is not among them. hCS lowers maternal glucose utilization to spare glucose for the fetus; promoting hepatic glycogen storage is not among its listed effects. hCS's listed effects are weak protein anabolism, fat mobilization, and decreased insulin sensitivity; direct PEPCK-driven gluconeogenesis is not among them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCS-induced insulin resistance is the physiologic basis of gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM). Pregnant women must increase insulin secretion to compensate. COMLEX: GDM is a CP3 condition directly linked to hCS metabolic action — connecting SDL 29 insulin resistance pathophysiology to pregnancy physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone increases protein synthesis, stimulates fat mobilization, and decreases glucose utilization; hCS replicates these same three metabolic effects at approximately 1/100th the potency to divert maternal nutrients toward the fetus.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"hCS decreases maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose utili","C":"hCS's listed metabolic effects are weak protein anabolism, f","D":"hCS lowers maternal glucose utilization to spare glucose for","E":"hCS's listed effects are weak protein anabolism, fat mobiliz"}},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"Although human chorionic somatomammotropin was originally named \"human placental lactogen\" based on its first described function in animal studies, which statement most accurately characterizes its role in human lactation?","choices":{"C":"hCS causes at least partial breast development and lactation in several animal species, but attempts to use it to promote lactation in humans have not succeeded","A":"hCS is the primary driver of milk secretion in humans throughout the lactation period, with prolactin serving only a minor supplementary role after parturition","B":"hCS initiates the colostrum phase of human lactation by suppressing dopamine production from the arcuate nucleus, thereby disinhibiting prolactin release postpartum","D":"hCS acts identically to prolactin in humans, binding the prolactin receptor with equivalent affinity to drive full lobuloalveolar milk secretion after delivery","E":"hCS suppresses milk secretion during pregnancy by competing with prolactin for mammary gland receptor binding sites until estrogen and progesterone fall postpartum"},"explanation":"hCS was originally named \"human placental lactogen\" because its first recognized function was causing partial breast development and sometimes lactation in animal studies. However, despite being secreted in larger quantities than all other pregnancy hormones combined, attempts to use hCS to promote lactation in humans have not been successful. The true driver of milk secretion in humans is prolactin. During pregnancy, despite rising prolactin levels, estrogen and progesterone from the placenta inhibit actual milk secretion. Once the placenta is delivered and these hormones fall, prolactin is able to stimulate milk production. hCS does not initiate lactation in humans; attempts to use it for lactation have failed, and prolactin drives milk secretion once placental estrogen and progesterone fall. hCS does not act like prolactin in humans; attempts to use it to promote lactation have not succeeded, and prolactin is the true driver of milk secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolactin promotes lactation; estrogen and progesterone inhibit milk secretion during pregnancy despite rising prolactin. After delivery, the fall of estrogen/progesterone \"unlocks\" prolactin-driven lactation. COMLEX: This inhibition-unlocking mechanism is a high-yield CP3 lactation physiology concept.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone increases protein synthesis, stimulates fat mobilization, and decreases glucose utilization; hCS replicates these same three metabolic effects at approximately 1/100th the potency to divert maternal nutrients toward the fetus.","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The true driver of milk secret","B":"hCS does not initiate lactation in humans; attempts to use i","D":"hCS does not act like prolactin in humans; attempts to use i"}},{"id":"sdl36_q15","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":2,"stem":"After implantation, endometrial stromal cells around the blastocyst swell and become packed with stored nutrients. Trophoblast cells invade and digest these cells, and during the first week after implantation this is the embryo's main source of nutrition. Which hormone causes these endometrial cells to develop?","choices":{"D":"Progesterone, which causes decidual cells to develop in the endometrium and is secreted by the corpus luteum under hCG stimulation","A":"Estrogen, which causes these cells to develop by proliferating endometrial stroma and is produced mainly by the fetal adrenal cortex","B":"Human chorionic somatomammotropin, which converts endometrial stroma into nutrient stores by mobilizing maternal fatty acids","C":"Relaxin, which converts endometrial stroma into nutrient stores while it softens the cervix and relaxes the pelvic ligaments","E":"Prolactin, which converts endometrial stroma into nutrient stores in the same way that it stimulates milk synthesis in the breast"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 83) states that continued secretion of progesterone after implantation causes the endometrial cells to swell further and store even more nutrients; these cells are called decidual cells, and the total mass is the decidua. As trophoblast cells invade and digest the decidua, its stored nutrients are used by the embryo; during the first week after implantation this is the only means of nutrition, and it continues to supply some nutrition for up to 8 weeks. Under hCG, the corpus luteum grows to about twice its initial size, and its estrogen and progesterone maintain the decidual nature of the endometrium. Estrogen causes uterine enlargement and endometrial proliferation, and placental estrogen is made from adrenal androgen precursors rather than by the fetal adrenal itself. Human chorionic somatomammotropin mobilizes maternal fatty acids and reduces maternal insulin sensitivity. Relaxin softens the cervix and relaxes pelvic ligaments. Prolactin acts on the breast.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Progesterone in pregnancy (Guyton Ch 83): decidual cells that nourish the early embryo, decreased uterine contractility, increased secretions of the fallopian tubes and uterus, and breast preparation. hCG keeps the corpus luteum secreting until the placenta takes over. COMLEX: Removing the corpus luteum before about week 7 almost always causes spontaneous abortion (Guyton Ch 83).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 → Obj 5 — monthly endometrial cycle and the progesterone-driven secretory phase","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl36_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman at 36 weeks of gestation attends a prenatal education class. During a discussion of progesterone's roles in pregnancy, the instructor highlights an often-overlooked function that occurs even before implantation. Which of the following best describes how progesterone supports the very earliest stages of embryo development before placentation is established?","choices":{"C":"Progesterone increases secretory activity of the fallopian tube and uterine glands, providing nutritive fluid that nourishes the developing morula and blastocyst before implantation occurs","A":"Progesterone stimulates hepatic glycogen synthase in the embryo to build fetal glycogen stores, which the blastocyst uses as its primary energy source during the morula stage of development","B":"Progesterone activates trophoblast cell proteolytic enzymes that digest endometrial decidual cells to directly release stored nutrients for trophoblast absorption during implantation","D":"Progesterone stimulates the corpus luteum to secrete IGF-1, which acts on the endometrium to release stored glycogen through a paracrine mechanism available to the trophoblast","E":"Progesterone upregulates maternal intestinal nutrient absorption, increasing plasma glucose and amino acids that passively diffuse into the uterine cavity to reach the pre-implantation embryo"},"explanation":"One of progesterone's four essential pregnancy effects is that it specifically increases secretions of the fallopian tube and uterus. This provides appropriate nutrition for the developing morula (the spherical mass of 16–32 blastomeres) and blastocyst before implantation takes place and before trophoblastic nutrition begins. This function requires progesterone from the corpus luteum (since the placenta hasn't formed yet). The other three key progesterone effects are: (1) decidual cell development for embryo nutrition; (2) decreased uterine contractility preventing premature labor; and (3) preparing the breasts for lactation alongside estrogens. Before implantation the embryo is nourished by progesterone-stimulated secretions of the fallopian tube and uterus, not by embryonic hepatic glycogen synthesis. Trophoblastic nutrition begins only after implantation; progesterone's pre-implantation effect is increased tubal and uterine secretions, not activating trophoblast proteolytic enzymes. Progesterone nourishes the pre-implantation embryo by increasing fallopian tube and uterine secretions, not by raising maternal intestinal nutrient absorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Progesterone supports the morula/blastocyst by increasing fallopian tube and uterine secretions — an often-tested \"pre-implantation\" role. The four effects of progesterone in pregnancy are a classic Guyton list to memorize for boards. COMLEX: Progesterone deficiency in the luteal phase is a cause of implantation failure and early pregnancy loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 2 — Tubal secretions and capacitation in the female tract prepare for fertilization","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Before implantation the embryo is nourished by progesterone-","B":"Trophoblastic nutrition begins only after implantation; prog","E":"Progesterone nourishes the pre-implantation embryo by increa"}},{"id":"sdl36_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":2,"stem":"A medical student studying hCS notes that it has weak growth hormone-like anabolic effects but is 100 times less potent than GH for promoting growth. She is trying to understand the physiologic purpose of this metabolic activity during pregnancy. Which of the following best explains the combined anabolic and lipolytic functions of hCS in the context of fetal nutrition?","choices":{"B":"hCS promotes maternal tissue protein synthesis and simultaneously mobilizes free fatty acids from maternal fat stores, providing the mother with alternative energy while directing glucose toward the fetus","A":"hCS suppresses maternal IGF-1 synthesis by competing with GH for somatostatin receptor binding in the hypothalamus, thereby preventing maternal bone overgrowth during pregnancy","C":"hCS acts on maternal hypothalamic neurons to downregulate GH receptor expression, creating peripheral GH resistance that conserves maternal glucose stores for postpartum milk production","D":"hCS binds maternal insulin receptors in the arcuate nucleus to suppress appetite via a GH-mimetic mechanism, ensuring excess maternal glucose does not cause fetal macrosomia","E":"hCS stimulates synthesis of IGF-2 in placental trophoblast cells, which promotes spiral artery remodeling by activating matrix metalloproteinases in the uterine decidual stroma"},"explanation":"hCS has three interrelated metabolic functions analogous to growth hormone: (1) weak anabolic effect — promotes tissue protein synthesis in maternal tissues at 1/100th GH potency; (2) lipolytic effect — promotes free fatty acid mobilization from maternal fat stores, providing an alternative energy substrate for the mother's tissues so she does not need to burn glucose; and (3) anti-insulin effect — decreases insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization in maternal tissues, preserving glucose for fetal nutrition. Together, these effects constitute a \"maternal metabolic adaptation\" that nutritionally prioritizes the fetus by shifting maternal metabolism toward fat oxidation and protein anabolism while sparing glucose. hCS has weak GH-like anabolic, lipolytic, and anti-insulin effects; it is not described as suppressing IGF-1 or competing with GH at hypothalamic somatostatin receptors. hCS produces insulin resistance in maternal tissues to spare glucose for the fetus; it is not described as inducing GH resistance for postpartum milk production. hCS decreases insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization in maternal tissues; it is not described as an appetite suppressant acting on arcuate insulin receptors. hCS's described functions are weak protein anabolism, free fatty acid mobilization, and reduced insulin sensitivity; stimulating placental IGF-2 and spiral artery remodeling is not among them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCS = weak GH analog; its three effects are protein synthesis, fat mobilization, and insulin resistance. This triad directly explains why pregnant women shift to fat burning to spare glucose for the fetus. COMLEX: The metabolic adaptations of pregnancy — including hCS-driven lipolysis — are CP3 physiologic concepts in the COMLEX blueprint.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — Growth hormone metabolic effects: protein synthesis, fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"hCS has weak GH-like anabolic, lipolytic, and anti-insulin e","C":"hCS produces insulin resistance in maternal tissues to spare","D":"hCS decreases insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization in","E":"hCS's described functions are weak protein anabolism, free f"}},{"id":"sdl36_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman at 34 weeks of gestation asks her obstetrician why she is not yet producing milk despite being told her breasts are \"fully prepared.\" The obstetrician explains that a placental hormone discovered initially for its lactogenic properties in animals does not actually promote lactation in humans, and that milk secretion is actively suppressed until delivery. Which of the following correctly identifies this hormone and explains the mechanism of lactation suppression during pregnancy?","choices":{"E":"Human chorionic somatomammotropin; despite its original name and lactogenic animal data, active milk secretion is suppressed because simultaneously elevated placental estrogen and progesterone inhibit actual milk secretion at the mammary alveolar level","A":"Human chorionic gonadotropin; milk secretion is suppressed because hCG at peak levels competes with prolactin for the mammary gland prolactin receptor, blocking milk secretion until hCG levels decline postpartum","B":"Placental estrogen; milk secretion is suppressed because estrogen inhibits dopamine release from the arcuate nucleus and dopamine inhibition then paradoxically prevents prolactin from binding its receptor effectively","C":"Human chorionic somatomammotropin; milk secretion is suppressed because high progesterone levels block hCS receptor upregulation on mammary alveolar cells throughout pregnancy until parturition triggers receptor expression","D":"Placental progesterone; milk secretion is suppressed because progesterone specifically downregulates oxytocin receptor expression on myoepithelial cells, preventing milk ejection until parturition removes progesterone inhibition"},"explanation":"hCS was originally named \"human placental lactogen\" because early animal experiments showed it caused partial breast development and sometimes lactation. However, attempts to use hCS to promote lactation in humans have consistently failed. Despite hCS being secreted in larger quantities than all other pregnancy hormones combined, actual milk secretion is suppressed during pregnancy because of the simultaneously elevated levels of estrogen and progesterone from the placenta. These hormones inhibit milk secretion at the mammary alveolar level even in the presence of rising prolactin (which does cause breast development). After delivery, when the placenta is expelled and estrogen/progesterone levels plummet, prolactin can then stimulate active milk secretion within 1–7 days. hCG does not block milk secretion; it does not compete with prolactin, and the suppression comes from the high placental estrogen and progesterone acting at the mammary alveoli. Estrogen and progesterone both inhibit milk secretion while prolactin rises through pregnancy; Guyton gives no dopamine mechanism, and the hormone asked about is hCS. Milk secretion is inhibited by high estrogen and progesterone at the alveolar level, not by progesterone blocking hCS receptor expression. The hormone named is hCS, and suppression is by elevated placental estrogen and progesterone at the alveolar level, not oxytocin receptor downregulation on myoepithelial cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: During pregnancy, estrogen and progesterone INHIBIT milk secretion despite rising prolactin. After delivery, the fall of placental estrogen/progesterone removes this inhibition and prolactin-driven milk production begins. COMLEX: The pharmacologic corollary is that bromocriptine (a dopamine agonist suppressing prolactin) was historically used to suppress postpartum lactation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — Growth hormone metabolic effects: protein synthesis, fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"hCG does not block milk secretion; it does not compete with ","B":"Estrogen and progesterone both inhibit milk secretion while ","C":"Milk secretion is inhibited by high estrogen and progesteron","D":"The hormone named is hCS, and suppression is by elevated pla"}},{"id":"sdl40_n05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"A woman whose ovaries never functioned and who never received estrogen replacement is several inches taller than her sisters. Which effect of estrogen best explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen inhibits growth hormone secretion, so its absence prolongs pituitary growth hormone release","B":"Estrogen stimulates osteoclasts at the growth plates, so its absence lets cartilage keep thickening","C":"Estrogen blocks IGF-1 receptors on chondrocytes, so its absence speeds growth of the cartilage","D":"Estrogen causes the epiphyses to unite with the shafts of long bones, which ends linear growth","E":"Estrogen increases urinary calcium loss, so its absence leaves more calcium for bone lengthening"},"explanation":"At puberty estrogen speeds growth in height for several years but also causes the epiphyses to unite with the shafts of the long bones, an effect stronger in females than the similar effect of testosterone in males. A female eunuch who lacks estrogen usually grows several inches taller than a normal woman because her epiphyses do not unite at the normal time. Her extra height reflects delayed epiphyseal fusion, not increased growth hormone or IGF-1 action. Estrogen inhibits osteoclastic activity rather than stimulating it. Estrogen loss after menopause reduces bone calcium deposition and contributes to osteoporosis, so estrogen deficiency does not leave extra calcium for growth.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen closes epiphyseal plates in both sexes (in males through aromatized testosterone); estrogen deficiency delays closure and produces eunuchoid proportions, as in Klinefelter syndrome. COMLEX: Precocious puberty causes premature epiphyseal closure and short adult stature.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — Growth hormone drives epiphyseal cartilage growth until the plates fuse","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Her extra height reflects dela","B":"Estrogen inhibits osteoclastic","C":"Her extra height reflects dela"}},{"id":"sdl40_n06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly matches each hormone with its main contribution to breast development?","choices":{"A":"Progesterone grows the ducts, stroma, and fat; estrogen develops the lobules and alveoli","B":"Estrogen grows the ducts, stroma, and fat; progesterone develops the lobules and alveoli","C":"Estrogen grows the ducts, stroma, and fat; progesterone causes the alveoli to secrete milk","D":"Progesterone grows the ducts and alveoli; estrogen causes the alveoli to secrete milk","E":"Estrogen develops the lobules and alveoli; prolactin grows the ducts, stroma, and fat"},"explanation":"Estrogens cause development of the stromal tissue of the breasts, growth of an extensive duct system, and deposition of fat; they develop the lobules and alveoli only slightly. Progesterone develops the lobules and alveoli, making the alveolar cells proliferate, enlarge, and become secretory. Progesterone does not make the alveoli secrete milk; milk is secreted only after the prepared breast is further stimulated by prolactin. Swapping estrogen and progesterone reverses their roles. Assigning milk secretion to progesterone or to estrogen ignores the requirement for prolactin. Prolactin is needed for milk production, not for growth of the ducts, stroma, and fat.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prolactin induces and maintains lactation; after delivery the fall in estrogen and progesterone disinhibits prolactin and lactation begins. COMLEX: Premenstrual breast swelling reflects progesterone-driven lobular development and increased tissue fluid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 4 — Oxytocin ejects milk from alveoli prepared by estrogen, progesterone, and prolactin","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Swapping estrogen and progeste","C":"Assigning milk secretion to pr","E":"Prolactin is needed for milk p"}},{"id":"sdl40_n15","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the functions of estrogen and progesterone (including effects on bone, endometrium, breast, and secondary sex characteristics)","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman reached menopause at age 49 and never took hormone therapy. She develops acute back pain after lifting a bag of groceries, and radiographs show a vertebral compression fracture. DXA shows a T-score of -3.0. Serum calcium, phosphate, PTH, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D are normal. Which of the following best explains her bone loss?","choices":{"A":"Loss of estrogen lowers PTH secretion, so bone can no longer be remodeled after microfractures","B":"Loss of progesterone prevents vitamin D activation, so new osteoid fails to mineralize normally","C":"Rising FSH causes urinary calcium wasting, which depletes the mineral needed for new bone matrix","D":"Loss of estrogen raises intestinal calcium absorption, which suppresses bone formation by feedback","E":"Loss of estrogen removes its restraint on osteoclasts, so resorption rises and bone matrix falls"},"explanation":"Estrogens inhibit osteoclastic activity, in part by stimulating osteoprotegerin, and they decrease the number and activity of osteoclasts and reduce osteoblast apoptosis. After menopause, estrogen deficiency increases osteoclastic activity, decreases bone matrix, and decreases deposition of bone calcium and phosphate; in some women this leads to osteoporosis and vertebral fractures. Her normal calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D argue against osteomalacia or hyperparathyroidism. PTH is not low here, and PTH is not required for remodeling microfractures in this way. Vitamin D activation depends on PTH and the kidney, not on progesterone, and her vitamin D is normal. Urinary calcium wasting from FSH is not a mechanism of postmenopausal bone loss. Estrogen loss does not raise calcium absorption or suppress bone formation by such a feedback.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postmenopausal osteoporosis: increased osteoclast activity from estrogen loss; vertebral compression fractures with acute back pain, loss of height, and kyphosis; treatment includes bisphosphonates, teriparatide, SERMs, and denosumab, an anti-RANKL antibody. COMLEX: Weight-bearing exercise with adequate calcium and vitamin D intake throughout adulthood helps prevent osteoporosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Estrogen raises osteoprotegerin, limiting RANKL-driven osteoclast formation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl36_q20","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis including positive and negative feedback mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman at 28 weeks of gestation undergoes routine prenatal labs. Her FSH and LH are undetectable, her prolactin is elevated 10-fold above the non-pregnant normal, and her anterior pituitary is 50% larger than normal by MRI. Which of the following best explains why FSH and LH are suppressed despite elevated prolactin and an enlarged pituitary?","choices":{"A":"High levels of placental estrogens and progesterone suppress pulsatile GnRH release and pituitary responsiveness, almost completely inhibiting FSH and LH secretion throughout pregnancy","B":"High hCG levels directly suppress FSH and LH release from the anterior pituitary by binding a shared glycoprotein hormone receptor that mediates gonadotropin negative feedback","C":"Elevated placental hCS acts on the arcuate nucleus to upregulate dopamine, which suppresses pulsatile GnRH and secondarily inhibits both FSH and LH secretion from gonadotrophs","D":"Elevated fetal cortisol crosses the placenta and suppresses maternal CRH secretion, leading to reduced ACTH and a secondary downstream decrease in pituitary gonadotropin secretion","E":"Placental relaxin directly inhibits GnRH pulse generation in the hypothalamic arcuate nucleus, leading to complete absent FSH and LH secretion from pituitary gonadotrophs during pregnancy"},"explanation":"During pregnancy, extremely high levels of estrogens and progesterone from the placenta almost totally suppress FSH and LH secretion from the anterior pituitary. This is the same negative feedback mechanism that operates during the normal menstrual cycle, but amplified enormously by placental steroid output. Simultaneously, the anterior pituitary enlarges by at least 50% during pregnancy, increasing secretion of ACTH, thyrotropin, and especially prolactin (which rises 10–20-fold by term). Prolactin increases are driven by estrogen stimulating lactotroph proliferation. Despite this enlargement, FSH and LH remain suppressed due to the powerful inhibitory effects of placental estrogens and progesterone. FSH and LH are suppressed by placental estrogens and progesterone through negative feedback; hCG is not described as suppressing gonadotropins. The suppression comes from placental estrogens and progesterone, not from hCS driving dopamine in the arcuate nucleus. FSH and LH are almost totally suppressed by placental estrogens and progesterone, not by fetal cortisol acting through CRH and ACTH. The suppression is due to placental estrogens and progesterone, the same negative feedback as in the menstrual cycle but amplified; relaxin is not described as inhibiting GnRH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FSH and LH are suppressed throughout pregnancy by placental estrogens and progesterone — this is why ovulation does not occur during pregnancy. Prolactin rises 10–20-fold by term due to estrogen-driven lactotroph growth. COMLEX: The enlarged pituitary of pregnancy is a normal finding; hyperprolactinemia during pregnancy is expected.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — GnRH drives pituitary LH and FSH, and sex-steroid feedback suppresses both","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"FSH and LH are suppressed by placental estrogens and progest","C":"The suppression comes from placental estrogens and progester","D":"FSH and LH are almost totally suppressed by placental estrog","E":"The suppression is due to placental estrogens and progestero"}},{"id":"sdl40_n07","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis including positive and negative feedback mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Near the end of the luteal phase, FSH begins to rise about the time menstruation starts and initiates growth of the next group of follicles. Which change most directly permits this FSH rise?","choices":{"A":"A new dominant follicle begins secreting inhibin, which stimulates FSH release by the pituitary","B":"Prostaglandins released during menstruation travel to the pituitary and stimulate FSH release","C":"Involution of the corpus luteum ends its secretion of estrogen, progesterone, and inhibin","D":"The corpus luteum secretes rising amounts of activin, which overrides estrogen feedback on FSH","E":"Rising progesterone switches estrogen feedback from negative to positive and causes an FSH surge"},"explanation":"During the luteal phase the corpus luteum secretes progesterone, estrogen, and inhibin, which together suppress FSH and LH to their lowest levels about 3 to 4 days before menstruation; inhibin specifically inhibits FSH and, to a lesser extent, LH. Two to 3 days before menstruation the corpus luteum has almost completely involuted, so these hormones fall to a low ebb and release the hypothalamus and pituitary from negative feedback; a day or so later FSH rises as much as 2-fold and starts new follicle growth. Inhibin inhibits rather than stimulates FSH. Menstrual prostaglandins act locally on endometrial vessels and the uterus. The corpus luteum is involuting, so its secretions are falling, not rising. The positive feedback surge occurs at the end of the follicular phase, not at the end of the luteal phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhibin, from granulosa and Sertoli cells, selectively inhibits FSH; activin stimulates FSH. COMLEX: In Klinefelter syndrome, dysgenesis of the seminiferous tubules lowers inhibin B and raises FSH, the male counterpart of this feedback.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 7 — Sertoli cell inhibin provides the same selective FSH feedback in males","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Inhibin inhibits rather than s","D":"The corpus luteum is involutin"}},{"id":"sdl40_n08","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis including positive and negative feedback mechanisms","batch":1,"stem":"Combined oral contraceptives contain a synthetic estrogen and a synthetic progestin taken from early in the cycle through the time ovulation would normally occur. Which of the following best explains how they prevent ovulation?","choices":{"A":"The progestin triggers the LH surge early, so the ovum is released before the follicle matures","B":"The estrogen destroys the primordial follicles, so no oocytes remain available that month","C":"The progestin blocks aromatase in granulosa cells, so the follicles cannot make estradiol","D":"Steady estrogen and progestin feedback prevents the preovulatory LH surge, so ovulation fails","E":"The estrogen raises FSH secretion, so many follicles ovulate and none is fertilized normally"},"explanation":"Appropriate amounts of estrogen or progesterone given during the first half of the cycle inhibit ovulation by preventing the preovulatory LH surge, which is essential for ovulation; when progesterone is present, the inhibitory feedback of estrogen on LH and FSH is multiplied. Synthetic hormones are used because natural estrogen and progesterone are almost entirely destroyed by the liver after oral absorption. The progestin does not trigger an early surge; it suppresses the surge. Estrogen does not destroy primordial follicles. Aromatase inhibition is the mechanism of drugs such as anastrozole and letrozole, not of contraceptive progestins. Estrogen suppresses rather than raises FSH, so it does not cause multiple ovulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Combined hormonal contraceptives suppress LH and FSH; progestins also thicken cervical mucus, which inhibits sperm entry. COMLEX: Oral contraceptives use synthetic hormones such as ethinyl estradiol because natural sex steroids are destroyed on first pass through the liver.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — Negative feedback normally dominates estrogen and progesterone effects on gonadotropins","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"C":"Aromatase inhibition is the me"}},{"id":"sdl40_n16","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis including positive and negative feedback mechanisms","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with severe endometriosis starts leuprolide given as a continuous depot injection. Her pelvic pain worsens briefly during the first 2 weeks, and over the next 2 months she develops amenorrhea and hot flashes. Serum LH, FSH, and estradiol are all low. Which of the following best explains this hormonal pattern?","choices":{"A":"Continuous GnRH receptor stimulation downregulates gonadotrophs, so LH, FSH, and estrogen fall","B":"Continuous GnRH receptor stimulation raises FSH selectively, so follicles mature but do not ovulate","C":"Leuprolide blocks aromatase in granulosa cells, so estrogen falls while LH and FSH stay elevated","D":"Leuprolide destroys the remaining primordial follicles, producing an early natural menopause","E":"Leuprolide binds estrogen receptors in the hypothalamus and mimics estrogen negative feedback"},"explanation":"GnRH must be secreted in pulses; when GnRH is available continuously rather than in pulses, its ability to release LH and FSH is lost. Leuprolide is a GnRH analog: given continuously, it first acts as an agonist, which explains the brief flare, and then downregulates pituitary GnRH receptors, so FSH, LH, and estrogen fall. This hypoestrogenic state is why it is used for endometriosis and why it causes hot flashes and amenorrhea. A selective FSH rise would not lower all three hormones. Aromatase inhibitors lower estrogen but leave gonadotropins high. Destruction of follicles would produce the menopausal pattern with markedly high FSH, not low FSH. Leuprolide acts on GnRH receptors, not estrogen receptors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GnRH analogs (leuprolide, goserelin, nafarelin, histrelin): pulsatile use stimulates LH and FSH (infertility); continuous use suppresses them (prostate cancer, endometriosis, uterine fibroids, precocious puberty). Degarelix is an antagonist with no flare. COMLEX: GnRH agonists are a cause of drug-induced osteoporosis from prolonged hypoestrogenism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — Hypothalamic releasing hormones must reach the anterior pituitary in the proper pattern to drive secretion","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"A selective FSH rise would not","C":"Aromatase inhibitors lower est","E":"Leuprolide acts on GnRH recept"}},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the monthly endometrial cycle and menstruation","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman (G1P0) at 6 weeks of gestation presents for her first prenatal visit. Ultrasound reveals a single viable intrauterine pregnancy. She has not had a menstrual period since conception and asks how this is possible. Which of the following best explains the hormonal mechanism preventing menstruation at this gestational age?","choices":{"A":"hCG from the syncytial trophoblast maintains the corpus luteum, which continues secreting estrogen and progesterone to preserve the decidualized endometrium and prevent shedding","B":"Elevated estrogen secreted directly by the placental syncytiotrophoblast suppresses endometrial prostaglandin synthesis, blocking the shedding cascade at the level of the endometrial glands","C":"Elevated progesterone from the anterior pituitary gland suppresses FSH and LH secretion, preventing luteolysis and maintaining a false-positive luteal phase throughout early pregnancy","D":"hCG binds FSH receptors on the dominant follicle, preventing follicular atresia and maintaining continuous estrogen production from the residual follicular cells of the ovary","E":"Placental progesterone activates uterine natural killer cells that remodel the spiral arteries, reinforcing the endometrial lining against shedding from mechanical forces"},"explanation":"At 6 weeks of gestation, the trophoblast-derived hCG is the critical signal that prevents menstruation. hCG binds LH receptors on the corpus luteum, preventing its normal involution at day 14. The stimulated corpus luteum continues secreting estrogen and progesterone, which maintain the decidualized endometrium and prevent it from shedding. Without this hCG signal, luteolysis would occur, progesterone would fall, and menstruation would ensue. The anterior pituitary actually has suppressed FSH and LH during pregnancy; the pituitary is not the progesterone source at 6 weeks. Placental estrogen does not prevent shedding by blocking endometrial prostaglandins; hCG keeps the corpus luteum secreting estrogen and progesterone, which maintain the decidualized endometrium. hCG acts like LH on the corpus luteum, keeping it secreting estrogen and progesterone; it does not act on FSH receptors of a dominant follicle. At 6 weeks menstruation is prevented by the hCG-maintained corpus luteum's steroids; placental progesterone activating natural killer cells for spiral artery remodeling is not the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCG is detectable by 8–9 days post-ovulation; its rise prevents menstruation by rescuing the corpus luteum — this is the molecular basis of positive pregnancy tests. COMLEX: \"hCG rescues the corpus luteum\" is a signature CP3 reproductive physiology phrase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — Anterior pituitary hormones and their target organs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Placental estrogen does not prevent shedding by blocking end","C":"The anterior pituitary actuall","D":"hCG acts like LH on the corpus luteum, keeping it secreting ","E":"At 6 weeks menstruation is prevented by the hCG-maintained c"}},{"id":"sdl40_n09","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the monthly endometrial cycle and menstruation","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the uterine endometrium during the proliferative phase of the cycle?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen drives stromal and gland growth to about 3 to 5 mm, with thin, stringy cervical mucus","B":"Progesterone drives tortuous secretory glands to about 5 to 6 mm, with thick, sticky cervical mucus","C":"Estrogen drives glycogen storage in stromal cells to about 10 mm, with no cervical mucus secretion","D":"Progesterone drives reepithelialization to about 1 mm, with thin, watery cervical mucus present","E":"Inhibin drives spiral artery growth to about 3 mm, with no growth of the endometrial glands"},"explanation":"In the proliferative, or estrogen, phase before ovulation, estrogen secreted by the growing follicles makes the stromal and epithelial cells proliferate rapidly; the surface is reepithelialized within 4 to 7 days after menstruation begins, and the endometrium thickens to about 3 to 5 mm by ovulation. The cervical glands secrete thin, stringy mucus that aligns along the cervical canal and helps guide sperm into the uterus. Tortuous secretory glands with a thickness of 5 to 6 mm describe the secretory phase after ovulation, when progesterone also thickens the cervical mucus. Large lipid and glycogen deposits in stromal cells are a secretory-phase change driven by progesterone. Reepithelialization occurs under estrogen, not progesterone. Inhibin acts on the pituitary to suppress FSH, not on the endometrium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proliferative phase: estrogen, straight narrow glands. Secretory phase: progesterone, tortuous dilated glands and spiral artery development. COMLEX: Unopposed estrogen from repeated anovulatory cycles, as in PCOS, raises the risk of endometrial hyperplasia and cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — Estrogen-dependent cervical mucus guides sperm toward the uterus","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"D":"Reepithelialization occurs und","E":"Inhibin acts on the pituitary "}},{"id":"sdl40_n10","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the monthly endometrial cycle and menstruation","batch":1,"stem":"Normal menstrual fluid usually does not clot. Which of the following best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Estrogen withdrawal stops hepatic synthesis of clotting factors in the day before menses","B":"Menstrual blood lacks fibrinogen because the endometrium filters it out of the plasma","C":"Leukocytes released during menstruation digest the clotting factors in the uterine cavity","D":"Fibrinolysin is released with the necrotic endometrial tissue and dissolves the fibrin","E":"Progesterone withdrawal directly inactivates thrombin within the endometrial blood vessels"},"explanation":"Menstrual fluid is normally nonclotting because a fibrinolysin is released along with the necrotic endometrial material. If bleeding from the uterine surface is heavy, the fibrinolysin may be insufficient and clots pass; excessive bleeding with large clots can be clinical evidence of uterine disease. Hepatic clotting factor synthesis does not stop within a day of estrogen withdrawal. The endometrium does not filter fibrinogen from the blood. The many leukocytes released during menstruation make the uterus highly resistant to infection; they are not the reason the fluid stays liquid. Progesterone withdrawal triggers involution and vasospasm of endometrial vessels but does not inactivate thrombin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menstruation follows withdrawal of estrogen and especially progesterone when the corpus luteum involutes; about 40 mL of blood and 35 mL of serous fluid are lost. COMLEX: Classify abnormal uterine bleeding with PALM-COEIN (structural: polyp, adenomyosis, leiomyoma, malignancy; nonstructural: coagulopathy, ovulatory, endometrial, iatrogenic, not yet classified).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 4 — Oxytocin stimulates myometrial contraction, as menstrual prostaglandins do","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl40_n17","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the monthly endometrial cycle and menstruation","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman being evaluated for infertility has regular 28-day cycles. An endometrial biopsy on cycle day 22 shows tortuous glands filled with secretions, stromal cells rich in glycogen and lipid, and coiled blood vessels. The endometrium measures 6 mm. Which of the following best interprets this finding?","choices":{"A":"Unopposed estrogen is acting, which shows that this is an anovulatory cycle driven by estrogen alone","B":"Falling progesterone is acting, which shows that menstruation already began in this cycle","C":"Progesterone from a corpus luteum is acting, which shows that ovulation occurred this cycle","D":"FSH is acting on the endometrium directly, which shows that a dominant follicle is growing","E":"hCG is acting on the endometrium directly, which shows that implantation has taken place"},"explanation":"After ovulation, the corpus luteum secretes large amounts of progesterone and estrogen; progesterone causes marked swelling and secretory development of the endometrium, with tortuous glands, accumulation of secretory substances, large lipid and glycogen deposits in stromal cells, and highly tortuous blood vessels, reaching about 5 to 6 mm about 1 week after ovulation. Finding this secretory pattern on day 22 means a corpus luteum formed, so she ovulated. An anovulatory cycle produces no corpus luteum and almost no progesterone, so the endometrium would not show secretory change. Falling progesterone causes involution and menstruation, not a thick secretory lining. FSH acts on the ovary, not on the endometrium. hCG maintains the corpus luteum; its effect on the endometrium is indirect, through progesterone, and a day-22 secretory pattern is expected in any ovulatory cycle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secretory endometrium with tortuous glands reflects progesterone and is evidence of ovulation; a proliferative pattern late in the cycle suggests anovulation. COMLEX: Other markers of ovulation include a midluteal rise in serum progesterone, a rise in urinary pregnanediol, and a rise in basal body temperature.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — Progesterone acts on intracellular receptors to change endometrial gene expression","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"An anovulatory cycle produces ","D":"FSH acts on the ovary, not on ","E":"FSH acts on the ovary, not on "}},{"id":"sdl40_n11","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret laboratory findings to differentiate primary vs secondary ovarian dysfunction (FSH/LH/estrogen patterns)","batch":1,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman has had no menstrual period for 14 months and reports hot flashes. Which hormone pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low estradiol with markedly low FSH and an even lower LH level","B":"Low estradiol with markedly elevated FSH and a smaller rise in LH","C":"High estradiol with markedly elevated LH and a suppressed FSH","D":"Normal estradiol with markedly elevated prolactin and a low LH","E":"Low estradiol with a normal FSH and a large cyclic surge of LH"},"explanation":"As the number of primordial follicles approaches zero, ovarian estrogen production falls below the critical value needed to inhibit FSH and LH. The gonadotropins, mainly FSH, are then produced in large, continuous quantities. First Aid summarizes menopause as low estrogen, markedly elevated FSH (the most specific change), elevated LH without a surge, and elevated GnRH, diagnosed after 12 months of amenorrhea. Low FSH and LH with low estradiol indicate hypothalamic or pituitary failure. High estradiol would suppress gonadotropins, not raise LH. Elevated prolactin with normal estradiol does not fit menopause. A normal FSH with cyclic LH surges implies ongoing ovulatory cycles, which have stopped.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menopause: average age 51; HAVOCS (hot flashes, vaginal atrophy, osteoporosis, coronary artery disease, sleep disturbances); markedly elevated FSH is specific. Menopause before age 40 suggests primary ovarian insufficiency. COMLEX: Hormone therapy relieves symptoms but carries risks of breast and endometrial cancer, cardiovascular disease, and thromboembolism, so it is individualized.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 7 — Primary gonadal failure raises gonadotropins by removing negative feedback","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Low FSH and LH with low estrad","E":"A normal FSH with cyclic LH su"}},{"id":"sdl40_n12","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret laboratory findings to differentiate primary vs secondary ovarian dysfunction (FSH/LH/estrogen patterns)","batch":1,"stem":"Which laboratory pattern indicates that a woman's low estrogen state arises from hypothalamic or pituitary failure rather than from failure of the ovaries?","choices":{"A":"Low estradiol with high FSH and high LH","B":"High estradiol with low FSH and low LH","C":"Normal estradiol with a high LH to FSH ratio and high androgens","D":"Low estradiol with a high FSH and a normal LH","E":"Low estradiol with low FSH and low LH"},"explanation":"In hypogonadotropic, or secondary, hypogonadism the hypothalamus or pituitary fails to drive the ovary, so estradiol, FSH, and LH are all low; examples include Kallmann syndrome, CNS lesions, and hyperprolactinemia. In hypergonadotropic, or primary, hypogonadism the ovary fails, estrogen feedback is lost, and FSH and LH are high, as in Turner syndrome, primary ovarian insufficiency, and gonadal injury from chemotherapy or radiation. A high FSH, even with a normal LH, still points to the ovary. High estradiol with suppressed gonadotropins points to an estrogen source such as a granulosa cell tumor. A high LH to FSH ratio with androgen excess is the pattern of polycystic ovary syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary (hypergonadotropic): low estrogen, high FSH and LH, as in Turner syndrome, primary ovarian insufficiency, chemotherapy, or radiation. Secondary (hypogonadotropic): low estrogen with low FSH and LH, as in Kallmann syndrome, CNS lesions, hyperprolactinemia, or Sheehan syndrome. COMLEX: Hyperprolactinemia is a reversible cause of hypogonadotropic anovulation, so prolactin belongs in the workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 7 — The same LH and FSH interpretation distinguishes primary from secondary hypogonadism in men","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"In hypergonadotropic, or prima","B":"High estradiol with suppressed"}},{"id":"sdl40_n18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Interpret laboratory findings to differentiate primary vs secondary ovarian dysfunction (FSH/LH/estrogen patterns)","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for primary amenorrhea. She is 142 cm tall, has a broad chest with widely spaced nipples and a webbed neck, and has no breast development. Serum FSH is 86 mIU/mL, LH is 34 mIU/mL, and estradiol is undetectable. Which of the following best explains her gonadotropin levels?","choices":{"A":"A hypothalamic defect blocks GnRH release, so the pituitary oversecretes FSH and LH to compensate","B":"A pituitary adenoma secretes FSH and LH autonomously and suppresses ovarian estrogen production","C":"Defective androgen receptors prevent testosterone from suppressing FSH and LH by negative feedback","D":"Absent pituitary estrogen receptors block feedback, even though her ovaries make normal estrogen","E":"Streak ovaries make almost no estrogen, so negative feedback is lost and FSH and LH rise"},"explanation":"Her short stature, broad chest with widely spaced nipples, webbed neck, and primary amenorrhea are typical of Turner syndrome (45,XO), the most common cause of primary amenorrhea, in which ovarian dysgenesis produces streak ovaries. Without ovarian estrogen, negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary is lost, so FSH and LH rise, the hypergonadotropic pattern of primary ovarian failure. A hypothalamic GnRH defect would lower FSH and LH rather than raise them. Gonadotropin-secreting adenomas are not the explanation for this phenotype, and her undetectable estradiol reflects absent ovarian function. Androgen insensitivity occurs in genetic males and features breast development with high testosterone, estrogen, and LH. Her estradiol is undetectable, so her ovaries are not making normal estrogen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Turner syndrome: low estrogen with high LH and FSH; streak ovaries, bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of the aorta, horseshoe kidney, lymphatic defects; short stature responds to GH therapy. COMLEX: A uterus without breast development indicates hypergonadotropic hypogonadism in a genotypic female.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — Growth hormone therapy is used to prevent short stature in Turner syndrome","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"}]};
